window.MASTERY_SEARCH = [{"title": "01.01 · Scarcity, choice and opportunity cost", "url": "v01-c01.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "The problem economics begins with You have one evening. You can work for pay, study, spend time with family, or rest. Each option uses the same limited hours. The problem is not necessarily a shortage of money; it is that you cannot simultaneously do everything you value. Economics studies choices made under constraints, the interactions among those choices, and their consequences. A constraint describes what is feasible. A household faces time, income, borrowing and information constraints. A firm faces technology, capacity, financing and contractual constraints. A government faces resources, administrative capabilities, political commitments and legal constraints. Start an analysis by identifying the decision maker, the available actions, the constraints, and the objective. An objective is what the decision maker values. It might be consumption, profit, health, learning, reliability, or a combination. A financial return is one possible component. It is not a universal measure of wellbeing. When important outcomes cannot be expressed credibly in money, keep them explicit rather than assigning invented dollar values. Opportunity cost is an alternative The opportunity cost of a choice is the value of the best feasible alternative forgone. It is not necessarily an invoice or cash payment. Using a building you already own can have an opportunity cost because you could rent it out. Studying for three hours can have an opportunity cost because you could work or rest. The best alternative must be realistic and available. Suppose you can choose only one of three activities with net benefits of 100, 70 and 40 measured in the same units. If you choose the first, its opportunity cost is 70. Adding 70 and 40 would pretend you could undertake both forgone alternatives even though the constraint permits only one. The advantage of the first choice over the best alternative is 30. Be clear about the accounting convention. If you have already subtracted the forgone alternative's value when calculating an economic net benefit, subtracting it again would double count it. A useful approach is to calculate the future benefits and costs of each feasible action consistently, then compare their net values. Worked example 1 — Work, study, or rest You have four hours. Paid work earns $25 an hour but requires a $10 transportation expense. You estimate the value of studying during the same four hours at $130. Rest has an estimated value of $80. Assume the three values are comparable monetary equivalents, and the actions are mutually exclusive. Work's net value is 4 × $25 − $10 = $90. Study's net value is $130; rest's is $80. The best alternative to study is work, worth $90. Study's opportunity cost is $90. Study has a $130 − $90 = $40 advantage over the best alternative. The recommendation is conditional on those valuations. If the work is unavailable, it cannot be the opportunity cost. If studying causes exhaustion that was omitted from its valuation, the comparison needs correction. If rest and paid work can be combined in a different feasible schedule, that schedule must also be evaluated. Relevant costs and sunk costs A cost is sunk at the time of a decision when it has already been incurred and cannot be recovered through any currently available action. A past payment does not automatically qualify: if you can obtain a refund or sell what you purchased, the recoverable value affects the decision. Compare consequences that differ among the actions from the decision time forward. Suppose a business spent $40,000 developing a product. Launching now would generate $30,000 of revenue and require another $20,000 of cost. Abandoning would generate no future revenue or expense. With no other consequences, launching has a $10,000 advantage. The unrecoverable $40,000 affects the historical project's total return, but it does not reverse the current comparison. Historical accountability and current decision quality answer different questions. Managers should learn why the development money was spent and whether the original decision was justified. They should also choose the best remaining action. Continuing a bad project merely to make the past expenditure feel worthwhile can destroy more value. Past choices can still matter through their present consequences. A signed contract can impose a future cancellation charge. An acquired skill can improve a future benefit. A reputation can influence demand. Count these consequences if they differ between the actions; do not label all history irrelevant. Think at the margin An average describes a total divided by a quantity. A marginal quantity describes the change caused by an additional unit. A firm deciding whether to make one more item needs the additional revenue and additional cost, rather than the average historical profit of all items. For discrete actions, write the comparison explicitly: Incremental net benefit = incremental benefit − incremental cost An additional unit is worth taking when this quantity is positive, given the constraints and the consequences already included. When benefits decrease and costs increase with quantity, this often means expanding until the next unit is no longer worthwhile. Without those conditions, a local comparison need not find the best overall decision; compare the feasible alternatives directly. Later, calculus will formalize marginal changes with derivatives. The familiar condition “marginal benefit equals marginal cost” applies to particular interior, differentiable optimization problems. It is not a rule that every optimum must satisfy. Capacity constraints, indivisible choices and corner solutions often produce an optimum where equality fails. Worked example 2 — A partially completed workshop You already paid a nonrefundable $60 registration fee for a workshop. Attending requires another $10 transportation payment. You value the remaining workshop at $45. Staying home and working earns $40 net. Assume no additional benefits or costs. Current action Future benefit Future cash cost Net value Attend $45 $10 $35 Work instead $40 $0 $40 Working is better by $5. The $60 already paid is common to both actions and cancels from their comparison. Equivalently, attending provides $45 and costs $10 plus a $40 forgone alternative, for an incremental advantage of −$5. These are two consistent calculations of the same choice. Now suppose a refund of $20 is available only if you do not attend. The alternative becomes worth $60, and attending is worse by $25. The registration payment has a recoverable component and is no longer entirely sunk. A decision procedure you can reuse First specify the choice and the decision time. List realistic alternatives, including doing nothing when it is feasible. Then describe the consequences that differ among them. Put money on a common date when timing matters; Chapter 5 develops that calculation. Identify uncertain outcomes rather than silently treating them as certain; Chapter 8 develops scenarios. Check for double counting. A lost wage can appear as the alternative's benefit or as an opportunity cost of the chosen action, but it should not appear twice in the same comparison. An expense already deducted from profit should not be subtracted again. Nonmonetary outcomes should remain visible when monetary equivalents are unreliable. Finally state a conditional conclusion: which action is best under the assumptions, which assumptions matter most, and what new information could change the result. A disciplined decision is transparent about its limits. It need not pretend that uncertain personal values or future outcomes have been measured precisely. Common errors Counting only cash: overlooks the best use of time, owned assets, or scarce capacity. Adding incompatible alternatives: inflates opportunity cost beyond what was feasible. Counting the same loss twice: mixes a net-value comparison with an opportunity-cost adjustment inconsistently. Letting a sunk payment decide: confuses historical performance with the best current action. Using averages for additional decisions: ignores the consequences of the next unit. Inventing certainty: converts uncertain or nonmonetary values into precise-looking numbers without justification. Exercises Attempt each problem before opening the solutions. State your assumptions and units. You can work for five hours at $18 per hour with a $15 transportation cost, study for an estimated value of $95, or rest for an estimated value of $65. The choices are exclusive. Which is best, what is its opportunity cost, and what is its advantage? An owned office can be rented out for $900 per month after rental expenses. Using it for your own business requires $150 per month in additional cleaning costs. What monthly cost should the business compare with the benefits of occupying it? Explain why the historical purchase price is not enough information. A nonrefundable $120 ticket has already been purchased. Attending provides a benefit of $90 and costs $20 in transportation. An alternative activity provides $80 net. Compare the current choices. How does the result change if the ticket can be resold for $30 after fees? A project already cost $8,000. Completing it requires $2,000 and produces $2,700 of future revenue. Abandonment has no future consequence. Calculate the best current decision and the total project profit if completed. Explain why these can point to different judgments. The net benefits of three mutually exclusive actions are 160, 120 and 90. Someone says the first action's opportunity cost is 210. Identify the error and calculate the relevant comparison. A seller can produce up to four units. Additional-unit benefits are 30, 25, 18 and 10; additional-unit costs are 12, 15, 17 and 20. Assuming these are the complete consequences, how many units should be produced? Calculate the total net benefit. A student says, “I must finish this course because I have already spent 100 hours on it.” Give a case where continuing is rational and one where stopping is rational. Identify which future consequences would decide the comparison. Write a decision memo of no more than 150 words about a real choice. Identify the decision time, two feasible alternatives, the opportunity cost, a potentially sunk cost, uncertainty, and a fact that could change your decision. Do not invent monetary values for outcomes you cannot credibly price. Solutions and evaluation criteria Work is worth 5 × 18 − 15 = $75. Study is best at $95; its opportunity cost is $75, and its advantage is $20. Rest is not added to work because the forgone alternatives are exclusive. The relevant monthly cost is $900 forgone net rent plus $150 additional cleaning, or $1,050, assuming those consequences are distinct and complete. Historical purchase price does not describe the current best alternative. If selling is also feasible, compare its future consequences on a consistent horizon. Attendance is worth 90 − 20 = $70; the alternative is $80. Choose the alternative by $10. With resale available only if you do not attend, the alternative is worth $110 and is better by $40. The resale opportunity changes the current comparison. Complete: 2,700 − 2,000 = $700 more future value than abandonment. Total project profit is 2,700 − 8,000 − 2,000 = −$7,300. The original project lost money, yet completion minimizes the remaining loss under these assumptions. The analyst added alternatives that cannot both be taken. The best forgone alternative is 120; the first action's advantage is 160 − 120 = 40. Marginal net benefits are 18, 10, 1 and −10. Produce three units for a total net benefit of 29. The complete quantity comparison gives totals 0, 18, 28, 29 and 19, confirming the choice. Continuing is rational if its expected future benefits exceed those of the best alternative after remaining costs. Stopping is rational if an alternative has higher future net value. The 100 unrecoverable hours are sunk, but skills already acquired, remaining fees, completion requirements and future opportunities can affect the comparison. A strong memo compares genuinely feasible choices, uses a consistent time horizon, separates recoverable from sunk costs, avoids double counting, preserves important nonmonetary outcomes, and gives a conditional recommendation. There is no universal correct personal choice; the quality of the reasoning is assessable. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain opportunity cost to someone without economic training using an owned asset and a time allocation. Explain why a disappointing past investment can still rationally receive more spending. Delayed check: a month later, revisit an actual choice. Reconstruct the alternatives without reading this chapter. Can you distinguish the best forgone alternative, sunk costs, and future consequences that genuinely differ? Where this leads Later chapters formalize constraints and objectives with algebra, optimization, probability and dynamic models. Welfare economics asks whose benefits and costs are counted; information economics asks how knowledge changes choice; institutional analysis asks which choices are actually available. The discipline begins with a correct comparison."}, {"title": "01.02 · Numbers, units and percentage changes", "url": "v01-c02.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Numbers need a meaning A number without a unit, time period or denominator can mislead. Revenue of “50” could mean $50, $50 million, fifty units sold, or an index reading. Growth of “5” could mean five dollars, five percent or five percentage points. Before calculating, identify what each quantity measures and the population or period to which it applies. Write quantities in forms such as “$50 million per year,” “12 unemployed people per 100 labor-force participants,” or “$30 per item.” Units discipline the calculation. Dividing dollars by dollars yields a ratio. Dividing dollars by units yields dollars per unit. Adding a monthly rate to a yearly rate without conversion is a mistake even if a calculator accepts it. Fractions, ratios and decimals A fraction expresses a part relative to a whole: 3/4 means three parts when the whole is divided into four equal parts. A ratio compares quantities. Debt of $60 and equity of $40 produce a debt-to-equity ratio of 60/40 = 1.5, while debt's share of total financing is 60/(60 + 40) = 0.6. These answer different questions. A percentage is a ratio multiplied by 100. The decimal 0.06 equals 6%; 1.06 equals 106%; 0.006 equals 0.6%. When using a formula, translate percentages into decimal rates unless the formula explicitly expects percentage units. Multiplying $1,000 by 6 yields $6,000, whereas multiplying it by a 6% rate gives $60. Financial quantities can be negative. A loss of $20 relative to a $100 initial investment is a −20% return. However, percentage changes from zero are undefined, and changes from a negative base require careful interpretation. A business's profit moving from −$10 to +$10 is a $20 improvement; describing it as an ordinary −200% growth rate would obscure the economic meaning. Levels, changes and growth rates The absolute change is the new level minus the old level. The ordinary percentage change uses the old level as the denominator: Absolute change = new − old Percentage change = 100 × (new − old) / old This definition requires a nonzero base and is most straightforward for positive levels. An increase from 80 to 100 is 20 units and 25%. Reversing the move from 100 to 80 is −20 units and −20%. Percentage changes are asymmetric because their denominators differ. The fraction remaining after a loss matters for recovery. A 50% loss leaves half the original value. Doubling the remaining half requires a 100% gain. More generally, after a fractional loss L with 0 ≤ L < 1, recovering to the original level requires a gain of L/(1 − L). A complete loss cannot be recovered by applying a finite percentage return to the zero remaining balance. Worked example 1 — Growth, losses and changing denominators A business's monthly sales rise from $80,000 to $100,000, then fall back to $80,000. The increase is $20,000/$80,000 = 0.25, or 25%. The decrease is −$20,000/$100,000 = −0.20, or −20%. The combined growth factor is 1.25 × 0.80 = 1.00. Sales return to the initial level. Adding the rates gives 25% − 20% = 5%, which is not the combined percentage change. For consecutive changes, multiply growth factors. If a quantity rises 10% and then 20%, its factor is 1.10 × 1.20 = 1.32, a 32% increase. The extra two percentage points arise because the second change applies to a larger base. Percentages and percentage points When the measured quantity is itself a percentage, distinguish an absolute difference in percentage units from a relative percentage change. If an interest rate rises from 4% to 6%, it rises by two percentage points . Relative to the original 4% rate, it rises by (6 − 4)/4 = 50%. A basis point is one hundredth of a percentage point. One percentage point is 100 basis points. A rate increase from 4.00% to 4.25% is 0.25 percentage points, 25 basis points, and a 6.25% relative increase in the rate. The phrases are not interchangeable. Suppose a quoted unemployment rate falls from 10% to 9%. That is a decline of one percentage point or 10% relative to its previous rate. It does not establish that the number of unemployed people fell by 10%, because the labor-force denominator may also have changed. Reconstruct the counts when that is the question. Weighted averages An ordinary average gives each observation equal weight. A weighted average assigns specified weights that sum to one: Weighted average = w₁x₁ + w₂x₂ + … + wₙxₙ with w₁ + w₂ + … + wₙ = 1 To combine a rate across groups, the relevant weights typically correspond to the rate's denominator. Combining employment shares uses group populations in the applicable population measure. Combining profit margins uses revenues. A large and a small division should not receive equal weights merely because each is one division. For a portfolio measured over one common period with no intermediate flows, the simple portfolio return is the initial-value-weighted average of component simple returns. This statement does not imply that averaging yearly returns gives compound growth. Chapter 5 develops the distinction between growth over time and aggregation across assets. Worked example 2 — Combining profit margins correctly Division A has revenue of $100 and profit of $20. Division B has revenue of $900 and profit of $45. Assume the revenues and profits are measured over the same period and can be added without interdivision double counting. A's margin is 20/100 = 20%. B's is 45/900 = 5%. The equal-weight average is (20% + 5%)/2 = 12.5%. It gives the small division the same importance as the large one. The business's actual combined margin is (20 + 45)/(100 + 900) = 65/1,000 = 6.5%. Revenue weights are 0.1 and 0.9. The weighted margin is 0.1 × 20% + 0.9 × 5% = 6.5%. The method follows the denominator. If the objective were to describe a typical division's margin rather than the combined business's margin, the equal-weight average might answer that different question. Good analysis states which question it is answering. Rates across time and measurement conventions A rate needs its period. A monthly 1% return and an annual 1% return do not describe the same growth. Multiplying a monthly rate by 12 gives a simple annualized convention, not the exact effective annual growth when monthly gains compound. At 1% each month, that growth is (1.01) 12 − 1, approximately 12.68%. When reading a number, ask whether it is a level, an annual total, a monthly flow, a seasonally adjusted rate, or an index. A flow observed for one period cannot automatically be treated as a forecast for the next. A quarter's sales multiplied by four is an annualized representation, not necessarily the year's realized sales. Unit conversions must preserve the quantity. One million dollars equals 1,000 thousand dollars. If a dataset reports money in thousands and you treat it as dollars, the result will be off by a factor of 1,000. When dividing two monetary quantities, check both scales before forming a ratio. Precision, rounding and plausibility Do not round every intermediate calculation unless the contract or measurement convention requires it. Preserve sufficient precision, then round the displayed answer to a level justified by the inputs and the purpose. A forecast based on rough assumptions does not become more reliable when shown to eight decimal places. Use plausibility checks. A margin combining positive revenues should fall between the individual margins if all relevant profits and revenues are aggregated consistently. A 10% increase followed by a 10% decrease should leave slightly less than the initial amount: 1.10 × 0.90 = 0.99. If a conversion makes an ordinary household budget equal to national income, inspect the scale before advancing to a complicated model. Numerical competence is largely disciplined interpretation. The formula is often simple; the challenge is identifying the correct base, denominator, period, population and unit. Exercises A wage rises from $20 to $23 per hour. Calculate its absolute and percentage change. Then calculate the percentage reduction required to return from $23 to $20. A rate rises from 3.50% to 4.10%. Express the change in percentage points, basis points and relative percentage terms. An asset worth $200 loses 30% and then gains 30%. Find the final value and total percentage change. What gain after the initial loss would restore the original value? Division X has revenue of $400 and profit of $40. Division Y has revenue of $600 and profit of $120. Find the divisions' margins, the equal-weight mean margin and the combined margin. A portfolio begins with $300 in asset A and $700 in asset B. During one common period, A returns 10% and B returns −5%, with no external cash flows. Calculate the portfolio's ending value and simple return. A dataset lists debt as 2,400 in thousands of dollars and GDP as 3 in millions of dollars. Calculate debt in dollars and the debt-to-GDP ratio. Explain the unit correction. At time 0, a labor force of 1,000 includes 100 unemployed people. At time 1, a labor force of 1,200 includes 108 unemployed people. Calculate both unemployment rates, their percentage-point change, and the percentage change in the unemployed count. A report states, “Profit increased from −$5 million to $10 million, so profit grew −300%.” Explain the arithmetic behind that expression, why it misleads, and how you would report the result. Add a rule for reporting changes when the original level is zero. Solutions and evaluation criteria The wage rises $3 per hour, or 3/20 = 15%. The reverse change is −3/23, approximately −13.04%. The denominators differ. The increase is 0.60 percentage points or 60 basis points. Relative to 3.50%, it is 0.60/3.50, approximately 17.14%. The ending value is 200 × 0.70 × 1.30 = $182, a −9% total change. Recovery from the $140 remaining value requires 60/140 = 42.857…%, approximately 42.86%. X's margin is 10%; Y's is 20%. The equal-weight mean is 15%. Combined profit is $160 on $1,000 revenue, giving a 16% margin. Revenue weighting produces 0.4 × 10% + 0.6 × 20% = 16%. A ends at $330 and B at $665. Total is $995, a −0.5% return on $1,000. Initial-value weighting gives 0.3 × 10% + 0.7 × (−5%) = −0.5%. Debt is 2,400 × $1,000 = $2.4 million. GDP is $3 million. The ratio is 2.4/3 = 0.8 or 80%. Dividing the unconverted displayed values would mix scales. The initial rate is 100/1,000 = 10%; the later rate is 108/1,200 = 9%. The rate falls one percentage point even though the unemployed count rises by 8%. A changing denominator explains the difference. The ordinary formula gives (10 − (−5))/(−5) = −3. Reporting −300% conceals the improvement because the base is negative. State that the business moved from a $5 million loss to a $10 million profit, a $15 million improvement. With a zero initial level, ordinary percentage growth is undefined; report the absolute change and relevant context. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain why “the rate rose 50%” and “the rate rose two percentage points” can both describe the same event. Give your own numbers and identify the denominator. Delayed check: reconstruct the return after a 20% loss followed by a 20% gain, and explain the result without a memorized formula. Where this leads Algebra will express these relationships symbolically. Accounting will make denominator choices more consequential. Statistics will formalize sampling weights and uncertainty. Asset pricing will distinguish simple returns, logarithmic returns, compound growth and cash-flow-sensitive performance measures. Accurate units and bases remain essential at every level."}, {"title": "01.03 · Algebra, functions and graphs", "url": "v01-c03.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Turn a verbal claim into a relationship A model becomes easier to examine when its quantities and relationships are explicit. Suppose a taxi fare consists of a $4 starting charge plus $2 per kilometer. Let x be kilometers traveled and y be dollars charged. The rule is y = 4 + 2x. It makes a testable prediction, specifies units, and exposes an assumption: the per-kilometer charge is constant over the trip. A variable can take different values. A parameter is treated as fixed for a particular exercise. Here x varies; the numbers 4 and 2 are parameters. A parameter can change between scenarios: a new tariff could change the starting charge. Always distinguish a quantity chosen by an actor, a quantity determined within the model, and an input taken as given. An equation says two expressions are equal. An identity holds by the definitions involved, such as total spending equaling the sum of spending categories. A behavioral equation describes how an actor responds, such as a proposed relationship between price and quantity demanded. The mathematical equality sign does not make a behavioral relationship an accounting truth. Solve equations while preserving equality Perform the same legitimate operation on both sides. To solve 4 + 2x = 18, subtract 4 from both sides to obtain 2x = 14, then divide both sides by 2 to obtain x = 7. Substitute 7 into the original expression: 4 + 2 × 7 = 18. Substitution checks the answer and can detect algebraic mistakes. Division requires a nonzero divisor. Multiplying both sides by zero destroys information. Squaring both sides can introduce solutions that did not satisfy the original equation, so check candidates afterward. If an equation represents an economic problem, mathematical solutions must also satisfy its domain and constraints. A negative number of purchased books may solve a rearranged expression but still be infeasible. An inequality describes a feasible range. A budget of $20 with books priced at $5 and meals at $2 permits nonnegative quantities x and y satisfying: 5x + 2y ≤ 20 x ≥ 0, y ≥ 0 Equality identifies bundles that spend the entire budget. Inequality also permits unspent money. Multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number reverses its direction: −2x ≤ 6 implies x ≥ −3. The reversal follows from the ordering of negative numbers, not from an arbitrary convention. Functions, domains and slopes A function assigns one output to each input in its domain. The notation y = f(x) means that y is the output associated with x. For f(x) = 4 + 2x, f(3) = 10. Domain restrictions describe allowed inputs: distance is nonnegative, and a fare rule might apply only within a service area. A linear function has the form y = a + bx. The intercept a is the output when x = 0. The slope b is the change in output per additional unit of input. More generally, the slope between two points is: Slope = (y₂ − y₁) / (x₂ − x₁), for x₂ ≠ x₁ The slope carries units. A fare slope of 2 means $2 per kilometer. A budget slope describing meals against books means meals forgone per additional book. Changing units changes the numerical slope without changing the underlying relationship. A distance measured in meters rather than kilometers gives a fare slope of $0.002 per meter. A positive slope means the modeled quantities move in the same direction; a negative slope means they move in opposite directions. Neither sign establishes causation in observed data. A model specifying a causal mechanism and a graph of an observed association are different objects. Worked example 1 — Read a budget line With a $20 budget, books at $5 each and meals at $2 each, the full-spending boundary is 5x + 2y = 20. Rearrange: 2y = 20 − 5x y = 10 − 2.5x If x = 0, then y = 10: all money goes to meals. If y = 0, then x = 4: all money goes to books. If x = 2, then y = 5. Spending is 5 × 2 + 2 × 5 = $20. The slope is −2.5 meals per book. Another book costs as much as 2.5 meals. Budget line for books and meals The feasible boundary slopes from zero books and ten meals to four books and zero meals. Two books and five meals lies on the line. Bundles below the line are feasible under the budget. 0 2 4 10 5 Books (x) Meals (y) (2, 5) Feasible bundles The line is a constraint, not a prediction of what the consumer chooses. The pictured line permits fractional goods as a mathematical representation. If only whole books and meals are available, restrict x and y to nonnegative integers. The line still describes the cost trade-off, but some points are unavailable. A preference model is needed to predict which feasible point the consumer prefers. Graphs and inverse relationships A graph labels an input-output relationship on axes. Always inspect the labels, units and ranges before interpreting it. The same relationship can look very different when an axis is truncated or its scale is changed. A vertical line can describe an equation in two variables, but it is not an ordinary function y = f(x) because one input would correspond to many outputs. An invertible relationship can be rewritten with the other variable as the output. If quantity demanded is Q = 120 − 2P, solving for price gives P = 60 − Q/2. The slope of quantity with respect to price is −2 units per dollar; the slope of price with respect to quantity is −0.5 dollars per unit. These are different units and correspond to different axis arrangements. Economics often places price on the vertical axis and quantity on the horizontal axis even when a demand equation is written as Q(P). State the inverse form when interpreting that graph. Do not assume that the displayed graph's slope is the coefficient in the original equation. Worked example 2 — Solve a simple market model Suppose buyers demand Q d = 120 − 2P and sellers supply Q s = 20 + 3P, measured in units per day and dollars per unit. The proposed equilibrium has equal quantities demanded and supplied. Set 120 − 2P = 20 + 3P. Subtract 20 and add 2P on both sides: 100 = 5P. Divide by 5: P = $20 per unit. Substitute in each equation: Q d = 120 − 40 = 80 and Q s = 20 + 60 = 80. At P = $10, demand is 100 while supply is 50. The model has excess demand of 50 units at that price. Explaining how price actually adjusts requires assumptions about bargaining, contracts, inventories and institutions. Solving an equality does not itself establish the adjustment process or prove that the equations fit a real market. Nonlinear relationships Not every relationship has a constant slope. If total cost is C(q) = 10 + q 2 , increasing output from 1 to 2 raises cost from 11 to 14, an additional 3. Increasing output from 2 to 3 raises cost from 14 to 19, an additional 5. The same output increment has a different cost consequence at a different starting point. Compounding uses powers. An amount growing at rate r for n periods becomes its initial amount multiplied by (1 + r) n . A logarithm reverses exponentiation: if a x = b with appropriate positive values and a ≠ 1, then x = log a (b). Logarithms will help solve growth and timing problems. They do not permit the real logarithm of a negative quantity. Piecewise functions describe rules that change across ranges. A cost can be $5 per unit up to capacity and $8 per extra unit afterward. Write the ranges explicitly. Ignoring a threshold can produce an incorrect optimum even when the arithmetic within each range is correct. Check the economic interpretation After solving, verify equality or inequality in the original expressions. Then check the units, domain, constraints and meaning. Ask which parameters were fixed and whether their values are plausible. A mathematical result is conditional on the specification. The equation is a language for reasoning, not a substitute for it. A precise model can expose a mistaken claim, but it can also produce a precise answer to the wrong question. Tie each symbol back to a measured or conceptual economic quantity. Exercises A service charges $6 plus $3 per hour. Write its charge as a function of hours, calculate the charge for four hours, and solve for the hours corresponding to a $27 charge. State the units of its slope. A $60 budget purchases goods x at $10 each and y at $5 each. Write the feasible set and full-spending boundary. Find both intercepts and the slope of y against x. Is (4, 5) feasible? Solve 7 + 4x = 31 and verify your answer by substitution. Then solve −3x ≤ 12 and explain why the inequality direction changes. Demand is Q d = 150 − 3P and supply is Q s = 30 + 2P. Find equilibrium price and quantity. Calculate excess demand at P = 20. Rewrite Q = 80 − 4P with P as a function of Q. Give the slope and units for each representation. State a nonnegative-quantity domain for P. For C(q) = 20 + 2q + q 2 , calculate costs at q = 0, 1, 2 and 3 and the additional cost of each unit. Does a constant additional cost describe this rule? A $100 amount grows by 10% per period. Write the amount after n periods and calculate it after two periods. Solve 100 × 1.1 n = 121 by recognizing the power. Explain why n is a period count. A graph shows sales and advertising moving together. Explain what its slope could describe and why that relationship alone does not show that more advertising causes the observed sales increase. Propose one alternative explanation. Solutions and evaluation criteria Charge f(h) = 6 + 3h dollars. Four hours costs $18. Set 6 + 3h = 27 to obtain h = 7 hours. The slope is $3 per hour. The set is 10x + 5y ≤ 60 with x,y ≥ 0. The boundary is y = 12 − 2x, with intercepts (0,12) and (6,0). The slope is −2 y-units per x-unit. Bundle (4,5) costs $65 and is infeasible. 4x = 24, so x = 6; 7 + 4 × 6 = 31. Dividing the inequality by −3 gives x ≥ −4. Values at or above −4 satisfy the original inequality. 150 − 3P = 30 + 2P implies 120 = 5P, hence P = 24 and Q = 78. At P = 20, demand is 90 and supply is 70, giving excess demand of 20. P = 20 − Q/4. The Q-on-P slope is −4 quantity units per dollar; the P-on-Q slope is −0.25 dollars per quantity unit. For nonnegative prices and quantities, 0 ≤ P ≤ 20. Costs are 20, 23, 28 and 35. Additional costs are 3, 5 and 7. The function is nonlinear and its additional cost increases over these quantities. The amount is 100 × 1.1 n . After two periods it is $121, so n = 2 solves the equation. The base is the per-period growth factor; the exponent counts applications of that factor. The slope describes an association given the graph's measurement and scales. Firms may advertise more when they expect unusually high demand, or seasonal factors may increase both advertising and sales. A causal claim needs a credible counterfactual and a design that handles confounding. Several explanations are acceptable if their mechanisms are clear. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain the budget diagram without using the word “slope” until you have described what one more book requires giving up. Then connect that description to the equation. Delayed check: invent a new two-good budget and market-clearing problem, solve each from scratch, and verify your results in the original equations. Where this leads Calculus studies nonlinear marginal changes and optimization. Linear algebra handles many equations simultaneously. Econometrics estimates relationships from data and examines their interpretation. Throughout these developments, the essential habit is to state the variables, units, domain, assumptions and question before solving."}, {"title": "01.04 · Stocks, flows and balance sheets", "url": "v01-c04.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "A snapshot is different from a rate of activity A stock is measured at a point in time. Examples include cash held at the end of a day, outstanding debt on a reporting date, inventory in a warehouse, and the population living in a country. A flow is measured over an interval. Examples include wages earned during a month, purchases made during a quarter, interest charged during a year, and births during a period. Units often expose the distinction. A stock might be $10,000 at 31 December. A flow might be $1,000 per month or $12,000 earned over a year. Comparing a debt stock with annual income is meaningful if the ratio is described as debt relative to one year's income. It is not a statement that debt and income are the same kind of quantity. A frequent mistake is to treat income as wealth. A household can have a high income flow and little net wealth because it spends heavily or owes large debts. Another can have modest current income and substantial wealth from prior savings or asset ownership. Similarly, a government's annual deficit and its outstanding debt measure different objects. Reconcile the opening and closing stock A stock changes through transactions and other changes during the period. A general reconciliation is: Closing stock = opening stock + additions − reductions + other changes For physical inventory, additions can be purchases or production; reductions can be sales, use or spoilage. For cash, additions and reductions are cash receipts and payments. For the measured value of an asset, other changes can include price movements even when no transaction occurs. The categories depend on what the stock measures. Interest capitalized into a loan increases outstanding debt. A payment reduces the obligation according to the contract's allocation rules. When all interest and borrowing are added to a modeled balance and all payments are subtracted from that same balance, the reconciliation must preserve those conventions consistently. If a stock does not reconcile, possibilities include a missing transaction, a valuation change, inconsistent reporting dates, mismatched units, or an error. Identify the difference rather than hiding it in an unexplained “other” category. A reconciliation is an analytical check and often the start of an investigation. Worked example 1 — Reconcile a loan balance A simplified credit balance is $1,000 at the start of a month. During the month, $300 of new borrowing and $20 of interest are added. The borrower makes a $150 payment. There are no fees, write-offs or other changes. Closing debt = 1,000 + 300 + 20 − 150 = $1,170 Debt rises by $170 despite the payment because additions exceed the reduction. The $150 payment is a flow; the $1,170 closing balance is a stock. The payment cannot be equated with a $150 improvement in the household's net wealth without examining the cash used, the interest expense and the other transactions. If the closing statement instead shows $1,190, there is a $20 unexplained difference. Check for a fee, an omitted purchase, timing differences or an incorrect record. A correct analyst does not silently force the reported balance to match by inventing a transaction. Assets, liabilities and equity An asset is a resource or claim held by an entity. A liability is an obligation of that entity. Equity , or net worth in a simplified household balance sheet, is the residual after liabilities are subtracted from assets. The core identity is: Assets = liabilities + equity Equity = assets − liabilities The identity does not say that equity can always be realized at its recorded amount. Asset values may be uncertain; selling can incur costs; liabilities may have contractual and legal complexities. Later accounting and valuation chapters examine measurement. For now, use a consistent specified basis and reporting date. Borrowing money increases cash and liabilities by the same amount at origination in a simple transaction. It does not by itself create income or equity. Spending cash on an asset exchanges one asset for another. Paying down loan principal reduces cash and debt equally. Earning profit can increase equity, while an owner's capital contribution increases equity without being revenue. These distinctions prevent serious errors in business and policy reasoning. Cash inflow is not synonymous with revenue. Cash outflow is not synonymous with expense. A transaction's classification depends on what happened economically and on the specified accounting rules. Profit and cash flow Profit summarizes revenues minus recognized expenses for a period. Cash flow summarizes receipts and payments. Under accrual accounting, revenue can be recorded before a customer pays, and expenses can reflect resources consumed at a different time from the related cash payment. A company that sells $100 of goods on credit can record revenue while receiving no immediate cash. It holds a receivable instead. Purchasing a durable machine is an immediate cash outflow, but its cost is generally allocated across periods through depreciation under the applicable accounting method. A business can therefore be profitable while facing a cash shortage. Likewise, raising a loan can improve cash liquidity while leaving profit unchanged at origination. Interest affects later earnings and cash according to timing; repaying principal reduces the obligation. Keep the income statement, balance sheet and cash-flow statement distinct, then reconcile their connections. Worked example 2 — A simple business from transactions to statements An owner contributes $1,000 cash. The business borrows $600, purchases equipment for $500 cash, purchases inventory for $300 cash, and sells all that inventory for $450 cash. Assume no depreciation, taxes, interest, fees or other transactions during this short illustrative period. Transaction Cash change Other effect Owner contribution +$1,000 Equity +$1,000 Loan received +$600 Debt +$600 Equipment bought −$500 Equipment +$500 Inventory bought −$300 Inventory +$300 Inventory sold +$450 Inventory −$300; profit +$150 Ending cash is 1,000 + 600 − 500 − 300 + 450 = $1,250. Profit is sales revenue $450 minus inventory cost consumed $300 = $150. Assets are cash $1,250 plus equipment $500 = $1,750. Liabilities are $600. Equity is the contribution $1,000 plus retained profit $150 = $1,150. The balance sheet reconciles: 1,750 = 600 + 1,150. Cash-flow categories also reconcile: operating cash flow is 450 − 300 = $150; investing cash flow is −$500; financing cash flow is 1,000 + 600 = $1,600. Their sum is $1,250. The same final cash appears through a different organized view of the transactions. The example's excluded expenses are explicit assumptions, not a claim that real companies avoid depreciation, interest or tax. Including those consequences would change the statements and must be done consistently. The same claim has two sides A financial claim can be an asset for one party and a liability for another. A bank deposit is an asset of the depositor and an obligation of the bank. A loan is an asset of the lender and a liability of the borrower. When combining entities' balance sheets, do not count an internal claim as an external net asset of the combined group. Consider two entities where A lends B $100. A records a $100 claim; B records a $100 liability. Consolidating them eliminates the internal claim and obligation. This does not mean the transaction has no significance: contracts affect incentives, liquidity and risk within the group. It means the consolidated group's external net financial position must not count both sides as new net wealth. For an economy, the interpretation becomes more complex because domestic financial claims, real assets and claims on foreign parties differ. National accounting formalizes those boundaries. The general habit remains valuable: specify the entity or group, the date, the valuation convention and whether the claim is internal or external. Build an auditable reconciliation Begin with an opening statement whose identity balances. Record each transaction with effects on both assets and financing claims. Distinguish a transaction from a valuation change and from an estimate. Recompute the closing statement and explain any difference. Keep the original records so another analyst can follow the steps. A spreadsheet that balances only because an unexplained financing item absorbs every mistake is unreliable. Some forecasting models intentionally use a specified funding policy to close a cash gap; that is acceptable when the policy is explicit. An unexplained balancing plug is not equivalent to a realistic financing decision. Exercises Classify each quantity as a stock or flow and give a suitable time description: outstanding mortgage debt, annual wages, cash on hand, quarterly equipment purchases, inventory, and interest paid during a month. Inventory begins at 80 units. The business buys 50 units, sells 90 and loses 5 to spoilage. Find closing inventory. If the count instead shows 40, calculate the unexplained difference. A household has cash of $2,000, investments valued at $8,000 and debt of $3,000. Find equity. Then show the balance-sheet effects of borrowing another $1,000 and keeping the cash. Using the household after the borrowing in Exercise 3, repay $500 of loan principal with cash. Show closing assets, debt and equity. Explain why the principal payment is not by itself a $500 loss of net worth. A business earns $1,200 of revenue from a sale on credit but receives no cash yet. The sold inventory cost $700 and was already paid for. Ignoring all other expenses, what happens to receivables, inventory, profit, equity and current cash? A business's opening cash is $100. During the period it collects $900 from customers, pays $600 of operating costs, buys $200 of equipment, borrows $300 and repays $150 of principal. Calculate operating, investing and financing cash flows and closing cash. A shareholding is valued at $5,000 at the beginning of a period and $5,400 at the end. There are no purchases, sales or dividends. Explain the $400 stock change and why it is not a transaction inflow to cash. Entity A has $500 cash and a $200 loan claim on B. Entity B has $300 cash and owes A $200. There are no other assets or liabilities. Calculate each entity's net worth and the consolidated group's cash, external liabilities and net worth. Explain the elimination. Solutions and evaluation criteria Mortgage debt, cash and inventory are stocks at a date. Wages, purchases and interest payments are flows over intervals. The units and reporting dates must match the classification. Closing inventory is 80 + 50 − 90 − 5 = 35 units. A count of 40 exceeds the reconciliation by 5. Investigate records, timing and counting rather than assuming an unrecorded purchase. Opening equity is 2,000 + 8,000 − 3,000 = $7,000. Borrowing increases cash to $3,000 and debt to $4,000, leaving assets of $11,000 and equity of $7,000. Cash becomes $2,500, investments remain $8,000 and debt becomes $3,500. Assets are $10,500 and equity remains $7,000. Cash and principal owed decline together. Interest, fees or other consequences would require additional entries. Receivables rise $1,200; inventory falls $700. Profit and equity rise $500. Current cash does not change on this credit sale. Future collection converts the receivable into cash without recording the sale a second time. Operating flow is 900 − 600 = $300. Investing flow is −$200. Financing flow is 300 − 150 = $150. Net cash change is $250; closing cash is $350. Borrowing is financing, not operating revenue. The holding has a $400 valuation increase. It changes measured asset value and, under an appropriately specified measurement basis, net worth; it does not add cash. Accounting recognition of the gain depends on the applicable classification and rules. A's net worth is $700. B's is $300 − $200 = $100. Combined cash is $800; there is no external liability in the stated case. Consolidated net worth is $800. Remove A's internal $200 claim and B's matching $200 obligation; summing the individual net worths also gives $800. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain why receiving loan proceeds can increase cash without increasing profit or net worth, and why earning revenue can increase profit without immediately increasing cash. Delayed check: record five original business transactions and reconstruct their effects on cash, other assets, liabilities and equity. The closing identity must balance without an unexplained plug. Where this leads Financial accounting develops formal recognition and measurement. Corporate finance converts forecasts into value. Macroeconomics connects income flows, capital stocks and sector balances. Banking and crises require balance-sheet reasoning because financing claims transmit obligations, liquidity demands and losses across institutions."}, {"title": "01.05 · Compounding and present value", "url": "v01-c05.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Put cash flows on the same date A dollar available today and a dollar promised a year from now differ in timing, risk and purchasing power. To compare choices, specify when each payment occurs and bring the payments to one valuation date. Start with a simple setting: certain cash flows, a constant per-period rate, and a feasible opportunity to borrow or invest at that rate without fees or restrictions. These assumptions make the timing arithmetic transparent. Real decisions may require different rates, credit adjustments, taxes, currency conventions or borrowing constraints. Later finance chapters develop those issues. A correct present-value calculation still needs an economically justified discount rate. Let V 0 be an amount at time 0 and r the rate per period expressed as a decimal. If interest stays invested, the amount after one period is V 0 (1 + r). After two periods, interest also accrues on the previous period's interest. Repeating the rule gives: Future value after n periods = V₀(1 + r)ⁿ Specify what a period means. If r is a monthly rate, n counts months. If r is an annual rate, n counts years. A rate of 5% enters as 0.05. For standard positive discount factors, require 1 + r > 0. Negative rates above −100% are mathematically possible, although their economic interpretation and feasibility must be examined. Compounding and effective rates Simple interest on the original principal alone yields V 0 (1 + nr). Compound interest yields V 0 (1 + r) n . At $100 and 10% per year for two years, simple interest produces $120; annual compounding produces $121. The additional dollar reflects interest earned on the first year's interest. If a nominal annual rate j is compounded m times per year, the specified periodic rate is j/m and its effective annual rate is (1 + j/m) m − 1. That is a rate convention, not a universal interpretation of any quoted number. A 12% nominal rate compounded monthly gives a 1% monthly rate and approximately 12.68% effective annual growth. Fees, interest-calculation conventions and cash-flow timing can make a quoted rate differ from an all-in borrowing cost. Read the specified terms and calculate using the actual payments. Do not assume an illustrative mathematical rate equals a jurisdiction's legally defined disclosure measure. Discounting reverses compounding If an amount C is available n periods from now, the present value is the amount that would grow to C under the specified rate: Present value = C / (1 + r)ⁿ At a 10% annual rate, $1,000 due in two years has a present value of 1,000/1.1 2 , approximately $826.45. Check by compounding the unrounded result forward two years. A higher positive discount rate gives a lower present value for the same positive future payment because less initial money is needed to grow to that payment. For several dated cash flows, discount each to the same date and add: Present value = Σ Cₜ / (1 + r)ᵗ Net present value = −initial cost + present value of later net cash flows The symbol Σ means “sum over the listed periods.” Use net cash flows that include the relevant benefits and costs. Do not subtract an expense again if it was already deducted in constructing a net cash flow. Cash at time 0 has no period of discounting: (1 + r) 0 = 1. Worked example 1 — An investment with two payments A project requires $1,000 now and pays $600 at the end of each of the next two years. Assume these are certain net cash flows, the opportunity rate is 10% annually, and there are no other consequences. Time-0 cash flow is −$1,000. The first payment's present value is 600/1.1 = $545.4545…. The second payment's present value is 600/1.1 2 = $495.8678…. NPV is −1,000 + 545.4545… + 495.8678… = $41.3223…, displayed as $41.32. The positive NPV means the project exceeds the specified feasible opportunity in this setting. Adding $600 + $600 − $1,000 gives $200, but ignores timing. Using the same unrounded amounts, the project's advantage at year 2 is 600 × 1.1 + 600 − 1,000 × 1.1 2 = $50. Discounting $50 back two years gives the same $41.32 NPV. If the opportunity rate is 20%, NPV becomes −1,000 + 600/1.2 + 600/1.2 2 = −$83.33 approximately. A decision can change when an important assumption changes. Uncertain project payments would require a risk analysis rather than being labeled certain for convenience. Annuities and repeated payments An ordinary annuity pays the same amount A at the end of each period for n periods. Let d = 1/(1 + r). Its present value is A(d + d 2 + … + d n ). Multiplying the sum by d shifts every term one period. Subtracting the shifted sum from the original cancels the middle terms: S = d + d² + … + dⁿ dS = d² + d³ + … + dⁿ⁺¹ (1 − d)S = d − dⁿ⁺¹ For r ≠ 0, rearranging yields the annuity formula: PV = A × [1 − (1 + r)⁻ⁿ] / r If r = 0, use PV = nA directly. An annuity paid at the beginning of each period is an annuity due; with identical rates and n payments, it has the ordinary annuity's value multiplied by (1 + r), because each payment arrives one period earlier. Draw the timeline before choosing a formula. A perpetual constant end-of-period payment A has PV = A/r if r > 0 and the payments truly continue indefinitely. This mathematical limit is useful, but a finite business, contract or investment need not actually pay forever. Terminal-value models must justify their assumptions. Worked example 2 — A level-payment loan A loan advances $1,200 now. It has a 1% monthly rate and is repaid through 12 equal end-of-month payments, with no fees or additional charges. The present value of those payments must equal the advance. 1,200 = A × [1 − 1.01⁻¹²] / 0.01 A = 1,200 × 0.01 / [1 − 1.01⁻¹²] A ≈ $106.62 per month Using the unrounded payment, total payments are approximately $1,279.42. The difference from the $1,200 advance is approximately $79.42. Contractual rounding can make the final payment slightly different. The first month's interest is $1,200 × 0.01 = $12. The principal reduction is approximately 106.6185 − 12 = $94.6185, leaving approximately $1,105.3815. As principal declines, the interest portion declines and the principal portion rises if the payment and rate remain constant. The loan recurrence is B t = B t−1 (1 + r) − A. Applying it for 12 periods with the unrounded payment produces a final balance of zero up to numerical precision. This independent check tests the formula against the actual payment schedule. Nominal and real cash flows A nominal amount is expressed in the money units of its date. A real amount is adjusted to a specified purchasing-power basis. If a nominal investment return is r and the applicable price index grows at rate π over the same period, the purchasing-power growth factor is (1 + r)/(1 + π). The exact real return is that factor minus one. A 10% nominal gain with 5% inflation gives 1.10/1.05 − 1, approximately 4.76% real growth, rather than exactly 5%. Subtracting inflation from the nominal return is an approximation that can be useful for small rates. It is not the exact relation. Chapter 7 examines the measurement and interpretation of price indices. Use nominal cash flows with nominal rates and real cash flows with corresponding real rates. Consistency also applies to currency, tax treatment and compounding frequency. A technically correct formula with incompatible inputs produces an economically incorrect comparison. What present value does not decide by itself The calculation does not identify the correct cash-flow forecast, measure every nonmonetary consequence, or determine which risk-adjusted rate fits a real investment. A borrower who cannot finance an attractive project faces a constraint absent from the simple example. Liquidity, default risk and the distribution of outcomes can matter even when expected NPV is positive. Use the timeline and formulas to make assumptions visible. Then ask what could change: payment dates, inflation, reinvestment opportunities, borrowing costs, risk, fees and feasible alternatives. The value of discounting is that it disciplines comparison rather than making uncertainty disappear. Solving for an unknown rate can require a nonlinear equation. For the two-payment loan in Exercise 8, substituting x = 1 + r produces a quadratic. For ax 2 + bx + c = 0 with a ≠ 0, the quadratic formula is x = [−b ± √(b 2 − 4ac)]/(2a). The expression under the square root must be nonnegative for real roots. Check candidates in the original equation and retain only values satisfying the model's domain, including x > 0 for a positive discount factor. Exercises Calculate the future value of $500 after three annual periods at 8% compounded annually. Compare it with simple interest at 8% over the same horizon. Find the present value of $2,000 due three years from now at a 5% annual rate. Verify the answer by compounding it forward. A project costs $900 now and pays $500 at the end of each of the next two years. At a 10% rate, calculate NPV and state the conditional decision. Calculate the present value of three end-of-year payments of $200 at 10% both by summing the payments and by the annuity formula. What is the value if the rate is zero? A contract promises three $100 payments: one immediately, one in a year and one in two years. Calculate its value at a 10% annual rate. Explain why the ordinary three-period annuity formula alone has the wrong timing. A nominal 6% annual rate is compounded monthly. Find the monthly rate and effective annual rate. State why this calculation alone does not measure the cost of a loan that also has fees. An investment gains 8% nominally while the applicable price index rises 3%. Calculate the exact real return and compare it with the subtraction approximation. A loan advances $1,000 now and requires two end-of-year payments of $600. Derive the equation for the effective annual financing rate, express it as a quadratic in x = 1 + r, and solve for the economically admissible positive x. Ignore fees and default. Solutions and evaluation criteria Compound value is 500 × 1.08 3 = $629.856, or $629.86. Simple-interest value is 500 × (1 + 3 × 0.08) = $620. The $9.856 difference comes from compounding. PV = 2,000/1.05 3 = $1,727.675… approximately $1,727.68. Multiplying the unrounded value by 1.05 3 restores $2,000. NPV = −900 + 500/1.1 + 500/1.1 2 = −$32.2314… approximately −$32.23. Under the stated opportunity rate, certain payments and complete consequences, reject the project in favor of the specified alternative. The sum is 200/1.1 + 200/1.1 2 + 200/1.1 3 = $497.370… approximately $497.37. The annuity expression gives the same answer. At zero interest, PV is 3 × 200 = $600. PV = 100 + 100/1.1 + 100/1.1 2 = $273.5537… approximately $273.55. The payments occur at times 0,1,2 rather than 1,2,3. It is a three-payment annuity due. The monthly rate is 0.06/12 = 0.005, or 0.5%. Effective annual growth is 1.005 12 − 1, approximately 6.17%. Fees alter the borrower's actual advance or payments, so calculate from the full cash-flow schedule. The exact return is 1.08/1.03 − 1, approximately 4.85%. Subtraction gives 5%; it is an approximation. 1,000 = 600/x + 600/x 2 , hence 1,000x 2 − 600x − 600 = 0. The positive root is x = (600 + √2,760,000)/2,000 ≈ 1.130662386. Thus r ≈ 13.0662%. The negative root is inadmissible for the positive discount-factor convention. Substitution verifies the two payments' present value equals the advance. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: draw a three-payment timeline and explain why moving a payment one period earlier changes its present value. Derive the ordinary annuity formula by showing which terms cancel. Delayed check: construct a loan balance schedule using the recurrence, verify its final balance, and explain the difference between a quoted periodic rate and an all-in cost based on actual payments. Where this leads Corporate finance evaluates project and business cash flows. Bond valuation discounts contractual payments using a term structure. Asset pricing determines how uncertain payments are valued across states. The simple formulas here establish timing discipline; advanced valuation adds the economics of risk, information and market constraints."}, {"title": "01.06 · Incentives, marginal decisions and equilibrium", "url": "v01-c06.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Actions respond to consequences An incentive changes the consequences of an action. A higher wage can make working an additional hour more attractive. A price increase can make conserving a good more attractive. A penalty for late delivery can encourage timeliness. Predicting the response requires knowing the alternatives, constraints, information and objectives; the existence of an incentive does not determine its magnitude automatically. A person can respond on several margins. A consumer facing a higher fuel price may drive less, buy a more efficient vehicle, move closer to work, or reduce other spending. Those responses take different amounts of time and may be constrained. A short-run response need not equal a long-run response. Incentives can also change reporting instead of the underlying activity. Paying employees for a measured target may encourage real improvement, manipulation of the measurement, or neglect of unmeasured tasks. Good analysis asks what actions are rewarded and what the metric fails to capture. Private choice and social consequences A privately attractive action may impose costs on others. A firm may gain by producing more while its emissions affect neighboring residents. Conversely, a person who improves public health can create benefits beyond their own. These consequences are called external effects when they are not fully accounted for through the relevant decision's prices or arrangements. Separate the decision maker's calculation from the evaluation of everyone's consequences. To predict behavior, ask what the actor faces. To evaluate a policy, specify whose outcomes count, the feasible alternatives and any distributional concerns. A statement that an actor optimized does not prove that the outcome is socially desirable. Rules and institutions shape the feasible choices. Property rights, contracts, enforcement, professional norms and information systems can change the incentive structure. When a policy fails, examine the mechanism and implementation rather than assuming that a stated intention became the actual incentive. Optimization and equilibrium are different ideas Optimization describes an actor choosing the best feasible action under a specified objective. Equilibrium describes a set of choices or prices that are mutually consistent under a model. A competitive market model often requires each buyer and seller to choose appropriately given prices and the quantities supplied and demanded to balance. A point where supply equals demand is a market-clearing condition. It does not show by itself that choices are optimal, that adjustment reaches the point, or that the outcome is fair. A game-theoretic equilibrium uses a different consistency condition: strategies must be best responses to one another. Later chapters formalize both concepts. A model can have multiple equilibria or none under its assumptions. Equilibrium is an analytical concept; observed markets can contain rationing, queues, inventories, delayed contracts or price rules. Use a model's conditions to examine reality rather than assuming reality must satisfy every condition. Worked example 1 — A marginal production decision A producer can sell additional units for $20 each and make up to four additional units. Their incremental costs are $8, $12, $18 and $25. Assume there are no other consequences, each unit can be chosen sequentially, and these costs include all relevant opportunities. The incremental net benefits are 12, 8, 2 and −5 dollars. Producing three units adds $22 of value. Producing a fourth reduces total value to $17. If price falls to $15, the incremental net benefits become 7, 3, −3 and −10. Producing two units is best, adding $10. The response follows a margin: the third unit becomes unattractive when its $18 cost exceeds the new $15 benefit. A historical fixed cost already incurred does not change this additional-unit comparison unless it implies a distinct future consequence. Capacity also matters: even a higher price cannot create a fifth unit if the technology permits only four. Movements along a curve and shifts of a curve A demand relationship specifies quantities buyers would choose at different prices while other specified influences are held fixed. Changing price under that fixed relationship produces a movement along the curve. Changing income, preferences, expectations or another relevant input can change the relationship itself, producing a shift. A supply relationship similarly connects price and offered quantity while fixing technology, input costs and other relevant conditions. Cheaper inputs can shift supply even if the final product's price has not changed. To analyze a new equilibrium, identify which relationship changed, write the new rule and solve the consistency condition. These are conditional statements, not universal signs. A simple demand curve often slopes downward; exceptions and aggregation complications require more careful models. Holding other variables fixed is especially important when observed price and quantity both change because their equilibrium responds to several forces. Worked example 2 — A per-unit tax creates a wedge Buyers demand Q d = 120 − 2P b ; sellers supply Q s = 20 + 3P s . P b is the buyer's price and P s is the seller's receipt. Without tax, both equal $20 and quantity is 80. A $4 per-unit tax creates P b = P s + 4. 120 − 2(Pₛ + 4) = 20 + 3Pₛ 112 − 2Pₛ = 20 + 3Pₛ Pₛ = 92 / 5 = $18.40 Pᵦ = $22.40 Q = 20 + 3(18.40) = 75.20 Relative to the original price, buyers pay $2.40 more and sellers receive $1.60 less. Buyers bear 60% and sellers 40% of the $4 price wedge in this model. Statutory remittance—the party legally sending the tax payment—does not alone determine economic incidence. Tax receipts are 4 × 75.2 = $300.80 per modeled period. That revenue is not itself a complete welfare calculation; some effects are transfers, while changed quantities and external effects require additional analysis. The model excludes evasion, collection costs, market power and effects in other markets. Its fractional quantity is a continuous approximation. Adjustment and institutions If price is below a model's market-clearing price, quantity demanded can exceed quantity supplied. Calling this excess demand is a statement about the model's planned quantities. It does not tell you which buyer receives the available good. Queues, priority rules, lotteries, contracts or other mechanisms can allocate the supply. If price is above the clearing value, planned supply can exceed demand. Sellers might accumulate inventory or revise future production. Whether they cut prices depends on contracts, coordination, information and the relevant market structure. An explicit adjustment rule is needed to make a prediction about speed or stability. A price floor or ceiling changes feasible prices under a policy. If a ceiling lies below the unconstrained clearing price and the simple curves remain applicable, excess demand arises. The policy's actual effect depends on enforcement, quality changes, rationing and other responses. Describe these margins rather than stopping after naming a shortage. Elasticity measures responsiveness A slope measures change in units; an elasticity measures a proportional response. For a finite comparison from a positive base, a simple elasticity calculation divides the percentage change in quantity by the percentage change in price. The base convention must be stated. Later calculus gives point elasticity, and midpoint formulas offer a symmetric finite-change convention. Elasticity is useful because units such as dollars versus cents no longer change its value when the ratio is constructed consistently. It still depends on the starting point, range, time horizon and which other influences are held fixed. “Demand is inelastic” is incomplete without explaining where and over what horizon. In the tax model, the relatively more responsive supply side adjusts quantity more readily and bears less of the price wedge. This is a mechanism to analyze under stated competitive conditions. Incidence in settings with market power, incomplete contracts or dynamic responses can require a different model. Evaluate claims by tracing a mechanism For an incentive claim, identify the affected action, the changed consequence and the constraint. For an equilibrium claim, identify the consistency conditions. For a welfare claim, identify whose consequences are included. Then ask what evidence would support the proposed mechanism or distinguish it from alternatives. A policy can achieve one goal while creating another problem. That is a reason to compare the full consequences and feasible alternatives, not a reason to assume every intervention fails. Transparent analysis states trade-offs, uncertainty and the assumptions on which a recommendation rests. Exercises A producer sells at $12 per unit. The first four incremental-unit costs are $4, $8, $11 and $15. Find the best additional output and total incremental net benefit. Repeat at a $9 price. Demand is Q d = 100 − 2P and supply is Q s = 10 + P. Find the equilibrium. Then income changes demand to 130 − 2P while supply stays fixed. Find the new equilibrium and distinguish the shift from the movement along the unchanged supply curve. Using the original demand and supply from Exercise 2, impose a $6 per-unit tax with P b = P s + 6. Find buyer price, seller price, quantity, buyer and seller shares of the price wedge, and tax receipts. Using the original untaxed model in Exercise 2, set a binding price ceiling of $20. Find planned demand, planned supply and excess demand. Explain why the model does not identify who gets the goods. Price rises from $10 to $12 while quantity demanded falls from 50 to 45 along a fixed relationship. Calculate the percentage changes using the original bases and their ratio. Why would a different finite-change convention need to be stated? A school rewards teachers for a single test score. Describe a response that improves learning and a response that improves the score while harming another educational objective. Identify the relevant measurement limitation. A firm chooses output optimally given prices, but its emissions harm neighbors and are unpriced. Explain why private optimization alone does not establish social efficiency. Identify what a policy comparison would need to include. A commentator observes price and quantity increasing together and concludes that demand curves slope upward. Give a demand-shift explanation consistent with downward-sloping demand. State what additional information is needed to estimate a demand response. Solutions and evaluation criteria At $12, net increments are 8,4,1,−3; produce three for $13. At $9, increments are 5,1,−2,−6; produce two for $6. Compare the cumulative alternatives as a check. 100 − 2P = 10 + P gives P = 30 and Q = 40. After the demand shift, 130 − 2P = 10 + P gives P = 40 and Q = 50. Demand's rule changed; suppliers move along their unchanged relationship as price rises. 100 − 2(P s + 6) = 10 + P s gives P s = 26, P b = 32 and Q = 36. Buyers pay $2 more than 30 and sellers receive $4 less, giving one-third and two-thirds of the $6 wedge. Tax receipts are 6 × 36 = $216. At 20, planned demand is 60 and supply is 30, leaving excess demand of 30. Allocation requires a rationing or trading institution; the two equations alone do not specify it. Price rises 20%; quantity falls 10%; the ratio is −0.5. Original-base and midpoint conventions can differ for finite changes, so an analyst must identify the convention. The ratio describes the stipulated fixed relationship, not an arbitrary observational association. Teachers may improve comprehension through better instruction. They may also narrow instruction to tested items or manipulate reporting. Evaluate actual learning across relevant objectives, not only the rewarded measure. Several coherent mechanisms are acceptable. The firm compares private benefits and costs while omitting the neighbors' harm. A policy analysis must consider that harm, changes in production and consumption, implementation costs, distribution, and feasible alternatives. Private optimality under incomplete prices is insufficient to establish efficiency. An outward demand shift can raise both clearing price and quantity while each demand curve slopes downward. Estimating movement along demand requires separating supply-driven price variation from demand shifts and addressing confounding. Observation of equilibrium changes alone cannot identify the curve. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain the difference between an actor's optimum, market clearing, and a socially desirable outcome. Use a tax wedge to explain why the remitting party need not bear the whole economic burden. Delayed check: invent a supply-demand model with a price rule or tax, solve its conditions, and describe an institutional detail that could change the real-world response. Where this leads Microeconomics formalizes preferences, technology, competition and strategic behavior. Public economics analyzes incidence, externalities and distribution. Institutional analysis examines implementation. Econometrics asks whether a mechanism is supported by credible evidence. Incentive and equilibrium reasoning connects those fields."}, {"title": "01.07 · Prices, inflation and purchasing power", "url": "v01-c07.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Separate money amounts from purchasing power A higher salary does not necessarily buy more goods and services. If money income rises by 5% while the prices relevant to a household rise by 8%, the household's purchasing power falls. To analyze this, distinguish a nominal amount, a price level or index, and a real amount expressed on a specified purchasing-power basis. The price of a good is the money required per unit of that good. A relative price compares its price with another price. An economy contains many prices, so a general price index summarizes selected movements using specified items and weights. It is a measurement construction, not a price that every household literally pays. Inflation is the percentage increase in a specified price index over a specified interval. State which index, which population or activity it represents, the interval, and whether the comparison uses adjusted or unadjusted values. A statement that “prices rose 4%” leaves important measurement choices unstated. Construct a simple fixed-basket index Choose a basket of quantities q i,0 in a base period, and price that same basket in each comparison period. Let p i,t be the price of item i in period t. An illustrative fixed-basket index is: Basket cost at time t = Σ pᵢ,ₜ qᵢ,₀ Price index Iₜ = 100 × basket cost at t / basket cost in the base period The base period's index is 100 by construction. An index of 120 means the specified basket costs 20% more than in the base period. It does not mean that every item rose 20%, that every household's expenditures rose 20%, or that inflation during the latest period was 20%. Inflation between two dates uses their ratio: Inflation from s to t = Iₜ / Iₛ − 1 If an index moves from 120 to 126, inflation is 126/120 − 1 = 5%, not 6%. The six-index-point increase is a difference in index levels. The preceding chapter's denominator discipline applies directly. Worked example 1 — Price a basket and deflate income A hypothetical basket contains two loaves of bread and three transit trips. In the base period, bread costs $2 and a trip costs $10. Later, bread costs $3 and a trip costs $11. These are illustrative prices, not observations about October 2026. Base basket cost is 2 × 2 + 3 × 10 = $34. Later basket cost is 2 × 3 + 3 × 11 = $39. The later index is 100 × 39/34 ≈ 114.71. Basket inflation is 39/34 − 1 ≈ 14.71%. If nominal income rises from $3,400 to $3,900, it buys 100 baskets in each period. Purchasing power relative to this basket is unchanged. Bread rises 50%, while trips rise 10%. The basket inflation is neither the unweighted mean of those rates nor a statement that every person experiences the same change. Base expenditure weights are 4/34 for bread and 30/34 for trips. Their weighted price changes reproduce the basket's 5/34 increase. Deflate a nominal amount To express a nominal amount N t in the base period's purchasing power when the index is based at 100, divide by I t /100: Real amount in base-period money = Nₜ / (Iₜ / 100) More generally, to express an amount at time t in the price basis of period b, multiply by I b /I t . An income of $63,000 when the index is 126 equals $50,000 in the price basis of an index at 100. The calculation is conditional on the chosen index being appropriate for the question. For growth between two dates, use nominal growth factor divided by the price growth factor. A nominal wage increase of 6% with 8% inflation produces a real growth factor of 1.06/1.08, or approximately −1.85% real growth. Simply subtracting the rates gives a −2% approximation. Comparisons also need consistent populations and hours. An increase in average annual earnings can reflect more hours or a changing worker composition. A real hourly wage for comparable work answers a different question from average annual nominal earnings across a changing population. Worked example 2 — A wage gain with a purchasing-power loss A worker earns $50,000 at time 0 and $53,000 at time 1. The relevant illustrative price index moves from 100 to 108. Nominal wage growth is 53,000/50,000 − 1 = 6%. Price growth is 108/100 − 1 = 8%. The later wage in time-0 purchasing power is 53,000/1.08 ≈ $49,074.07. Real wage growth is 1.06/1.08 − 1 ≈ −1.85%. The conclusion concerns purchasing power under the chosen basket and assumes comparable work. If the worker's expenditure basket differs substantially, another index could be more informative. Changes in taxes, household size or working hours require separate analysis rather than being silently attributed to inflation. Inflation, disinflation and deflation Inflation describes a rising price index. Disinflation describes a reduction in its positive rate of increase: for example, annual inflation dropping from 8% to 3%. The index can continue rising. Deflation describes a fall in the index, meaning a negative inflation rate over the relevant interval. If an index starts at 100, rises 8%, then rises 3%, it becomes 100 × 1.08 × 1.03 = 111.24. Inflation slowed, but the final price level is 11.24% above the starting level. A slowing increase is different from a reversal. A single good becoming cheaper is not necessarily general deflation. Likewise, an increase in one highly visible price does not establish the inflation rate of a broad basket. Relative price changes can be large even when the general price level changes little. Measurement limits and appropriate indices An index must define the population, items, weights, measurement timing and treatment of changing products. A fixed basket is transparent, but people can substitute between goods when relative prices change. Quality can improve or deteriorate; new goods appear; the relevant basket can evolve. A complete cost-of-living comparison asks how much spending would maintain a specified level of wellbeing, which is more demanding than repricing an unchanged basket. The U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics explains that CPI averages need not match an individual household's experience and that its index is not a complete measure of every factor affecting living standards. Use its documentation for the actual index's scope and methodology. Our two-item calculations are teaching models, not replicas of its full statistical process. BLS CPI documentation . Consumer-price, producer-price and output-price indices answer different questions. A measure designed for household consumption is not automatically the best deflator for a firm's output or a national production aggregate. Choose the index for the conceptual quantity being analyzed and document the match. Index bases and comparisons Rebasing an index changes its scale without changing proportional movements. If the current series is 100, 110 and 121, rebasing the middle date to 100 produces 90.909…, 100 and 110. Both series imply a 10% increase in each interval. Differences in displayed index levels across series do not establish differences in their current purchasing power. Comparing places is another question. An inflation index tracks changes through time within its defined setting; it does not by itself provide a cross-country price-level comparison. Exchange rates convert currencies but can differ from purchasing-power comparisons. Later international economics develops those distinctions. When comparing data releases, distinguish the reference period from the release date. Some series are revised or seasonally adjusted. Record the exact version used in an analysis. This chapter makes no claim about the latest inflation reading; it teaches how to interpret a documented series. Why this matters for decisions Real and nominal consistency affects wages, budgets, investment returns and contract values. A fixed nominal payment can lose purchasing power when prices rise. A nominal debt obligation and a variable-rate debt contract respond differently to inflation and rate changes. The effects also depend on expectations and contractual terms, so a general statement that “inflation helps borrowers” needs qualifications. For analysis, state the price basis of every real number, keep the time period consistent, and preserve the distinction between money gains and purchasing-power gains. The arithmetic is simple enough to audit independently. The harder step is choosing the appropriate economic measure. Exercises A fixed basket contains four units of A and one unit of B. Base prices are $5 and $20; later prices are $6 and $22. Calculate both basket costs, the later base-100 index and inflation. An index rises from 125 to 130. Calculate the index-point increase and the percentage change. Explain why they differ. A salary is $60,000 when the price index is 120. Express it in the purchasing-power basis where the index is 100. If nominal salary rises to $63,000 while the index rises to 126, calculate real salary growth. An asset gains 12% nominally over a period in which the relevant price index rises 7%. Calculate its exact real return. A price index starts at 100 and rises 10%, then 2%. Find the final level. Explain whether the second period has inflation, disinflation or deflation. Rebase the index series 80, 100, 120 so that the first period equals 100. Show that the final-to-middle percentage change is unchanged. A household spends 80% of its base budget on category X and 20% on Y. X prices rise 5% and Y prices rise 20%, with the fixed basket otherwise unchanged. Calculate its basket inflation. Compare with a household whose weights are reversed. A report concludes that every household became 4% worse off because a consumer-price index rose 4%. Identify at least four missing pieces of information or conceptual problems. Give a more defensible statement. Solutions and evaluation criteria Base cost is 4 × 5 + 20 = $40. Later cost is 4 × 6 + 22 = $46. The index is 115 and inflation is 15%. The increase is 5 index points. Percentage growth is 5/125 = 4%. The index-point difference uses the scale; the percentage comparison uses the earlier level as denominator. The first salary is 60,000/1.20 = $50,000 in base purchasing power. The later one is 63,000/1.26 = $50,000. Real growth is zero: nominal salary and the index both rise 5%. Real return is 1.12/1.07 − 1 ≈ 4.67%. The five-percentage-point subtraction is only an approximation. The final index is 100 × 1.10 × 1.02 = 112.2. The second period has 2% inflation and disinflation relative to the earlier 10% rate. It does not have deflation. Multiply all values by 100/80 = 1.25 to obtain 100,125,150. The final-to-middle change is 120/100 − 1 = 20% in the original series and 150/125 − 1 = 20% in the rebased series. First basket inflation is 0.8 × 5% + 0.2 × 20% = 8%. Reversed weights produce 17%. This uses fixed quantities and base expenditure weights; the two households have different exposure. Needed information includes nominal income changes, household-specific expenditure patterns, taxes, household composition, relevant nonmarket outcomes and what “worse off” means. A defensible statement is that the specified index's measured price level rose 4% over the identified interval. Individual purchasing power and wellbeing require further analysis. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain why inflation falling does not mean prices have fallen. Demonstrate with three index levels and distinguish a relative price from a general price index. Delayed check: construct a new fixed basket, compute its inflation, rebase its index, and deflate a nominal income without changing the underlying economic comparison. Where this leads Macroeconomics explains inflation mechanisms and policy. Statistics develops index measurement. Finance requires real-nominal consistency in valuation and returns. Distributional analysis asks how different households experience the same set of price changes. Accurate interpretation precedes any policy conclusion."}, {"title": "01.08 · Risk, uncertainty and evidence", "url": "v01-c08.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "A decision can be good before its outcome is known Some choices have uncertain consequences. A business expansion can succeed or fail; a forecast can be wrong; an apparently safe receipt can arrive late. Evaluating a decision requires distinguishing the information available when the choice was made from the outcome that eventually occurred. A sound decision can lead to a disappointing realization, and a poorly reasoned gamble can happen to succeed. Evaluate the process using the probabilities, alternatives, constraints and information that were reasonably available at the decision time. Then use the realized outcome to improve future beliefs where it genuinely contains information. Define the outcomes before assigning probabilities. A scenario set should identify distinct possibilities and avoid overlap or omitted cases when it is presented as complete. “Revenue rises,” “revenue doubles,” and “revenue falls” are not three exclusive cases if doubling is included in rising. Make their definitions precise. Probabilities and expectations A probability lies between zero and one. Probabilities assigned to a complete set of mutually exclusive outcomes sum to one. Probabilities can arise from a specified chance mechanism, repeated observations under suitable conditions, or informed judgments. State their basis. Historical frequencies do not automatically apply to a changed environment. The expected value of a quantity is its probability-weighted average across outcomes: Expected value = Σ probability of outcome i × value in outcome i An expectation is not a guaranteed outcome and may not itself be one of the possible outcomes. If a project pays either $100 or −$50, an expected value of $55 does not mean the project literally pays $55 in a third scenario. Use expectations carefully. Expected revenue minus expected expense equals expected profit when the quantities are defined consistently, but a nonlinear function of an expectation generally differs from the expectation of that function. Later probability and decision theory formalize these distinctions. For now, compute each scenario's consequence first when the rule is nonlinear. Worked example 1 — Expected profit does not settle every decision Project A has net profit of $100 with probability 0.7 and a net loss of $50 with probability 0.3. Project B yields a certain net profit of $40. The choices are exclusive and occur over the same horizon. A's expected net profit is 0.7 × 100 + 0.3 × (−50) = $55. B's expected net profit is $40. A exceeds B by $15 in expected monetary profit. A still has a 30% probability of losing $50, while B has no loss in the specified model. An actor who maximizes expected monetary profit and can absorb the outcomes chooses A. An actor with a severe liquidity constraint or a strong preference for avoiding the loss may choose B. Neither preference can be inferred from the expectation alone. Specify the objective and financial capacity before making a recommendation. If A's success probability is p rather than a fixed 0.7, expected profit is 100p − 50(1 − p) = 150p − 50. It exceeds B's $40 when p > 0.6. The threshold shows which belief matters for the choice. Risk, uncertainty and ambiguity Usage differs across fields, but a useful distinction is between outcomes modeled with a specified probability distribution and uncertainty about the distribution itself. The latter is often called ambiguity. Knowing that profits can be +100 or −50 does not establish their probabilities. Where probabilities are poorly supported, present scenarios and sensitivity rather than assigning precision without evidence. For the project above, examine p across a plausible range. If all plausible values imply the same decision, the comparison is robust to that uncertainty. If the threshold lies inside the plausible range, additional information may be valuable. Different risks can interact. A household may lose income precisely when its investment performs badly. Treating the outcomes as independent can understate the bad combined scenario. Later covariance and dependence models quantify such relationships. At this stage, explicitly list which joint outcomes are feasible and why their probabilities are assigned. Downside and capacity to absorb loss Expected value averages across outcomes. It does not describe the worst case, the probability of a cash shortage, the size of losses when they occur, or whether an actor survives to benefit from favorable outcomes. A project with a high expected payoff can be infeasible if a required payment cannot be financed in a bad state. Describe the distribution and constraints relevant to the decision. Useful questions include: How much could be lost? When would cash be needed? Which obligations still fall due? What outcomes would violate a constraint? Can the actor delay, hedge, insure, reduce the position or obtain committed financing? The value of an outcome need not equal its money amount. Losing a small sum from a large buffer may have a different consequence from losing the same sum when it prevents essential spending. Expected-utility models later formalize preferences over uncertain outcomes. They do not eliminate the need to state whose objective and circumstances are being analyzed. Worked example 2 — Interpret a screening signal In an illustrative population of 10,000 applicants, 1% will default. A screening rule flags 90% of future defaulters and also flags 10% of applicants who will not default. Assume these rates apply to this population and that every applicant belongs to one of the two outcome groups. Actual outcome Applicants Flagged Not flagged Default 100 90 10 No default 9,900 990 8,910 Total 10,000 1,080 8,920 Among flagged applicants, 90/1,080 = 1/12 ≈ 8.33% default. The rule catches 90% of all defaults, but that does not mean 90% of flagged applicants default. The low original default frequency and the false-positive rate both matter. The example teaches conditional reasoning, not an actual credit decision. A lending policy would also need the costs of errors, repayment economics, legal constraints, fairness, measurement quality and whether the model transports to a new population. A signal's accuracy statistic alone cannot determine a responsible policy. Information can change a decision Information is valuable when it can improve the choice under the specified objective. Perfect information tells the relevant state before acting. Imperfect information changes beliefs without resolving all uncertainty. More information does not automatically justify a cost: it may not change the best action, or it may arrive after the decision. For an expected-money objective, compute expected payoff when decisions can respond to the signal, then subtract the best expected payoff without the signal. This is the gross value of information under the model. Its net value subtracts the cost of obtaining and using it. Later Bayesian decision theory develops the general calculation. Be careful about hindsight. Information acquired after a loss may explain what happened, but it could not guide a prior decision unless it was available then. Retrospective evaluations should reconstruct the information set at the decision time. Evidence and confidence Separate a claim from the observation offered to support it. An isolated success, an average from selected survivors, and a controlled comparison have different evidential value. Ask who was observed, who was omitted, how quantities were measured, and which alternative explanations remain possible. A large dataset can measure an association precisely while leaving its causal interpretation unresolved. A complicated forecast can be overfit. An impressive backtest can use information that was unavailable at the supposed decision time. Evidence needs a credible design and a transparent workflow, not only many observations or advanced terminology. Use uncertainty statements that identify what is uncertain: the outcome, the probability, the measured data, the causal effect, or the model itself. “We are uncertain” is less useful than explaining which assumption changes the conclusion and what evidence would resolve it. Exercises A project returns net profit of $200 with probability 0.4 and a net loss of $100 with probability 0.6. Calculate its expectation. Compare it with a certain $10 profit under an expected-money objective, and state a reason another objective could change the decision. A payoff is +$200 with probability p and −$100 otherwise. Find the success-probability threshold for positive expected profit. Evaluate the expectation at p = 0.3 and p = 0.5. Scenarios labeled “growth,” “growth above 10%,” and “decline” receive probabilities 0.5, 0.3 and 0.2. Explain why summing to one does not make them a valid exclusive scenario set. Rewrite categories that could be valid. In a population of 1,000, 10% have a specified condition. A signal detects 80% of those cases and falsely flags 10% of those without it. Find the probability of the condition among flagged cases using a count table. A loss of $100 occurs with probability 0.05; otherwise the loss is zero. Full insurance costs $6 and pays the $100 loss if it occurs. Calculate expected net monetary outcomes without and with insurance, ignoring all other consequences. Explain why the expected-money comparison does not decide every person's preference. A project yields +$80 in a favorable state with probability 0.6 and −$40 otherwise. Not undertaking it yields zero. Compute the best expected payoff without information, the expected payoff with perfect information before choosing, and the gross value of perfect information. You have only $30 available, no ability to borrow, and a project may require a $50 payment in a bad state. It has a positive expected profit. Explain why feasibility must be examined separately from expected profitability. A fund reports the performance of ten surviving strategies but excludes fifty discontinued strategies. Write a short critique identifying what evidence is missing, which inference is threatened, and what a better evaluation would require. Do not assume that all discontinued strategies lost money. Solutions and evaluation criteria The expectation is 0.4 × 200 − 0.6 × 100 = $20. It exceeds the certain $10 by $10 for the stated objective. A downside constraint or preference over risk can favor the certain alternative; identify the particular condition instead of asserting a universal preference. Expectation is 200p − 100(1 − p) = 300p − 100. It is positive for p > 1/3 and zero at p = 1/3. At 0.3 it is −$10; at 0.5 it is $50. “Growth above 10%” is included within “growth,” so the events overlap; unchanged output is also omitted unless assigned somewhere. A valid partition could be growth above 10%, growth from 0% through 10%, and negative growth, with zero explicitly in the middle category. Assign justified probabilities to those revised events. There are 100 cases, of which 80 are flagged, and 900 noncases, of which 90 are flagged. Of 170 flagged observations, 80 are cases: 80/170 ≈ 47.06%. Detection rate and the probability conditional on a flag are different. Without insurance, the expected net outcome is −$5. With full insurance and a certain premium, it is −$6 in either state. A risk-neutral expected-money objective favors being uninsured by $1. Loss aversion, risk aversion or a cash constraint can create a preference for insurance. Actual policies can also have exclusions and counterparty risk absent here. Expected project payoff is 0.6 × 80 − 0.4 × 40 = $32, exceeding zero. With perfect information, undertake it only in the favorable state: 0.6 × 80 + 0.4 × 0 = $48. Gross information value is $16 under the objective and assumptions. The bad-state obligation exceeds available cash by $20. Unless the contract or another feasible arrangement avoids or finances it, the project violates the stated constraint. Averaging across states does not produce cash in the bad state. The omitted strategies create selection and survivorship concerns. Ask for the full defined population, launch/closure dates, returns, costs, benchmarks and closure reasons. Reconstruct evaluation using the information available at each date. The critique should identify the threat without inventing the missing performance. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain why a high detection rate does not imply a high probability of the condition after a positive signal. Use counts to make the denominator visible. Delayed check: reconstruct a two-state decision, vary the success probability, and identify the threshold at which the recommendation changes. Then add a cash constraint and explain its separate role. Where this leads Probability formalizes distributions and conditioning. Statistics quantifies sampling uncertainty. Econometrics evaluates identification. Finance analyzes risk-bearing, hedging and asset prices. Decision theory connects evidence to actions through objectives and losses. The foundational habit is to keep outcomes, beliefs, evidence and constraints distinct."}, {"title": "01.09 · Money, credit and financial institutions", "url": "v01-c09.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Trace the claims before telling the story Money and credit become easier to understand when you identify who holds an asset and who owes the corresponding obligation. A household's deposit is its claim on a bank. A bank's loan is its claim on a borrower. These relationships do not become clearer when described only as money “moving around.” Write the parties' balance-sheet changes. Money commonly serves as a means of payment, a unit in which prices and obligations are stated, and a way to carry purchasing power across time. These functions need not be perfectly fulfilled by every instrument. Inflation changes purchasing power; a payment arrangement can impose access or settlement constraints; an asset may be valuable without being routinely accepted for payment. A loan is a credit contract. It specifies an advance or borrowing right, repayment obligations, timing, interest and other terms. The borrowing is an asset acquisition and obligation at origination, not earned income. The lender's expected economic value depends on repayment, default, costs and discounting, not only the face amount promised. Deposits and reserves are different claims In a simplified two-tier banking model, customers hold commercial-bank deposits, while banks hold reserve balances used to settle with one another through the central bank's accounts. A customer's balance and the bank's reserve balance are different instruments held by different entities. A deposit liability is not automatically matched by an equal quantity of cash held aside for that specific depositor. A bank holds a portfolio of assets and finances it with deposits, other liabilities and equity. Its ability to meet payments depends on available liquidity and funding, while its solvency depends on the value of its assets relative to obligations. The Bank of England's 2014 account explains that commercial-bank lending can create deposits and that a simple story of banks mechanically multiplying a fixed quantity of central-bank money is misleading. The discussion is a conceptual source, not an assertion about every jurisdiction's current operating rules. Bank of England, Money creation in the modern economy . A loan can create a deposit Consider a modeled bank granting a loan and crediting the borrower's deposit at that same bank. The bank records a loan asset and a matching deposit liability. The borrower records a deposit asset and a loan liability. Neither party gains equity merely from recording equal new claims at the same initial amount. This accounting possibility does not imply unlimited profitable lending. The bank must assess repayment risk, expected income and costs, capital, liquidity, funding, contractual obligations and applicable rules. Borrowers must want and qualify for credit. Subsequent payments can require settlement resources. An accounting entry is not the complete economics of a sustainable banking business. Paying loan principal using a deposit at the same bank can reduce both the loan asset and the deposit liability. Interest has a different effect because it is a payment for financing under the contract and can affect income and equity. Record the allocation between principal, interest and fees explicitly. Worked example 1 — Origination and a payment to another bank Bank A begins with reserves of $100 and loans of $400. It owes customer deposits of $450 and has equity of $50. Its identity is 100 + 400 = 450 + 50. It grants a new $50 loan and credits the borrower's deposit. Loans rise to $450 and deposits to $500. Reserves remain $100 and equity $50. Assets and liabilities plus equity both become $550. The borrower pays $40 to a seller at Bank B. Assume settlement transfers $40 of reserves from A to B. Bank A's reserves fall to $60 and deposits to $460. Its loans remain $450. Its balance sheet is 60 + 450 = 460 + 50. Bank B gains $40 reserves and records a $40 deposit for the seller. Its equity does not change from that payment alone. The cross-bank payment changes the location of deposits and reserves. It does not mean that the borrower's loan vanished. If the borrower later repays $10 of principal using a deposit at A, A's loans and deposits both fall $10: 60 + 440 = 450 + 50. Every step identifies the entity and the paired entries. The example assumes available settlement resources and no fees or default. Different settlement arrangements require corresponding additional entries; the accounting must match the specified institution. Payments, settlement and liquidity A payment instruction, the customer's balance change, and final settlement are conceptually distinct steps. An instruction might be authorized before the receiving party has an unconditional settled claim. A payment arrangement defines how obligations are transferred or discharged and what happens if a participant cannot meet them. Liquidity is the ability to obtain the resources needed to meet obligations when due at acceptable cost. An institution can own valuable assets and still have difficulty obtaining cash quickly. Selling a long-horizon asset immediately may require a discount; borrowing can require collateral and willing counterparties. Solvency concerns whether assets adequately cover obligations under an appropriate economic valuation. A shortfall of immediate cash is not identical to negative net worth. They can interact: distress sales can reduce asset values, and doubts about solvency can make funding harder to obtain. Later banking and crisis chapters analyze the feedback. Worked example 2 — A withdrawal and a credit loss Start again with a bank holding $100 of immediately usable settlement resources and $400 of loans, financed by $450 deposits and $50 equity. Assume no other claims or valuation differences. Depositors withdraw $80 using those resources. Cash/reserves fall to $20 and deposits to $370. Assets are now $420 and liabilities $370. Equity remains $50: withdrawals reduce an asset and a liability together. A further immediate $30 withdrawal exceeds the $20 available resources. The bank needs another feasible funding or asset-conversion arrangement. Separately, suppose the loans lose $60 of economic value and the loss is recognized. Loan value becomes $340; total assets become $360. With liabilities still $370, economic equity becomes −$10. The cash shortfall and value shortfall are different problems. Borrowing another $10 can cover a payment only if financing is available; it adds both cash and an obligation and does not automatically repair negative equity. New equity, asset recovery or a restructuring can affect solvency in different ways. A real response requires contractual, institutional and legal analysis. Other financial institutions and securities A bond represents a specified debt claim. Equity represents an ownership claim whose payoff depends on the entity and the rights attached to the instrument. Funds pool investments under a mandate. Insurers combine underwriting, investment and contractual payout obligations. Exchanges and intermediaries support trading and transfer of claims. The labels do not remove risk. A debt promise can default. An ownership claim can lose value. An investment vehicle can have redemption constraints and fees. To understand an instrument, identify cash flows, claim priority, contingencies, timing, liquidity and governance. Later volumes develop valuation and specific structures. Intermediation can provide useful services: collecting information, monitoring borrowers, matching financial needs, pooling risks and facilitating payments. It can also create incentive conflicts and fragility. Evaluate the actual mechanism rather than assuming that every intermediary performs the same function. Central-bank roles and limits of a simple story A central bank can occupy roles involving currency, settlement, monetary policy and financial stability, with responsibilities depending on its legal and institutional setting. Commercial banks and a central bank have different balance sheets, objectives and powers. A central-bank asset purchase exchanges claims; understanding its effects requires tracing the counterparties and payment mechanism. Do not infer that expanding one monetary quantity guarantees a fixed increase in lending, prices or output. The consequences depend on behavior, constraints, expectations and the policy framework. Monetary economics later connects these mechanisms to equilibrium and evidence. For any current institutional claim, identify the jurisdiction and date and use the relevant official source. This introductory model does not state current reserve requirements, deposit-insurance limits, policy rates or emergency lending terms. Those are dated facts, while the paired-entry reasoning is a reusable analytical tool. Exercises A bank has $80 reserves, $320 loans, $350 deposits and $50 equity. It grants a $40 loan by crediting a deposit. Write its new balance sheet and the borrower's paired entries. What happens to equity at origination? Following Exercise 1, the borrower pays $25 to a customer at another bank, settled by transferring reserves. Show the first bank's new reserves, loans, deposits and equity. Show the receiving bank's transaction changes. Following Exercise 2, $15 of loan principal is repaid from a deposit at the first bank. Show the balance-sheet changes and closing identity, excluding interest and fees. A bank has assets of $600, liabilities of $540 and equity of $60. It recognizes a $75 asset-value loss. Find closing assets and equity with liabilities unchanged. Explain why borrowing $20 at face value does not fix the net-worth shortfall by itself. An institution has valuable assets of $200 due next year, $10 cash, and a $25 payment due today. Assume it has positive net worth. Identify the immediate liquidity problem and two possible responses whose feasibility would need checking. A household borrows $500 and immediately buys equipment for $500. Ignoring fees and valuation differences, trace its cash, equipment, debt and net-worth changes. Explain why the borrowing is not $500 of income. Someone says, “If banks can create deposits when lending, they can lend profitably without limits.” Identify at least five omitted economic or institutional constraints. Compare a deposit, a bond and an equity claim using holder, issuer obligation, contingent payoff, timing and liquidity. Explain why neither the name nor the promised face value proves safety. Solutions and evaluation criteria The bank has reserves 80, loans 360, deposits 390 and equity 50: 440 = 390 + 50. The borrower gains a $40 deposit asset and a $40 loan obligation. Equity is unchanged at initial equal values. The first bank has reserves 55, loans 360, deposits 365 and equity 50: 415 = 365 + 50. The receiving bank gains $25 reserves and a matching $25 customer deposit liability, with no equity change from the transfer alone. Loans become 345 and deposits 350; reserves remain 55 and equity 50. Assets are 400 = 350 + 50. Principal repayment reduces both loan asset and deposit liability. Assets fall to 525, so equity is 525 − 540 = −$15. Borrowing $20 raises both assets and liabilities by $20, leaving equity −$15 absent other consequences. The institution lacks $15 of immediately usable resources. It might sell or pledge an asset, obtain a loan, secure a capital contribution or renegotiate timing. Each option has costs and conditions; positive net worth does not guarantee immediate access. Loan origination raises cash and debt by $500. The purchase exchanges the $500 cash for $500 equipment. Net worth remains unchanged in the specified initial-value accounting. Income arises from earning or relevant subsequent gains, not the equal borrowing entries. Relevant constraints include borrower demand, creditworthiness, expected losses, funding costs, capital, liquidity and settlement, regulation, collateral, competition and risk appetite. The answer should explain how several affect feasible or profitable lending. Identify the actual contract and parties. A deposit is a claim on a bank, a bond has specified debt terms, and equity has ownership rights and contingent residual outcomes. Liquidity and repayment depend on the instrument and setting. Do not invent universal insurance or legal guarantees. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: trace a loan, an interbank payment, and a principal repayment on both sides of the transactions. Explain the distinction between reserve resources and a customer's deposit. Delayed check: construct a balance-sheet case with a payment shortage but positive net worth, then one with enough immediate cash but negative economic net worth. Explain why the remedies differ. Where this leads Accounting formalizes measurement. Corporate finance studies financing claims. Banking develops intermediation, capital and liquidity. Monetary economics examines policy transmission. Crisis analysis studies how claims, funding and asset values interact under stress. All depend on tracing who owes what to whom."}, {"title": "01.10 · Models, causation and economic arguments", "url": "v01-c10.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Four kinds of statement An economic argument can contain definitions, accounting identities, behavioral models, and empirical claims. Confusing them allows weak reasoning to appear stronger than it is. Ask what kind of statement is being made and what would establish it. A definition specifies how a concept is used. An identity follows from the defined components, such as assets equaling liabilities plus equity. A behavioral model describes how choices or outcomes depend on assumptions. An empirical claim concerns the world and requires evidence from an appropriate measurement and research design. A policy recommendation adds values and a choice among alternatives. Evidence that a policy raises total output does not alone establish that it should be adopted: distribution, costs, rights, feasibility and other objectives may matter. Separate the positive claim about consequences from the normative judgment about which consequences are desirable. Models isolate mechanisms A model deliberately abstracts from some features to examine others. It should define the actors, objectives, constraints, information, sequence of events and consistency conditions. Its conclusion follows conditionally from those choices. The question is whether the abstraction clarifies the mechanism relevant to the problem. “Holding other things fixed” makes a conditional comparison. A demand response to price with income fixed differs from a historical change in which price, income, product quality and expectations all moved. A model can help interpret that historical change, but the data do not automatically isolate the model's particular response. Assess a model by its logic, its relevance and its confrontation with evidence. An unrealistic assumption can still help isolate a useful mechanism, but a conclusion that depends critically on a false institutional feature can mislead. State which assumptions are essential and test how the conclusion changes when they are relaxed. Causation requires a counterfactual A causal effect compares what happens under an intervention with what would happen without that intervention for the relevant units and setting. The unobserved alternative is the counterfactual . Observing an outcome after an action does not reveal the counterfactual by itself. If a company trains workers and productivity later rises, possible explanations include training, improving demand, new equipment, worker selection or measurement changes. A causal claim needs a way to distinguish those mechanisms. Naming plausible alternatives is a useful start, but not an estimate of their magnitudes. Identification concerns whether the target causal or structural quantity can be recovered under the stated information and assumptions. Estimation then calculates it from finite data. A precisely estimated association can remain causally unidentified. Large sample size addresses some sampling uncertainty; it does not automatically repair confounding. Worked example 1 — Why a before-and-after change is incomplete A shop's weekly sales rise from $1,000 to $1,300 after it begins advertising. The manager attributes the entire $300 increase to the advertisement. During the same period, a comparable untreated shop's sales rise from $1,000 to $1,200. The treated shop's before-after increase is $300. The comparison shop's increase is $200. The difference in those changes is $100. Under an assumption that the comparison's $200 trend describes the treated shop's counterfactual change without advertising, the implied advertising effect is $100. The fourth step is the crucial assumption. The shops may face different customers, demand shocks or spillovers. They may not be comparable even if their original sales levels match. The $100 calculation is transparent, but its causal interpretation remains conditional on the design. This introduces the intuition of a difference-in-differences comparison. The advanced econometrics volume examines its assumptions, treatment timing, heterogeneous effects and inference in detail. A single illustrative pair of shops cannot validate those conditions. Randomization and comparisons Random assignment can make treatment and control groups comparable in expectation before the intervention, helping distinguish its effect from systematic selection. The intervention must be implemented and measured consistently. Attrition, noncompliance, interference across participants and chance imbalance can still complicate interpretation. Random assignment is different from random sampling. Assignment concerns who receives an intervention and can support internal causal comparisons. Sampling concerns who enters a study and influences how results generalize to a larger population. A randomized intervention among volunteers does not automatically identify effects for everyone elsewhere. Observational studies can also support causal reasoning when their design and assumptions are credible. Potential strategies include using a documented assignment threshold, an external source of variation or a carefully justified comparison. Each has conditions and limitations. The label attached to a method cannot substitute for examining them. Worked example 2 — Describe an experiment's result honestly In an illustrative randomized study, 200 participants are assigned to a program and 200 to control. At the specified follow-up, 40 program participants and 60 control participants have the adverse outcome. Assume outcomes are observed for everyone, assignment is implemented as intended and there are no spillovers. The observed program rate is 40/200 = 20%. The observed control rate is 60/200 = 30%. The observed difference is −10 percentage points. The relative reduction compared with the control rate is (30% − 20%)/30% = 1/3, approximately 33.33%. State the population, intervention, outcome and horizon. These figures are sample results, not proof that the exact population effect is ten percentage points or that the same effect applies in a different setting. Statistical inference quantifies sampling or assignment uncertainty under appropriate assumptions. For a policy decision, also ask about costs, implementation, other outcomes and distribution. A causally interpretable benefit is important evidence, but it is not the entire decision criterion. Prediction answers a different question A predictor aims to forecast an outcome using information available before the forecast target is realized. A causal analysis asks what would happen if an action were changed. A variable can be highly predictive without being a useful intervention. An umbrella can predict rain because people carry one when they expect rain. Giving everyone an umbrella does not create rainfall. In an economic setting, a company's expansion announcement may predict future sales because management already knows demand is improving. Changing the announcement alone need not generate the sales increase. Predictive evaluation must respect timing. If a backtest uses next month's information in a supposed decision today, it benefits from leakage. If a model is repeatedly modified until it fits the test period, that period is no longer an independent test. Later computation and econometrics provide disciplined evaluation procedures. Falsifiability and competing explanations A useful claim states what observations would be consistent with it and what would count against it. “The policy always works, except whenever it does not” provides no meaningful test. A model can specify conditional predictions whose validity depends on measurable conditions. Do not treat failure to reject a claim as proof that it is true. Evidence can be insufficient, measurement weak, or the study underpowered. Likewise, a statistically detectable association can be economically small or irrelevant to the decision. Distinguish correctness, precision, magnitude and practical consequence. Compare explanations through different predicted observations or designs that separate them. If both a supply shock and a demand shock could explain a price increase, examine the quantity change, input costs, institutional details and credible sources of variation. Several mechanisms can operate together; a forced single-cause story can be wrong. Make the argument auditable A strong argument names the question, defines the target quantity, states the model or design, presents the evidence and explains uncertainty. It separates observed facts from inference and gives the alternatives that remain plausible. A reader should be able to follow why each step supports the next. Sources should support the associated claim, not merely discuss the same topic. Dates and units matter. An opinion article, a dataset release, a theoretical paper and a causal study have different roles. Use each for what it actually establishes. Economic reasoning remains useful even before advanced methods are learned. The essential discipline is to avoid moving from identity to behavior, from correlation to causation, from prediction to intervention, or from one valued outcome to an entire policy conclusion without explaining the additional assumptions. Exercises Classify these statements as definition, identity, behavioral hypothesis, empirical claim or recommendation: “Equity equals assets minus liabilities”; “Consumers buy less when price rises, other relevant influences fixed”; “The observed program group earned 12% more”; “The government should adopt the program”; “A stock is measured at a point in time.” A firm reports sales rising from $500 to $650 after a pricing change. An untreated comparison rises from $400 to $500. Calculate both changes and their difference. State the assumption required for the difference to represent the pricing change's causal effect. In a randomized illustrative study, 100 of 500 treated participants and 150 of 500 controls experience an adverse outcome. Calculate both rates, their percentage-point difference and the relative reduction. Explain why uncertainty still matters. A survey finds that people with more schooling have higher income. Identify three possible explanations for the association and explain what a causal effect of additional schooling would compare. Explain the difference between random sampling and random assignment. Give a setting with assignment but a limited sampled population. A prediction model for defaults uses a field recorded only after a borrower defaults. Explain the evaluation error and how an honest forecasting dataset should be constructed. A researcher finds no statistically significant effect and concludes that the intervention has no effect. Identify missing information and give a more careful interpretation. Write a causal claim about an economic choice. State its counterfactual, one confounder, a possible design, an assumption that design needs, and one limit to generalizing the result. Solutions and evaluation criteria Respectively: identity; behavioral hypothesis; empirical descriptive claim; normative recommendation; definition. The reported group difference alone is not necessarily causal. The treated change is $150 and comparison change $100, leaving $50. Causal interpretation requires the comparison's change to represent the treated firm's counterfactual trend without its pricing change, with relevant spillovers or differential shocks addressed. The rates are 20% and 30%, a −10 percentage-point treated-minus-control difference and approximately 33.33% relative reduction. These are realized sample rates; inference is needed for uncertainty about the target effect, and external validity is separate. Schooling may increase skills; family resources may affect both schooling and income; ability, location or selection may contribute. A causal effect compares the same relevant person's or population's income under differing schooling interventions, with other design conditions specified. Sampling determines study inclusion; assignment determines treatment among included units. Randomized assignment within a volunteer group can support an internal comparison without establishing that the group represents all possible recipients. The model uses post-outcome information unavailable at forecast time, causing leakage. Freeze features according to their actual availability at each decision date, preserve a genuinely held-out evaluation, and document timing. Needed information includes the effect estimate, interval, study size, power, design, measurement and relevant effect magnitude. A nonsignificant result can be consistent with small, zero or sizable effects depending on the uncertainty. State what the evidence can and cannot exclude. A strong answer defines an intervention and outcome, identifies the counterfactual, explains the confounding mechanism, gives a feasible design and its identifying assumptions, and recognizes a population or institutional limit. Several well-supported designs are possible. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain how an identity can be true without identifying the response to an intervention. Then explain why a highly predictive variable might be a poor policy lever. Delayed check: find an economic causal claim in a public discussion. Reconstruct its counterfactual, evidence, assumptions and feasible competing explanation without appealing to the speaker's status. Where this leads Economic theory clarifies mechanisms and consistency. Econometrics formalizes identification and inference. Research practice evaluates contribution and reproducibility. Teaching and leadership require communicating those distinctions clearly when decisions must be made despite incomplete evidence."}, {"title": "01.11 · Reading, writing and explaining analysis", "url": "v01-c11.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Make reasoning visible to another person Analysis has limited value if another person cannot determine what was asked, what was measured, how the conclusion follows, and what could change it. Good communication is part of technical competence. It forces the analyst to identify hidden assumptions and separate a defensible inference from an attractive story. Begin with the question. “Describe unemployment” is broad; “Did the unemployment rate fall because employment rose or because participation changed?” directs attention to particular counts and mechanisms. “Should we expand?” requires a comparison among feasible actions, not just a forecast of revenue. Write the conclusion early, with the condition that matters most. Then present the evidence and reasoning needed to assess it. A reader should not have to search through computations to discover whether the project was recommended, which alternative was considered, or which assumption makes the difference. A reusable analytical memo A concise memo can follow five elements: the question and decision; the recommendation or finding; the mechanism and calculation; uncertainty and alternatives; and the action or evidence needed next. The arrangement can change with the audience, but the chain of reasoning must remain clear. For an executive, a central risk and its consequence can matter more than the derivation's intermediate lines. For a technical reviewer, the derivation and identification assumptions must be accessible. Keep supporting detail in a table or appendix rather than replacing it with unexplained authority. State who benefits, who bears costs and which objective guides the recommendation. “This is optimal” is incomplete without specifying the objective and constraints. An economically positive project may conflict with another institutional priority, face financing constraints, or distribute costs unequally. Distinguish facts, assumptions and inference A measured fact might be the observed sale count in a supplied dataset. An assumption might be that its reporting coverage is consistent through time. An inference might be that demand changed. A recommendation might be to increase capacity. Label the transitions rather than presenting all four as equally established facts. Use language that matches the evidence. “The supplied records show,” “under the stated assumption,” “this is consistent with,” and “the design identifies” have different meanings. Avoid changing “associated with” to “caused by” during a summary. Concision should preserve the epistemic status of the claim. Quantify an assumption's relevance when possible. “Results are sensitive” is vague. “The recommendation switches when the success probability falls below 60%” identifies a decision threshold. “The effect's plausible range includes outcomes that would reverse the decision” explains why uncertainty matters. Worked example 1 — Rewrite a revenue-only recommendation An analyst writes: “Buy the equipment. It will bring in $600 next year and $600 the year after. The total is $1,200, so we make $200.” The equipment costs $1,000 now, and the specified opportunity rate is 10% annually. A more complete version is: Under the supplied certain net-cash-flow assumptions, purchase is better than the specified 10% opportunity by $41.32 in present value: −1,000 + 600/1.1 + 600/1.1². The recommendation requires the forecast to include all relevant operating costs and the purchase to be financeable. At a 20% discount rate, NPV is −$83.33. Verify the cash-flow forecast and rate before committing. The revised memo names the objective, timing, alternative and assumptions. It does not convert unverified forecasts into facts. It also distinguishes net receipts from revenue, a difference that could materially change the calculation. Tables and graphs are arguments A table should make comparisons easy to inspect. Put like quantities in the same column, give units in headers, identify the time period, and include denominators when rates matter. Totals and subtotals should reconcile. A note should explain missing values, rounding and relevant measurement choices. A graph must label axes and scales and identify the data or model it represents. A bar chart with a truncated axis can exaggerate differences; a time-series plot can use a narrower range appropriately if its purpose and scale are explicit. The issue is whether the presentation makes the comparison understandable rather than manipulating its impression. A plotted line connecting observations can suggest continuous measurement or a trend model. A diagram of a theoretical relationship is different from a graph of observed data. Label it as a model or illustrative example when that is what it is. Do not add fabricated empirical observations for visual polish. Worked example 2 — Show counts as well as rates A report says, “Unemployment improved from 10% to 9%.” The underlying illustrative labor-force counts are: Period Labor force Unemployed Unemployment rate 0 1,000 people 100 people 10% 1 1,200 people 108 people 9% A careful summary is: “The unemployment rate fell one percentage point, while the unemployed count rose 8%. The labor force increased 20%, changing the denominator. These figures alone do not establish the cause or the change in every person's labor-market experience.” The table preserves the facts needed to assess the claim. Its purpose is not to declare whether every aspect improved or worsened; it separates the measured quantities before evaluating them. Source provenance and reproducibility A citation should let a reader locate the material supporting a claim. Record the source, dataset or series identifier, date or version, measurement units, and transformations. For a hypothetical example, say that its numbers are constructed. A reader should never have to guess whether a figure is observed or invented for teaching. Reproducibility means another person can follow the documented inputs and procedure to obtain the stated output, within explained numerical differences. For an early manual calculation, supply the original values and steps. For later computational work, provide the code, data access instructions, environment and relevant settings. Reproducing a result is important but does not prove that its interpretation is correct. A biased analysis can reproduce perfectly. Keep computational reliability, measurement validity and causal interpretation as distinct review questions. Explain a mechanism before its vocabulary Technical vocabulary is efficient once a concept is understood. Introduce the idea first: an extra book requires giving up some meals under a budget. Then name the opportunity cost and slope. If the learner can recite the term but cannot reason through a new example, the instruction has not achieved transfer. Use multiple representations carefully. An intuitive story, a balance-sheet table, an equation and a graph can describe the same mechanism. Check that they agree. An analogy helps when its relevant mapping is explicit; it misleads when an irrelevant feature of the analogy becomes part of the argument. Ask learners to reconstruct a result rather than merely recognize it. “Explain why principal repayment leaves initial-value net worth unchanged” is more diagnostic than “Is principal repayment an expense?” Follow with a case containing interest or fees to see whether the distinction survives a changed problem. Read actively and fairly When reading an argument, reconstruct its strongest defensible version before criticizing it. Identify its question, definitions, assumptions, evidence and conclusion. Determine whether the objection concerns mathematical correctness, measurement, identification, generalization or the objective being optimized. A disagreement about values should not be disguised as a false arithmetic claim. A disagreement about causal identification should not be dismissed as a difference in taste. Precise criticism helps the author revise the part that actually needs work. Keep a record of questions rather than copying every sentence into notes. For each important result, write its mechanism, conditions and one counterexample or boundary case. Return later and derive it without looking. This practice creates a testable record of understanding. Editing for clarity Remove repetition, unsupported adjectives and claims of importance that do not explain a consequence. Replace “it is obvious” with the reasoning. Replace an unexplained acronym with a definition where it first appears. Use units and concrete verbs. A sentence should contribute a fact, a logical step, a condition or a decision-relevant implication. Keep essential qualifications near the claim they limit. Hiding all assumptions at the end of a long document can allow the main text to overstate its conclusion. The aim is readable rigor: enough detail to examine the reasoning, organized so the reader can find it. Exercises Rewrite: “Profit grew 200%, proving the strategy worked.” Specify the missing base, period, population, costs and causal evidence. Give a version that reports only what the supplied statement could establish. A project costs $500 now and pays a certain $600 in one year. At a 10% opportunity rate, calculate NPV and write a 75-word memo including timing, assumptions and the financing question. Build a table for two divisions: A has $200 revenue and $50 profit; B has $800 revenue and $40 profit. Include revenues, profits and margins and calculate the consolidated margin. Write one sentence explaining why an equal-weight average answers a different question. Rewrite: “Inflation fell from 6% to 2%, so prices became cheaper.” State the correct inference and give an illustrative two-period index path starting at 100. Read an economic claim and classify each supporting item as measured evidence, assumption, calculation, inference or recommendation. Identify a transition that would need additional justification. Design a provenance note for a hypothetical chart of annual real wages. Specify the information a reader needs, including how “real” is defined. Explain what changes if the numbers are constructed for teaching. Prepare a two-minute explanation of why a loan can increase cash without increasing equity at origination. Use one table and one everyday explanation, then ask a question that tests transfer. Give a fair critique of this claim: “Our randomized study among 100 volunteers identifies the effect of the program everywhere.” Separate the possible internal validity from external validity and list further evidence needed. Solutions and evaluation criteria A 200% increase requires a defined positive base and comparable period. Even if measured correctly, it is a descriptive result, not proof of causation. A careful rewrite states the specific observed profit change, its accounting basis and period, then says the strategy's causal contribution remains to be established. NPV is −500 + 600/1.1 = $45.4545… approximately $45.45. A strong memo gives a conditional recommendation, identifies the assumed complete certain net payment, names the 10% alternative and checks whether financing is feasible. Its word count should not force omission of the decisive assumptions. A's margin is 25%; B's is 5%. Total profit is $90 on $1,000 revenue, so the consolidated margin is 9%. Equal weighting gives 15% for a typical two-division average, while consolidated performance requires revenue weights. The positive inflation rate slowed. An index rises from 100 to 106, then to 108.12. Prices in that index continue rising in the second period; they do not fall. Assess whether categories are separated accurately and whether the chosen transition needs an additional measurement, identifying assumption or normative criterion. The content of the selected claim determines the answer. Record the wage source/series, population, hours basis, period, currency, nominal measure, price index, base year, vintage and transformation. If constructed, label the chart illustrative and supply the invented values without presenting them as observations. The table should add equal cash and debt at initial equal values, leaving equity unchanged. A good transfer question adds a fee, interest or asset purchase and asks which entries differ. Evaluate the learner's reconstruction, not their recognition of a slogan. Random assignment can support a causal comparison among participants under implementation, measurement and interference assumptions. Generalization to other populations and institutions needs evidence about representativeness, mechanisms, treatment differences and context. The sample size also affects precision; it does not alone invalidate randomization. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: present a finding in 30 seconds, then defend it for five minutes. The short version must preserve the principal assumption and uncertainty; the long version must expose the reasoning. Delayed check: revisit an old memo and reconstruct its calculations from the documented inputs alone. Identify one claim whose wording is stronger than its evidence, if any, and revise it. Where this leads Research papers require an explicit contribution and auditable methods. Teaching requires diagnosing understanding. Institutional leadership requires decisions and accountability. Clear communication is the bridge between analysis and those responsibilities."}, {"title": "01.12 · Foundation integration and diagnostic", "url": "v01-c12.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Integrate the foundations before moving ahead The earlier chapters taught separate habits: compare feasible alternatives, preserve units and denominators, express relationships, reconcile stocks and flows, discount dated cash flows, trace incentives, deflate money amounts, reason under uncertainty, identify financial claims, evaluate causal arguments and communicate clearly. This chapter puts those habits into one case. Integration is a test of whether you can decide which tool applies. A formula named in a question is easier to recognize than a method hidden inside a realistic narrative. Begin by organizing the case into entities, dates, units, decisions and assumptions. Then perform calculations in a sequence that allows independent checks. Every number below is constructed for instruction. The case is deliberately simplified: it excludes taxes, depreciation and many contract details so the relevant relationships can be seen. Those exclusions must remain visible. Later volumes add the omitted economics and accounting rather than pretending the simplified case is a complete business model. The business case An owner contributes $1,800 to a newly formed business. The business borrows $1,200, purchases equipment for $1,000 cash and inventory for $800 cash, and sells the entire inventory for $1,200 cash. It pays $60 interest during the illustrative period. No principal is repaid. There are no other transactions, expenses or valuation changes. The questions are: What happened to cash and equity? Did the business earn its borrowing as revenue? Can its future investment proposal be judged from revenue alone? Which conclusions follow by accounting, and which need behavioral or empirical evidence? Worked example 1 — Statements that reconcile Cash changes are +1,800 +1,200 −1,000 −800 +1,200 −60 = +$2,340 from a zero opening balance. Revenue is $1,200. Inventory expense is $800, and interest expense is $60. Profit is $340. Closing assets are cash $2,340 plus equipment $1,000 = $3,340. Closing liabilities are $1,200. Equity is the $1,800 contribution plus $340 retained profit = $2,140. The identity holds: 3,340 = 1,200 + 2,140. The cash inflow from borrowing is financing, not sales revenue. The equipment purchase converts cash into another asset. This simplified case explicitly omits depreciation; introducing it would affect equipment's carrying amount and profit consistently, while not creating an additional cash payment at recognition. For cash-flow presentation, classification can depend on the applicable rules. An internal analysis can separately show operating receipts and inventory payments of $400 net, an interest payment of $60, equipment investment of −$1,000, and initial financing of $3,000. Their sum remains $2,340 regardless of how the stated interest payment is grouped. A future investment proposal For the next one-year period, the business can spend $800 immediately on a separate project. Its end-of-year net receipt is $1,200 in a favorable state and $700 in an unfavorable state. The assigned favorable-state probability is 0.6. Assume these receipts already include all subsequent project costs; the initial $800 is not included in them. The expected receipt is 0.6 × 1,200 + 0.4 × 700 = $1,000. At a specified 10% discount benchmark, the discounted expectation minus the initial expenditure is 1,000/1.1 − 800 ≈ $109.09. This is a transparent benchmark calculation, not a proof that 10% properly prices the project's risk. At the same benchmark, the favorable-state NPV is 1,200/1.1 − 800 ≈ $290.91. The unfavorable-state NPV is 700/1.1 − 800 ≈ −$163.64. The expected calculation does not remove the 40% unfavorable-state probability or tell us whether the owner can absorb it. Let p be the favorable-state probability. The expected receipt is 700 + 500p. The benchmark NPV is positive when (700 + 500p)/1.1 > 800, or p > 0.36. A probability estimate near that threshold is decision-relevant; an unexamined point estimate is insufficient evidence. The household financing case A different prospective owner has only $500 of cash available for the same $800 project. A loan of $300 is offered with a $330 end-of-year repayment. Keeping and investing the $500 instead produces $550 in one year under a specified certain 10% opportunity. The project receipts are as above, and the new loan repayment must be deducted separately. Worked example 2 — Expected advantage with a cash constraint At initiation, $500 existing cash plus $300 loan proceeds funds the $800 cost. Remaining cash is zero. In the favorable state, final cash after repayment is 1,200 − 330 = $870. In the unfavorable state, final cash is 700 − 330 = $370. Expected final cash is 0.6 × 870 + 0.4 × 370 = $670. The alternative produces $550, so the project exceeds it by $120 in expected year-end cash. Relative to that alternative, the project gives +$320 in the favorable state and −$180 in the unfavorable state. The expected advantage is not a recommendation for every household. Starting with zero remaining cash can violate the person's needs for interim spending or buffers. If an additional $100 payment can arise before project receipts and no funding is available, the proposal may be infeasible even though its expectation is favorable. Do not subtract the original $500 again from only one alternative. Both use the same starting cash. Comparing year-end wealth under consistent actions already accounts for it. If expressing each action's NPV instead, apply the same valuation date and complete cash flows to both. Add purchasing power and evidence If the applicable price index rises 5% over the year, final cash amounts expressed in initial purchasing power are divided by 1.05. The project expectation becomes 670/1.05 ≈ $638.10 and the alternative 550/1.05 ≈ $523.81. This common deflation preserves their comparison but clarifies the units. The favorable-state probability needs support. Was it estimated from comparable projects? Were failed projects omitted? Did the economic environment change? Is the receipt forecast genuinely net of all future costs? These questions concern measurement and evidence, not the arithmetic of a supplied probability. Suppose the business's sales rose after buying the equipment. The rise is not by itself a causal estimate of the equipment's effect. Demand, staffing, prices and seasonality can also change. A comparison design needs an explicit counterfactual. Accounting can document sales; it cannot independently establish the causal contribution of the equipment. Use an integration checklist Entity: distinguish business, owner, borrower, bank and economy. Time: identify the valuation date and every payment date. Units: separate money, rates, percentages, percentage points and real price bases. Accounting: reconcile all stocks and flows. Decision: name the best feasible alternative and incremental consequences. Uncertainty: specify outcomes, probabilities and constraints. Mechanism: identify incentives and consistency conditions. Evidence: separate constructed inputs, measured facts and causal inference. Communication: state a conclusion with the assumptions that could change it. A mistake found during integration often points to a specific prerequisite. Incorrect rate bases lead back to Chapter 2. Unreconciled financing leads to Chapter 4 or 9. Discounting inconsistency leads to Chapter 5. Unsupported probabilities lead to Chapter 8. Causal overstatement leads to Chapter 10. Use this map for targeted remediation. Exercises Reconstruct the business's balance sheet and profit from the case without looking at Worked Example 1. Then suppose it pays another $100 of principal from cash. Show the new cash, assets, liabilities and equity. For the business case, add a $100 depreciation expense and a corresponding $100 reduction in equipment carrying value. Keep the actual cash transactions unchanged. Recompute profit, cash, assets and equity and reconcile the identity. Calculate the project's expected receipt and 10%-benchmark NPV when the favorable-state probability is 0.3 and when it is 0.5. Verify the probability threshold for positive benchmark NPV. For the household financing case, change the year-end loan repayment from $330 to $360. Recompute both final-cash outcomes, expected final cash and the expected advantage over the $550 alternative. In the original household financing case, vary the favorable-state probability p. Derive expected final cash and the threshold at which it exceeds the $550 alternative. With a 5% price-index increase, express the original project's favorable and unfavorable household final cash and the alternative in initial purchasing power. Explain why a common deflator does not settle a preference over risk. A promoter reports a 60% favorable-state rate based only on businesses still operating after five years. Write a critique identifying selection, comparability, timing and the data needed to assess the estimate. Write a 200-word investment memo using the household financing case. Include a conditional decision, figures that reconcile, the alternative, probability sensitivity, risk and interim liquidity, a price basis, and a fact that could change the decision. Present no guaranteed outcome. Solutions and evaluation criteria Before repayment, cash is 2,340, equipment 1,000, assets 3,340, debt 1,200 and equity 2,140; profit is 340. Principal repayment reduces cash to 2,240 and debt to 1,100. Assets become 3,240 and equity remains 2,140: 3,240 = 1,100 + 2,140. Profit becomes 240; cash remains 2,340 because this entry is not another cash payment. Equipment becomes 900, assets 3,240, liabilities 1,200 and equity 2,040: 3,240 = 1,200 + 2,040. This assumes the specified depreciation treatment and no tax effect. Expected receipt is 700 + 500p. At 0.3 it is 850, giving NPV 850/1.1 − 800 ≈ −$27.27. At 0.5 it is 950, giving approximately $63.64. Solving 700 + 500p = 880 gives p = 0.36. A risk-appropriate valuation still requires more than this benchmark. Final cash is 840 or 340. Expectation is 0.6 × 840 + 0.4 × 340 = 640. Expected advantage is 640 − 550 = $90, reduced by the additional $30 repayment. Expected final cash is 870p + 370(1 − p) = 370 + 500p. It exceeds 550 when p > 0.36. At the threshold it matches the alternative in expectation, while their outcome distributions remain different. Real amounts are 870/1.05 ≈ 828.57, 370/1.05 ≈ 352.38 and 550/1.05 ≈ 523.81. The common price basis does not remove uncertain states or establish the owner's utility, constraints or risk preference. Surviving businesses can be a selected subset, and the data may omit failures or differ from the proposed project. Seek a defined original cohort, all outcomes, start dates, industry and conditions, costs, reasons for exit and the information available at the forecast date. Do not assume the survivor rate equals the proposal's success probability. Evaluate consistency and conditional reasoning. The memo should distinguish expectation from a guarantee, give the relevant comparison, expose the liquidity constraint and data weakness, use a stated price basis and avoid double counting. A justified decision to proceed, reduce scale, seek more information or decline can be defensible under explicit additional assumptions. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain the complete case to a beginner, then defend its accounting and uncertainty treatment to a technical reader. Both explanations must describe the same mechanism and assumptions. Delayed check: change the case's prices, loan terms and probabilities and reconstruct the analysis from the transaction list and timelines. Diagnose the first point of failure if your statements or comparisons do not reconcile. Readiness for the next volume You are ready for the mathematical toolkit when you can translate relationships into equations, preserve units, solve basic systems, compare dated amounts, distinguish probabilities from outcomes, and explain assumptions. Use the volume diagnostic and cumulative assessments to identify gaps. Advancing with an unresolved error in these foundations makes later sophisticated calculations less reliable. The next volume develops proof, calculus, linear algebra and optimization. Those tools will strengthen the same reasoning rather than replace it. The aim is to derive and evaluate unfamiliar results with confidence grounded in explicit steps."}, {"title": "02.01 · Mathematical language and proof", "url": "v02-c01.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Why an economist needs proof A calculation answers a particular question with particular inputs. A proof establishes what follows throughout a stated domain. If you claim that every affordable plan remains affordable after income rises, three successful numerical trials are insufficient. The claim concerns all plans and all eligible income changes. Conversely, one valid counterexample defeats a universal claim that every positive-profit business has enough cash to pay its bills. Mathematical discipline begins before advanced formulas. Define the objects, specify the domain, state what is assumed, and distinguish the conclusion from what you would like it to mean economically. A logically valid theorem about a model does not establish that the model describes observed behavior. Theoretical validity and empirical relevance are separate questions, both necessary for good research. This chapter supplies the language used throughout the library. You should be able to read an unfamiliar proposition, identify its exact logical burden, construct an argument, and explain which step fails when an assumption changes. The examples are original simplified economic models; none is an empirical claim. Objects, sets and feasible alternatives A set is a collection of distinct objects called elements. Write x ∈ A when x belongs to A, and x ∉ A otherwise. The empty set ∅ has no elements. Here ℕ = {1, 2, 3, …}, ℕ₀ = {0, 1, 2, …}, ℤ denotes the integers, ℚ the rational numbers, and ℝ the real numbers. A rational number is a ratio of integers with a nonzero denominator. Real numbers also include irrational values such as √2. A ⊆ B means every element of A also belongs to B. Equality A = B requires both inclusions. A proper subset is written A ⊊ B. Distinguish an element from a subset: 2 ∈ {1, 2}, while {2} ⊆ {1, 2}. Repetition and order do not change a set, although they matter in a sequence or an ordered pair. A ∪ B contains elements in at least one of the two sets; A ∩ B contains elements in both. The difference A \\ B contains elements in A but outside B. A complement Aᶜ means U \\ A relative to a specified universe U. Without U, “everyone outside the eligible group” is ambiguous: everyone in the applicant pool, population, or world? Interval notation records boundaries. [a, b] includes both endpoints, (a, b) excludes both, and [a, b) includes only a. Thus [0, ∞) is the set of nonnegative real numbers; infinity is not an included real endpoint. Set-builder notation {x ∈ ℝ : x ≥ 0} describes that same set through a condition. The Cartesian product A × B contains ordered pairs (a, b) with a ∈ A and b ∈ B. Consequently ℝ² represents two real coordinates, and ℝⁿ represents n coordinates. Coordinates have an interpretation and order: a bundle (food, housing) cannot be swapped silently. A feasible set collects all choices satisfying the constraints, not merely the choice somebody eventually makes. For nonnegative quantities x and y, positive prices p and q, and income m ≥ 0, define B(m) = {(x, y) ∈ [0, ∞)² : px + qy ≤ m}. This one expression specifies dimensions, signs, and the budget restriction. It includes unspent income. Replacing ≤ by = would define a different set, excluding bundles that leave money unused. Statements, implication and quantifiers A proposition is a statement with a truth value. “x is affordable” becomes a proposition after x, prices and income are specified; before that it is a condition depending on inputs. P ∧ Q means both statements hold, P ∨ Q means at least one holds, and ¬P means P is false. The “or” used here is inclusive. P ⇒ Q means there is no case in which P is true and Q is false. P is sufficient for Q; Q is necessary for P. The converse Q ⇒ P is a separate assertion. The contrapositive ¬Q ⇒ ¬P is equivalent to the original implication, whereas the inverse ¬P ⇒ ¬Q generally is not. To establish P ⇔ Q, or “if and only if,” prove both directions. For example, with a real number r, r > 2 implies r > 0. Exceeding 2 is sufficient for positivity; positivity is necessary for exceeding 2. Positivity alone is insufficient: r = 1 is a counterexample to the converse. A necessary condition can narrow the candidates without selecting the answer. Later, a zero derivative will often be a necessary condition for an interior optimum, and it will need additional conditions to be sufficient. The symbol ∀ means “for every”; ∃ means “there exists”; ∃! means “there exists exactly one.” State their domains. “∀x ∈ B, P(x)” requires P for every feasible x. Its negation is “∃x ∈ B such that ¬P(x).” Negating “∃x ∈ B, P(x)” gives “∀x ∈ B, ¬P(x).” Negation exchanges the quantifier as well as the condition. Order changes meaning. “For every positive tolerance ε, there exists a sufficiently large sample size n” permits n to depend on ε. “There exists n that works for every positive ε” demands one n for arbitrarily severe tolerances. The first may be true when the second is false. Limits in the next chapter depend on precisely this distinction. Universal statements over ∅ are true in the logical sense because there is no violating element. An existence claim over ∅ is false. Thus “every optimal plan is affordable” provides no evidence that an optimal plan exists. A proof of existence must supply an object or use a theorem whose hypotheses ensure one. Relations, functions and identification A binary relation on A specifies which ordered pairs in A × A satisfy a condition. It is reflexive if x relates to itself for every x, symmetric if xRy implies yRx, and transitive if xRy and yRz imply xRz. An equivalence relation has all three properties. For example, “has the same account balance as” is an equivalence relation on a fixed collection of accounts at a fixed date. The accounts need not be identical. A weak ordering can instead be reflexive, transitive and complete: every pair is comparable in at least one direction. It need not be symmetric. A partial order is reflexive, transitive and antisymmetric, meaning xRy and yRx require x = y. Coordinatewise ≤ on bundles is a partial order: (1, 3) and (2, 2) are incomparable. Do not assume that every mathematically defined comparison yields a complete ranking. A function f: A → B assigns exactly one output in B to each input in its domain A. B is the codomain; the image f(A) consists of outputs actually reached. A rule and its domain jointly define the function. f(x) = x² on ℝ and the same rule on [0, ∞) have different invertibility properties. Injectivity means distinct inputs have distinct outputs: f(x) = f(y) ⇒ x = y. Surjectivity means every element of the codomain is reached. A bijection has both properties and admits an inverse f⁻¹: B → A. x²: ℝ → [0, ∞) is surjective but not injective because 2 and −2 both give 4. Restricting the domain to [0, ∞) makes it a bijection with inverse √x. This is an early form of identification reasoning. If several parameter values produce the same observed prediction, the prediction alone cannot determine which parameter generated it. More data may help only if the enlarged mapping distinguishes the relevant alternatives. A numerical solver returning one answer is not a proof of uniqueness. Worked example 1 — An income increase expands a feasible set Claim: for fixed positive prices and 0 ≤ m₁ ≤ m₂, B(m₁) ⊆ B(m₂). Begin with an arbitrary bundle (x, y) ∈ B(m₁). By definition x, y ≥ 0 and px + qy ≤ m₁. Since m₁ ≤ m₂, transitivity of ≤ gives px + qy ≤ m₂. The signs of x and y have not changed. Hence (x, y) ∈ B(m₂), proving the inclusion for every original bundle. If m₂ > m₁ and p > 0, the inclusion is strict: (m₂/p, 0) is in B(m₂), spends exactly m₂ and is outside B(m₁). The construction supplies a witness to strictness rather than merely drawing a larger triangle. The conclusion concerns opportunity, not behavior. It does not prove the household buys more of each good. It also requires fixed prices. If income rises from 10 to 12 while p rises from 1 to 2, the old bundle (10, 0) becomes unaffordable. That counterexample violates the fixed-price assumption rather than refuting the proved theorem. Choosing and writing a proof A direct proof starts from the hypotheses and established definitions and reaches the conclusion. To prove a set inclusion, take an arbitrary element of the first set. To prove equality of sets, prove both inclusions. To prove existence, construct a witness when possible. To prove uniqueness, suppose two objects meet the conditions and show they must coincide. A contrapositive proof is useful when failure of the conclusion reveals failure of a hypothesis. If a product ab is nonzero, both a and b are nonzero: prove the contrapositive by observing that a zero factor makes the product zero. A contradiction proof assumes the hypotheses and the negation of the desired conclusion, then derives an impossibility. The contradiction must come from those assumptions, not from an unsupported claim. For a classical example, suppose √2 = a/b for relatively prime positive integers a and b. Squaring gives a² = 2b², so a² is even. An odd integer has form 2k + 1 and an odd square, so a must be even. Write a = 2c. Substitution gives b² = 2c², so b is also even. Both being even contradicts their being relatively prime. Therefore √2 is irrational. The proof covers every rational representation, unlike decimal inspection. A proof by cases partitions the domain into exhaustive possibilities. If you divide an equation by x, separate x = 0 from x ≠ 0 first. Squaring can introduce extraneous candidates; applying a logarithm requires a positive argument; multiplying an inequality by a negative value reverses it. Every transformation should preserve the asserted implication or explicitly narrow the remaining candidates. A counterexample must satisfy the hypotheses and violate the conclusion. If a theorem assumes positive prices, setting a price to zero may illustrate why that assumption matters but cannot disprove the theorem as stated. Counterexamples to overbroad claims are often more informative than additional confirming cases. Induction and repeated financial relationships Induction proves statements indexed by integers. First prove the base case P(0), or whichever first index applies. Then for arbitrary k in the domain assume P(k) and prove P(k + 1). The assumption is local to that step; it is not permission to assume the desired result for all k. Together the base and step reach every subsequent integer. Why does this work? If any index failed, the nonempty set of failing nonnegative integers would have a least element. It could not be the proved base. Its predecessor would therefore satisfy the statement, and the induction step would imply that the supposedly failing index satisfies it too—a contradiction. This uses the well-ordering property of nonnegative integers. An argument of the same form does not automatically establish a statement for all real times. Worked example 2 — A recurring payment formula with its exceptional case Let V₀ be a balance, let g be a fixed gross growth factor, and let d be the same addition at the end of each period. Define Vₙ₊₁ = gVₙ + d for n ∈ ℕ₀. For g ≠ 1 the claim is Vₙ = gⁿV₀ + d(gⁿ − 1)/(g − 1). At n = 0 the expression is V₀ + 0, so the base case holds. Suppose it holds at k. Then Vₖ₊₁ = g[gᵏV₀ + d(gᵏ − 1)/(g − 1)] + d = gᵏ⁺¹V₀ + d[g(gᵏ − 1) + (g − 1)]/(g − 1) = gᵏ⁺¹V₀ + d(gᵏ⁺¹ − 1)/(g − 1). That is the claimed formula at k + 1. Induction completes the proof. At g = 1, division by g − 1 is invalid. The recurrence instead directly gives Vₙ = V₀ + nd, also proved by the base and one-step addition. With V₀ = 100, g = 1.1 and d = 20, direct balances are 130, 163 and 199.3. The closed form at n = 3 gives 133.1 + 66.2 = 199.3. The formula remains an algebraic identity for real g ≠ 1, but a negative gross factor generally lacks the intended ordinary account interpretation. Beginning-of-period deposits would instead obey Vₙ₊₁ = g(Vₙ + d). Correct algebra cannot compensate for a mistaken timing equation. A practical proof audit Before accepting an argument, read its conclusion without the prose surrounding it. Ask what is quantified, what is fixed, and what may depend on what. Then annotate each substantive step with a definition, a hypothesis, or an already established result. A step that depends on the conclusion is circular. A string of equalities that assumes nonzero denominators without checking them is incomplete. Existence, uniqueness and construction are different achievements. Showing at most one solution does not show there is a solution. Finding one solution does not show there are no others. Deriving a candidate from an equation requires checking that candidate in the original equation and domain. Good explanations make these burdens visible and use the least machinery that settles them. Exercises Let U = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}, A = {1, 2, 4} and B = {2, 3, 4}. Find A ∪ B, A ∩ B, A \\ B and (A ∪ B)ᶜ. Prove, for arbitrary subsets of a fixed universe, (A ∪ B)ᶜ = Aᶜ ∩ Bᶜ. Compare the statements ∀ε > 0, ∃n ∈ ℕ such that 1/n < ε and ∃n ∈ ℕ, ∀ε > 0 such that 1/n < ε. Explain which is true, using the fact that integers can exceed any given real number. Write the exact negation of the first statement. On ℝ define xRy when |x − y| ≤ 1. Test reflexivity, symmetry and transitivity. Supply a counterexample for each property that fails. For f: ℝ → [0, ∞) defined by f(x) = (x − 3)², test injectivity and surjectivity. Restrict the domain to [3, ∞), obtain a bijection, and derive its inverse including the inverse's domain. Prove that under fixed positive prices, B(m₁) = B(m₂) if and only if m₁ = m₂, for nonnegative incomes. Identify which implication needs a constructed bundle. A loan balance obeys Lₙ₊₁ = 1.05Lₙ − 30 with L₀ = 200. Derive and prove by induction a closed form while this recurrence is applied without truncation. Compute L₁ and L₂. Explain why an actual payoff rule would eventually require a changed recurrence. For a ≥ −1 and integer n ≥ 1, prove (1 + a)ⁿ ≥ 1 + na by induction. Identify why the sign restriction is needed in the induction step. Find an a outside the restriction and an n for which the inequality fails. A researcher claims that a model has a unique parameter because f(θ) = θ² = 9 has the solution θ = 3. Audit the claim on ℝ and on [0, ∞). Give separate proofs of existence and uniqueness on the restricted domain. Explain the logical gap in treating one solver output as uniqueness. Solutions and proof guidance The sets are {1, 2, 3, 4}, {2, 4}, {1}, and {5, 6}. For any x ∈ U, x ∈ (A ∪ B)ᶜ exactly when x is in neither A nor B, exactly when x ∈ Aᶜ and x ∈ Bᶜ. Thus membership in the two sides is equivalent, proving both inclusions. The shared universe is essential to the complement notation. The first is true: for each chosen ε > 0 choose an integer n > 1/ε. The second is false: after any proposed n choose ε = 1/(2n), so 1/n is not less than ε. The first statement's negation is ∃ε > 0, ∀n ∈ ℕ, 1/n ≥ ε. The example illustrates dependence on tolerance, not a single universal n. Reflexive because |x − x| = 0 ≤ 1; symmetric because |x − y| = |y − x|. Not transitive: 0R1 and 1R2 but not 0R2. Approximate equality under a fixed tolerance therefore need not form equivalence classes. Not injective: f(2) = f(4) = 1. Surjective: for every z ≥ 0, x = 3 + √z is a real input with f(x) = z. On [3, ∞), x − 3 is nonnegative; equality of squares forces equality of those differences and hence of inputs. The inverse is f⁻¹(z) = 3 + √z for z ∈ [0, ∞), with values in [3, ∞). If incomes equal, the definitions coincide. For the reverse implication, suppose they differ and, after relabeling, m₂ > m₁. The bundle (m₂/p, 0) is feasible only at m₂, so the sets differ. This contradicts equality. The constructed bundle establishes the reverse direction; fixed prices prevent changing the definition between comparisons. The form is Lₙ = 200(1.05)ⁿ − 30[(1.05)ⁿ − 1]/0.05 = 600 − 400(1.05)ⁿ. Base n = 0 gives 200. If true at k, then 1.05[600 − 400(1.05)ᵏ] − 30 = 600 − 400(1.05)ᵏ⁺¹, proving the step. L₁ = 180 and L₂ = 159. Eventually the expression becomes negative; an actual debt paid off with a smaller final payment would cease following this unrestricted recurrence. No negative debt balance is being asserted. At n = 1 equality holds. Given the bound at k, multiply by 1 + a ≥ 0 to obtain (1 + a)ᵏ⁺¹ ≥ (1 + ka)(1 + a) = 1 + (k + 1)a + ka² ≥ 1 + (k + 1)a. A negative factor would reverse the first inequality. For a = −4 and n = 3, the left side is −27 and the right side is −11, so the claimed inequality fails outside the restriction. On ℝ, both 3 and −3 solve the equation, so uniqueness is false. On [0, ∞), 3 is a permitted solution, proving existence. If θ ≥ 0 and θ² = 9, (θ − 3)(θ + 3) = 0 and θ + 3 > 0, so θ = 3. This proves uniqueness. A solver reports a found solution; it does not eliminate other permissible solutions or establish the parameter domain. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain necessity, sufficiency, existence and uniqueness using a single budget or parameter example. Then defend the induction proof of recurring balances without skipping its base case or timing assumption. Delayed check: after a week, negate a nested quantified statement, prove a new feasible-set inclusion, and construct a valid counterexample to its overbroad economic interpretation. Reconstruct the recurring-balance proof with g = 1 treated separately. Further study and scope For independent mathematical study, MIT's 18.100A lecture-note collection covers proof, sets and functions before developing analysis. Use Lectures 1–2 for additional practice. This chapter's economic constructions and exercises are original; the reference is a further-study route, not an empirical justification for the models. These are working foundations rather than an axiomatic construction of all mathematics. Later chapters add precise convergence, continuity and optimization conditions. Maintain a proof notebook: statement, domain, hypotheses, argument, counterexample when a hypothesis is dropped, and economic interpretation."}, {"title": "02.02 · Functions, limits and continuity", "url": "v02-c02.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "What does “arbitrarily close” mean? A finite decimal display cannot tell you whether a sequence converges. A graph that looks smooth cannot establish continuity, and two price observations with opposite excess-demand signs do not guarantee a clearing price unless the behavior between them satisfies the appropriate condition. Limits turn such visual impressions into statements that can be defended. The essential idea is control: specify an acceptable output error, then show how an input restriction or sufficiently late sequence index guarantees that error. The order of these choices matters. This chapter develops that language and uses it to prove existence, diagnose jumps, and bound a numerical search. Its functions and market quantities are synthetic instructional examples. Domains, composition and basic function shapes A function needs a domain, a rule, and a codomain. For f(x) = 1/(x − 2), the natural real domain excludes 2. For √x it is [0, ∞), and for ln x it is (0, ∞). A formula for a mathematical function can allow inputs that the model does not: a demand equation defined algebraically for negative prices may have an economic domain restricted to nonnegative prices and quantities. If f: A → B and g: B → C, their composition g ∘ f maps x to g(f(x)). More generally, the composition is defined only for x where the intermediate value f(x) is in g's domain. For ln(1 − x²), this requires 1 − x² > 0, hence −1 < x < 1. Evaluating the outer function without checking this condition gives an undefined real expression. A function is increasing if x < y implies f(x) ≤ f(y), and strictly increasing if the output inequality is strict. Decreasing has the opposite output inequality. Monotonicity is a statement over the whole stated interval; checking two endpoints is insufficient. For a ≥ 0, powers xᵃ on positive x, exponentials and logarithms provide useful shapes, while negative powers decrease. The natural logarithm is the inverse of eˣ on positive outputs; ln(eˣ) = x and e^(ln x) = x for x > 0. Ordinary real exponentials and logarithms are continuous on their domains. We use their established real-number definitions here; a later integration chapter connects ln x with the accumulated integral of 1/x. For limits, the relevant fact is domain-aware continuity, not a decimal approximation to e. Piecewise rules require special attention where their formulas change. Sequences and their limits A sequence is a function from integer indices to values; write (aₙ) with n ∈ ℕ or ℕ₀ as specified. The notation aₙ → L means: for every ε > 0, there exists an integer N such that for every n ≥ N, |aₙ − L| < ε. Absolute value measures distance. N may depend on ε, but cannot then depend on which n ≥ N you inspect. For aₙ = 1/n, choose an integer N > 1/ε. Then n ≥ N gives |aₙ − 0| = 1/n ≤ 1/N < ε. This proves convergence to zero. It does not say a term ever equals zero. For 3 + 2/n the same reasoning with N > 2/ε proves convergence to 3. A limit is unique. If a sequence converged to distinct L and M, choose ε = |L − M|/3. Beyond the larger of the two required indices, both distances would be less than ε. The triangle inequality would give |L − M| ≤ |L − aₙ| + |aₙ − M| < 2|L − M|/3, an impossibility. This proof uses one index that satisfies both bounds. Convergent sequences are bounded: after some index their terms lie within 1 of the limit, and a finite number of earlier terms has a finite maximum absolute value. Boundedness alone is insufficient. The bounded sequence (−1)ⁿ alternates between −1 and 1 and has no limit, since its even and odd terms approach different values. If aₙ → A and bₙ → B, then their sum approaches A + B and their product approaches AB. A quotient approaches A/B if B ≠ 0. For the sum, bound the error by |aₙ − A| + |bₙ − B| and give each term half the tolerance. Products additionally use boundedness of one factor. The nonzero denominator condition keeps bₙ eventually away from zero; it cannot be omitted. The squeeze principle says that if lₙ ≤ aₙ ≤ uₙ eventually, and both bounds converge to L, then aₙ also converges to L. For example, −1/n ≤ sin(n)/n ≤ 1/n proves convergence to zero without requiring sin(n) to settle down. A statement need only hold from some sufficiently late index; finitely many initial terms cannot change a limit. A nondecreasing sequence bounded above converges to its least upper bound in ℝ. Briefly, if L is that bound, every ε > 0 admits a term greater than L − ε; otherwise L − ε would itself be an upper bound. All later terms remain between that term and L. Thus their distance from L is less than ε. The real numbers' least-upper-bound property is called completeness and is examined more deeply in Chapter 10. Infinite sums are limits of partial sums, not an instruction to add endlessly without a criterion. For |g| < 1, gⁿ → 0: if 0 < |g| < 1, put c = 1/|g| − 1 > 0 and use the preceding chapter's bound (1 + c)ⁿ ≥ 1 + nc, giving |g|ⁿ ≤ 1/(1 + nc) → 0; g = 0 is immediate. Multiplying the finite sum S = 1 + g + … + gⁿ by 1 − g cancels its interior terms, yielding S = (1 − gⁿ⁺¹)/(1 − g). Its limit is therefore 1/(1 − g). For g ≥ 1 this infinite sum does not have that finite limit. A perpetuity formula needs its convergence condition as well as its payment timing. Limits of functions Write lim x→a f(x) = L when: for every ε > 0 there exists δ > 0 such that x in the domain and 0 < |x − a| < δ imply |f(x) − L| < ε. The point a must be approachable by distinct domain points. The exclusion of x = a means the limit does not require f(a) to be defined or equal to L. The input tolerance δ may depend on ε and the point a. It must work for every domain input in that punctured neighborhood. Testing one trajectory or a handful of small distances is evidence about those inputs, not proof of the universal bound. Limits from the right restrict x > a; limits from the left restrict x < a. At an interior point approachable from both sides, a two-sided finite limit exists exactly when both one-sided limits exist and coincide. At a boundary of the domain, continuity is interpreted using only domain inputs. For example, √x is continuous at 0 relative to its nonnegative domain. The same algebraic rules for sums, products and quotients apply to function limits, with a nonzero limiting denominator for division. If x² − 4 is divided by x − 2, canceling gives x + 2 only for x ≠ 2. That is enough to compute the limit at 2, but it does not fill the missing function value unless a new definition explicitly does so. Worked example 1 — Prove a removable limit with a usable tolerance For x ≠ 2, let f(x) = (x² − 4)/(x − 2). We claim its limit at 2 is 4. Factor the numerator to obtain f(x) = x + 2 on the permitted domain. Therefore |f(x) − 4| = |x − 2|. Given any ε > 0, choose δ = ε. Every permitted input with 0 < |x − 2| < δ has output error less than ε. This is a complete limit proof. Suppose instead we extend the function by defining f(2) = 9. The limit remains 4 because its definition ignores the value exactly at 2. The extension is discontinuous there. The continuous extension must assign f(2) = 4. Thus assigning a value and proving a limit are separate tasks. For an uncanceled quadratic, lim x→2 x² = 4, the bound requires another step: |x² − 4| = |x − 2||x + 2|. If |x − 2| < 1, then 1 < x < 3 and |x + 2| < 5. Choose δ = min{1, ε/5}. The product is then less than ε. The preliminary neighborhood bound makes the second factor controllable. Continuity and what it buys you A function is continuous at a point a in its domain if input closeness guarantees output closeness including the value at a. At an approachable point this says lim x→a f(x) = f(a). Polynomials are continuous on ℝ; rational functions are continuous where their denominators are nonzero. Sums and products of continuous functions are continuous, and their quotients are continuous at points with nonzero denominators. Compositions are continuous wherever the component functions are continuous at the relevant inputs. Continuity does not mean an output change is small under any specified input perturbation. A continuous function can be steep, and its required δ can be very small. Nor does continuity imply a finite slope everywhere: |x| is continuous at zero but has left slope −1 and right slope 1. A derivative requires those local slopes to agree, as the next chapter shows. The intermediate value theorem says a real-valued continuous function on [a, b] takes every value between f(a) and f(b). In particular, if f(a) < 0 < f(b), it has at least one zero inside the interval. Continuity rules out jumping over the desired value. The theorem gives existence, not uniqueness or a closed formula. One proof outline uses repeated halving: retain a closed half-interval whose endpoint values bracket the target. Its lengths tend to zero and its nested endpoints approach a common real point. Continuity at that point forces its value to be the target. Completeness of ℝ and closed endpoints ensure the limiting point remains in the interval. Chapter 10 supplies the more general convergence machinery. The extreme value theorem says a continuous function on a closed bounded interval attains a maximum and a minimum. “Attains” means there is an actual input realizing each extreme, not only an upper or lower bound. Domain conditions matter: f(x) = x on (0, 1) has supremum 1 but no maximum; 1/x on (0, 1] is unbounded above. An optimization routine should not be asked to discover an optimum whose existence has not been established. A discontinuity may be removable, a finite jump, or an unbounded singularity. For a fixed participation fee, C(q) = 0 at q = 0 and C(q) = 10 + 2q at q > 0 jumps at zero. The limit from the right is 10, not C(0). Treating that cost as continuous would erase a discrete decision: whether to participate at all. Worked example 2 — Existence, uniqueness and a price bracket Let synthetic demand be D(p) = 40/(1 + p) and supply S(p) = 4 + 2p on [0, 10], in common quantity units. Excess demand z(p) = D(p) − S(p) is continuous because 1 + p is positive there. At p = 0 it is 36 > 0; at p = 10 it is 40/11 − 24 < 0. The intermediate value theorem establishes a clearing price. For p₂ > p₁ ≥ 0, 1/(1 + p₂) < 1/(1 + p₁), while 4 + 2p₂ > 4 + 2p₁. Hence z(p₂) < z(p₁). Strict decrease establishes at most one zero. Combined with existence, the zero is unique. Solving 40 = (4 + 2p)(1 + p) gives p² + 3p − 18 = 0, or (p − 3)(p + 6) = 0. Only p = 3 lies in the domain, and D(3) = S(3) = 10. Algebra provides a location; the preceding argument explains existence and uniqueness. To locate it without a closed formula, use bisection on [0, 10]. The successive retained brackets are [0, 5], [2.5, 5], [2.5, 3.75], [2.5, 3.125] and [2.8125, 3.125]. After five halvings the bracket length is 10/2⁵ = 0.3125. Its midpoint 2.96875 is guaranteed within 0.15625 of the true root; its actual error here is 0.03125. The guaranteed bound comes from bracketing, not from knowing the solution in advance. If supply or demand jumps, opposite endpoint signs alone need not imply equality at any price. If excess demand is continuous but not strictly monotone, multiple clearing prices may exist. These are mathematical conditions on this model; no real market's adjustment process is established by the example. Limits and modeling decisions A small numerical residual does not always imply a small error in the underlying parameter. If a function is almost flat near its zero, a substantial input error can produce little output error. Bisection's bracket gives an input error bound; evaluating |z(p)| alone gives an output mismatch. Keep the criterion aligned with what the decision requires. At discontinuities, a limit from one side may represent a meaningful scenario but should be named explicitly. When a rule changes at a tax threshold or transaction size, specify whether the threshold itself uses the old or new rule. An algebraic equality established for x ≠ a cannot justify the value at a without an additional argument. Exercises Find the real domains of ln(4 − x²), √(x − 1)/(x − 3), and h(x) = ln(√x). State each as a set or interval and explain the excluded endpoints. Prove that aₙ = 5 − 3/n converges to 5. Give an explicit N ensuring an error strictly less than 0.01. Explain why convergence does not require a term equal to 5. Determine whether (−1)ⁿ + 1/n converges. Then prove sin(n)/n² → 0 using a bound. Distinguish a bounded sequence from a convergent one. For f(x) = (x² − 9)/(x − 3), x ≠ 3, compute the limit at 3 and choose f(3) for continuity. Prove directly that x² → 9 as x → 3 using a δ depending on ε. Define T(x) = 2x for x < 1 and T(x) = x + c for x ≥ 1. Find c making T continuous at 1. Determine whether the function with that c has a derivative at 1 using its left and right slopes. Let z(p) = 30/(1 + p) − 3p on [0, 10]. Establish existence and uniqueness of a zero without relying on the quadratic formula. Then compute the permissible root. How many bisection halvings from [0, 10] suffice to guarantee midpoint error at most 0.01? Construct a function on [0, 2] with a negative value at 0, a positive value at 2, and no zero. Explain exactly which hypothesis of the intermediate value theorem fails. Contrast this with f(x) = x on (0, 1), which has no maximum. A sequence of truncated discounted receipts is Pₙ = Σ t=1 n 12/(1.2)ᵗ. Derive Pₙ, its limit, and the exact omitted tail. Find the smallest positive integer n making the tail less than 1. Explain what fails if the gross discount factor is 1. Solutions and proof guidance The logarithm requires 4 − x² > 0, so (−2, 2). The square root requires x ≥ 1 and the denominator excludes 3, giving [1, 3) ∪ (3, ∞). For ln(√x), the intermediate square root must be strictly positive, so x > 0. Zero is allowed for a square root alone but excluded by the outer logarithm. The error is 3/n. For any ε > 0 choose an integer N > 3/ε; then n ≥ N implies 3/n < ε. For 0.01 take N = 301, since at 300 the error equals rather than falls below 0.01. Each finite term is below 5, consistently with convergence to it. The even subsequence approaches 1 and the odd subsequence approaches −1, so no common limit exists. Every subsequence of a convergent sequence must share its limit, directly from the “all sufficiently late indices” condition. For the second sequence, |sin(n)/n²| ≤ 1/n²; for each ε > 0 choose N > 1/√ε. Boundedness of the oscillating numerator supplies a bound, not its own convergence. For x ≠ 3 cancel to x + 3; the limit is 6 and the continuous extension sets f(3) = 6. To prove the quadratic limit, if |x − 3| < 1 then |x + 3| < 7. Choose δ = min{1, ε/7}; |x² − 9| = |x − 3||x + 3| < ε. The proof does not divide by an expression that could be zero. The left limit is 2; the right limit and value are 1 + c, so c = 1. With that choice the left slope is 2 and the right slope is 1. Continuity holds but a common two-sided derivative does not. Continuity holds on the domain. z(0) = 30 > 0 and z(10) = 30/11 − 30 < 0, so a zero exists. Strictly decreasing demand minus strictly increasing supply gives uniqueness. The equation p² + p − 10 = 0 gives p = (−1 + √41)/2 ≈ 2.701562; the other root is negative. After k halvings the midpoint bound is 10/2ᵏ⁺¹. Requiring it ≤ 0.01 gives 2ᵏ ≥ 500, hence k = 9 suffices; k = 8 does not. Let f(x) = −1 for x < 1 and f(x) = 1 for x ≥ 1. It jumps at 1 and never equals zero. Continuity on the whole closed interval fails. For x on (0, 1), continuity holds, but the domain is not closed: every candidate value can be exceeded by moving toward 1, and 1 is absent. The missing maximum is not a failure of the extreme value theorem. With v = 1/1.2 = 5/6, Pₙ = 12v(1 − vⁿ)/(1 − v) = 60(1 − (5/6)ⁿ). The limit is 60 and the omitted tail is 60(5/6)ⁿ. At n = 22 the tail is about 1.08684; at 23 it is about 0.90570, so 23 is smallest. With gross factor 1, each term is 12 and Pₙ = 12n diverges rather than approaching a finite value. The question fixes a discount benchmark and constant receipts; it does not price uncertain risk. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain the order of ε and N, then the order of ε and δ, using a concrete error requirement. Explain why opposite endpoint signs need continuity for existence and why strict monotonicity supplies a different conclusion. Delayed check: prove a new polynomial limit with an explicit neighborhood bound, classify a piecewise threshold, and rebuild a bisection error guarantee. Derive a discounted tail rather than relying on the rounded infinite-sum value. Further study and scope MIT 18.100A's lecture collection provides independent study of sequences, function limits and continuity; Lectures 6–10 and 13–16 are relevant. More general compactness and interchange-of-limits issues return in Chapter 10. The examples here require only single-variable real functions, not yet multidimensional equilibrium theory. The immediate successor is differentiation. A derivative adds a precise first-order response to the continuity framework. Keep existence of a function value, existence of its limit, continuity and differentiability as separate questions."}, {"title": "02.03 · Derivatives and economic change", "url": "v02-c03.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "From changes over intervals to local responses A marginal cost measures how total cost responds locally to output. A price elasticity measures a proportional response rather than an absolute one. A bond's rate sensitivity measures how its value changes when a discount-rate input changes under specified assumptions. These quantities share one mathematical tool: the derivative. A derivative is not merely a symbol produced by rules. It is a limit of changes divided by input changes, and it describes a local approximation. Keep the domain, units, held-fixed inputs and size of the contemplated change visible. A derivative in a model is not automatically a causal effect in data; the model's relationship must first be justified. The definition and its approximation meaning For an interior domain point a, define f′(a) = lim h→0 [f(a + h) − f(a)]/h when the limit exists and is finite. The numerator is the output change; h is the input change. A two-sided derivative requires the same limiting ratio for positive and negative h. At a boundary, specify a one-sided derivative rather than implying movement beyond the feasible domain. The derivative of f(x) = x² follows directly: [(a + h)² − a²]/h = 2a + h for h ≠ 0, which approaches 2a. For f(x) = c, the numerator is zero, so the derivative is zero. For f(x) = mx + b, it is mh, giving derivative m. These computations connect constant and linear functions to the familiar slope. Writing the limit as an approximation gives f(a + h) = f(a) + f′(a)h + r(h), where r(h)/h → 0 as h → 0. The remainder is of smaller order than the input change. The notation r(h) = o(h) records that ratio condition; it does not mean the remainder is zero. For x² the remainder is exactly h². If a derivative exists, continuity follows. The output difference equals h times a difference quotient that approaches a finite value, so it approaches zero. The converse fails: f(x) = |x| is continuous at zero, but its difference quotient is 1 for h > 0 and −1 for h < 0. A participation fee can cause discontinuity, while a change in marginal pricing can leave continuity with a kink. Neither should be smoothed away merely to use differentiation. If output is dollars and input is units of goods, f′ has dollars-per-unit dimensions. If output is a price and input is a rate expressed as a decimal, the derivative measures dollars per unit change in that decimal. A 1 percentage point rate increase is Δr = 0.01. Multiplying by 1 would instead analyze a 100 percentage point change. Rules, their derivation and their domains For differentiable functions, linearity gives (af + bg)′ = af′ + bg′ for constants a and b. The product rule is (fg)′ = f′g + fg′. To see why, insert and subtract f(a + h)g(a) in the output difference. Dividing by h yields g(a)[f(a + h) − f(a)]/h + f(a + h)[g(a + h) − g(a)]/h. Taking limits uses continuity of f and produces the two terms. Differentiating a product by multiplying derivatives would omit both correct contributions. The chain rule says (f ∘ g)′(x) = f′(g(x))g′(x), provided g is differentiable at x and f is differentiable at g(x). The inner input first changes by g′(x)h + o(h); the outer output then changes by f′(g(x)) times that change plus a smaller remainder. This derivation works even when the inner change is zero on some inputs, unlike an informal cancellation that divides by it. For example, d[(3x + 1)²]/dx = 2(3x + 1) × 3. The outer derivative is evaluated at the inner value. When a composite function has several layers, work from the outside inward and multiply the layer responses. Write intermediate variables if this helps preserve the dependence structure. Integer powers satisfy d(xⁿ)/dx = nxⁿ⁻¹, obtained by repeatedly using the product rule. Differentiating 1 = xⁿx⁻ⁿ where x ≠ 0 extends the rule to negative integer powers. For general real powers on x > 0, the same formula follows from xᵃ = e^(a ln x). Square roots require separate boundary care: d√x/dx = 1/(2√x) for x > 0, with no finite right derivative at zero. The standard exponential satisfies d(eˣ)/dx = eˣ; equivalently its defining local normalization is lim h→0 (eʰ − 1)/h = 1. The inverse-function relationship then gives d(ln x)/dx = 1/x for x > 0. For a positive constant b, d(b x )/dx = b x ln b. The sine and cosine rules are d(sin x)/dx = cos x and d(cos x)/dx = −sin x, with angles in radians. These standard elementary-function results can be justified from their constructions; the present economic applications chiefly use powers, exponentials and logarithms. The reciprocal and product rules give the quotient rule: (f/g)′ = (f′g − fg′)/g² where g ≠ 0. One can derive the reciprocal rule directly by subtracting 1/g(a + h) and 1/g(a), putting them over a common denominator, and taking the limit. A valid symbolic derivative is usable only where both the original function and the required differentiability conditions hold. Higher derivatives apply differentiation again: f″ is the second derivative and f‴ the third. A positive f″ means the first derivative is increasing on an interval under the usual continuity/differentiability conditions; it does not imply the function's level is positive or increasing. For f(x) = x² − 10x, f″ = 2 but f′ = 2x − 10 is negative for x < 5. The mean value theorem and slope claims If f is continuous on [a, b] and differentiable on (a, b), with a < b, the mean value theorem ensures some c ∈ (a, b) has f′(c) = [f(b) − f(a)]/(b − a). The average slope over the interval equals a local slope somewhere inside. It does not identify c or assert that the slope is constant. The result follows by subtracting the straight line through the two endpoint values. The resulting function has equal endpoint values. If it is nonconstant, its attained maximum or minimum is interior, where its derivative is zero; if constant, its derivative is zero everywhere. This is Rolle's theorem. Adding the straight-line slope back yields the stated result. The interior zero-slope condition itself follows from the opposing signs required of right and left difference quotients at a differentiable local extremum. Consequently f′ > 0 throughout an interval implies strict increase there. If |f′| ≤ M, then |f(b) − f(a)| ≤ M|b − a|. The derivative at just one point cannot establish either assertion over an interval. Also, a zero derivative is not a sufficient optimality condition: x³ has f′(0) = 0 and no local extremum there, while x² has a minimum rather than a maximum. Constrained and global optimization are developed in Chapters 8–9. Worked example 1 — Marginal, average and proportional cost responses Suppose total cost is C(q) = 40 + 6q + q² dollars for continuous quantity q ≥ 0. For interior q, C′(q) = 6 + 2q dollars per unit and C″(q) = 2 dollars per squared unit. At q = 4, total cost is 80, marginal cost is 14, and average cost is C(q)/q = 20 dollars per unit. They answer different questions. A move from q = 4 to 4.1 gives the first-order estimate ΔC ≈ 14 × 0.1 = 1.4. The exact change is C(4.1) − C(4) = 1.41. The omitted quadratic term is (0.1)² = 0.01, so the linear approximation understates the cost increase. For q > 0, average cost is A(q) = 40/q + 6 + q, with A′(q) = −40/q² + 1. At 4 the derivative is −1.5: average cost is falling locally while total cost is rising. Different denominators and functions eliminate the apparent contradiction. The cost elasticity is ε C,q = C′(q)q/C(q), defined here for q > 0 and C(q) > 0. At 4 it is 14 × 4/80 = 0.7. A 1% quantity increase predicts approximately a 0.7% total-cost increase near that point. It is neither the marginal cost of 14 nor the average cost of 20. Elasticity and logarithmic change For a positive differentiable y = f(x) with x > 0, elasticity is ε y,x = (dy/dx)(x/y). It is dimensionless and equals d ln y/d ln x through the chain rule. This makes proportional comparisons independent of a change from dollars to thousands of dollars or units to thousands of units. For y = Axᵏ with A > 0, elasticity is k everywhere on x > 0. Taking logs gives ln y = ln A + k ln x, so log changes satisfy Δ ln y = k Δ ln x exactly. Ordinary percentage changes satisfy y₂/y₁ = (x₂/x₁)ᵏ; replacing them by k times the input percentage change is usually only a local approximation. Demand elasticity is often negative; some economic discussions report its absolute magnitude. State the convention. A zero or negative level does not permit a real natural logarithm, and elasticity at a zero denominator is undefined. Near zero, large calculated elasticities may reflect the base as much as the economically relevant absolute response. Implicit differentiation Sometimes x and y are related by an equation F(x, y) = 0 rather than an explicit y = f(x). If there is a differentiable solution branch y(x), differentiate the equation while remembering that y depends on x. This produces F x + F y y′ = 0 and hence y′ = −F x /F y when F y ≠ 0. The subscripts mean derivatives with respect to one coordinate holding the other fixed; Chapter 7 formalizes these partial derivatives. The calculation alone does not prove that the branch exists. A continuously differentiable F with nonzero F y at a solution has a local differentiable branch under the implicit-function theorem. At a zero denominator, inspect the original relationship rather than treating an infinite symbolic ratio as an ordinary derivative. For x² + y² = 25, differentiating gives 2x + 2yy′ = 0, so y′ = −x/y where y ≠ 0. At (3, 4), the upper branch has slope −3/4. The lower branch y = −√(25 − x²) has the opposite sign at x = 3. The equation defines more than one branch; the chosen point and domain determine the local response. Worked example 2 — Rate sensitivity with a second-order correction A certain payment of 121 at date 2 is discounted at a constant per-period decimal rate r > −1: P(r) = 121(1 + r)⁻². This is a stipulated discount model, with payment and date held fixed. Differentiation gives P′(r) = −242(1 + r)⁻³ and P″(r) = 726(1 + r)⁻⁴. At r₀ = 0.10, P = 100, P′ ≈ −181.818182 and P″ ≈ 495.867769. Raising the rate by h = 0.01 gives a linear estimate P ≈ 100 − 1.818182 = 98.181818. Including the quadratic term gives P ≈ 100 − 1.818182 + 0.5 × 495.867769 × 0.01² = 98.206612. The exact price at 0.11 is 121/1.11² ≈ 98.206314. The first-order price error is about −0.024496; the second-order error is about +0.000298. Positive curvature explains why the tangent-line estimate lies below the actual price for this increase. For a bound, P‴(r) = −2904(1 + r)⁻⁵. On [0.10, 0.11] its absolute value is at most 2904/1.1⁵ ≈ 1803.155522. Taylor's second-order remainder is therefore bounded by M|h|³/6 ≈ 0.000301. This bound is meaningful because the interval and third-derivative bound are specified. The local proportional sensitivity −P′/P is 2/(1 + r), equal to about 1.818182 at 0.10. Later fixed-income chapters distinguish duration definitions and multiple yield-curve changes. This example varies a single rate and says nothing about credit-risk changes or which discount rate is appropriate. Taylor approximation and disciplined error claims If f has continuous second derivative near a, the quadratic expansion is f(a + h) = f(a) + f′(a)h + ½f″(a)h² + o(h²). Under stronger regularity, Taylor's theorem supplies an explicit bound: with continuous third derivative on the interval between a and a + h and |f‴| ≤ M there, the second-order remainder has absolute value at most M|h|³/6. A first-order expansion similarly has error at most K|h|²/2 if |f″| ≤ K on that interval. A higher-order approximation may improve accuracy without settling model error. A bond formula evaluated to ten decimals can still be economically unsuitable if the payment is uncertain or the discount assumptions are wrong. Separate truncation error, arithmetic rounding and uncertainty in the relationship itself. For ln(1 + h) around zero, the quadratic approximation is h − h²/2. It requires h > −1 and is locally accurate; for h = 0.1 it gives 0.095 versus ln(1.1) ≈ 0.09531018. Saying “log growth equals percentage growth” without the approximation qualifier would be false. Exercises Derive the derivative of f(x) = 3x² + 2x − 7 directly from its difference quotient. At x = 2 and h = 0.05, compare the exact output change with the first-order estimate and state the exact remainder. Differentiate (x² + 1)e −x , ln(2x + 3), and (3x + 1)/(x − 2). Give the relevant real domains and identify which rules are used. For C(q) = 25 + 4q + 2q² with q ≥ 0, compute total, marginal and average cost at q = 5. Compute the total-cost elasticity there and the derivative of average cost. Interpret their units and signs. Synthetic demand is Q(p) = 80p −1.5 for p > 0. Find the signed elasticity. For a price increase of 10%, compare the elasticity-based local prediction for quantity change with its exact proportional change. For x² + xy + y² = 7, calculate the local y′ at (1, 2). State the denominator condition. Find the two solutions for y at x = 1 and explain why specifying the branch matters. Use a quadratic Taylor expansion at zero to approximate e 0.2 . Bound its error using |f‴| ≤ e 0.2 < 1.222 on [0, 0.2]. Compare with the exact exponential and explain whether the estimate is above or below it. Explain why f′(a) = 0 does not establish a maximum, using x², −x² and x³ at zero. Explain why |x| at zero has a minimum without a two-sided derivative, and why an endpoint optimum need not satisfy an interior condition. A payment of 110 at date 1 has P(r) = 110/(1 + r), r > −1. At r = 0.10, compute P, P′ and P″. Estimate the price at 0.12 using first- and second-order Taylor expansions and compare with the exact price. State the decimal rate change and the assumptions held fixed. Solutions and derivations The quotient is 3[(x + h)² − x²]/h + 2 = 6x + 3h + 2, approaching 6x + 2. At 2 the slope is 14; the linear change is 0.7. The exact change is 0.7 + 3(0.05)² = 0.7075, and the remainder is 0.0075. The constant cancels. The derivatives are e −x [2x − (x² + 1)] on ℝ, 2/(2x + 3) for x > −1.5, and [3(x − 2) − (3x + 1)]/(x − 2)² = −7/(x − 2)² for x ≠ 2. They use product plus chain, logarithm plus chain, and quotient respectively. None acquires a value at an excluded domain point by differentiation. C(5) = 95 dollars, C′(5) = 24 dollars per unit, and C(5)/5 = 19 dollars per unit. Elasticity is 24 × 5/95 = 24/19 ≈ 1.263158, dimensionless. Average cost is 25/q + 4 + 2q; its derivative at 5 is −25/25 + 2 = 1 dollar per squared unit. Both total cost and average cost increase locally there. Q′ = −120p −2.5 and pQ′/Q = −1.5. A 10% price rise predicts approximately −15% quantity change. Exactly Q₂/Q₁ = 1.1 −1.5 ≈ 0.866784, so quantity falls about 13.3216%. Constant elasticity makes the relation between log changes exact, not the finite ordinary percentage approximation. Differentiate to get 2x + y + (x + 2y)y′ = 0, hence y′ = −(2x + y)/(x + 2y), requiring x + 2y ≠ 0. At (1, 2), slope is −4/5. At x = 1, y² + y − 6 = 0 gives y = 2 or −3; the latter has slope −1/5. Each solution is on a different local branch, so the relation alone does not specify which response is intended. The quadratic value is 1 + 0.2 + 0.2²/2 = 1.22. The error bound is 1.222 × 0.2³/6 ≈ 0.001629333. The exact value is about 1.221402758, giving error about −0.001402758, within the bound. All higher exponential derivatives are positive, so the quadratic expansion is below the exact value for this positive increment; Taylor's third-order remainder has positive sign. x² has a minimum, −x² has a maximum, and x³ has neither at zero, despite all three derivatives being zero. |x| has a minimum at its nondifferentiable kink. On [0, 1], f(x) = x has a maximum at 1 with slope 1; the interior first-order condition does not apply at that boundary. A condition can be necessary under particular hypotheses without being sufficient or universally necessary. At 0.10, P = 100, P′ = −110/1.1² ≈ −90.909091, and P″ = 220/1.1³ ≈ 165.289256. h = 0.02, a 2 percentage point increase. Linear price is 98.181818; quadratic price is 98.214876. The exact price 110/1.12 is about 98.214286, so respective errors are approximately −0.032468 and +0.000590. Payment certainty, date, amount and the single-rate discount model are fixed. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: derive a product rule rather than merely naming it. Explain why marginal and average cost differ, translate an elasticity into a local proportional response, and state the assumptions behind a Taylor error bound. Delayed check: differentiate a new composite function, compute a response with correct units, diagnose a kink, and construct a stationary point that is not an optimum. Recompute the rate-sensitivity example with a decrease and compare predicted and exact prices. Further study and scope MIT's Single Variable Calculus course supplies further derivative instruction and independent practice. This chapter is an original economic application of standard calculus, not a reproduction of its problems. For proof-oriented extensions, MIT 18.100A Lectures 17–20 cover derivative definitions, differentiation rules, the mean value theorem and Taylor's theorem. Multivariable responses and existence of implicit branches receive fuller treatment in Chapter 7; optimization conditions follow in Chapters 8–9."}, {"title": "02.04 · Integrals and accumulated quantities", "url": "v02-c04.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Recovering totals from rates of change A marginal cost curve tells you how total cost changes with output, but it does not reveal the initial fixed cost. A flow of dollars per year does not equal a dollar stock without accumulation over time. A stream of discounted receipts requires both a timing model and a rule for adding contributions. Integration makes these relationships precise. The simplest picture is many narrow rectangles: each has height equal to a rate and width equal to a small amount of the input. Their summed area approximates accumulated output. The integral is the limiting total when the partition becomes arbitrarily fine. Its units are the rate's units multiplied by the input's units. This chapter focuses on real single-variable Riemann integration, sufficient for the continuous models here; probability and more general integration come later. Definite integrals and signed accumulation Partition [a, b] into ordered endpoints a = x₀ < x₁ < … < xₙ = b. In each interval choose a tag ξᵢ and width Δxᵢ = xᵢ − xᵢ₋₁. A Riemann sum is Σᵢ f(ξᵢ)Δxᵢ. The largest interval width is the partition's mesh. A bounded function is Riemann integrable if these sums approach the same finite number as the mesh approaches zero, regardless of the tags and partitions. Write that number ∫ a b f(x) dx. The “regardless” clause matters: one selected sequence of rectangles is not the whole definition. Continuous functions on closed bounded intervals are integrable. Many piecewise continuous functions with finitely many jumps are also integrable: narrow intervals around the jumps contribute arbitrarily little area, while continuity controls the rest. A point's value can affect continuity without changing the integral. An integral is signed accumulation. Where f is negative, its contribution is negative. The geometric area between a curve and the horizontal axis instead uses ∫|f|. For example ∫ −1 1 x dx = 0, while ∫ −1 1 |x| dx = 1. Positive and negative contributions cancel in the first, not in the second. Integration is linear: ∫(αf + βg) = α∫f + β∫g over the same interval for constants α and β and integrable functions. It is additive over adjacent intervals: ∫ a c f = ∫ a b f + ∫ b c f. Reversing endpoints changes the sign, and equal endpoints give zero. If f ≥ g, then ∫f ≥ ∫g for an interval oriented from smaller to larger input. If |f| ≤ M on [a, b], the integral's absolute value is at most M(b − a). This inequality explains how a small input interval limits accumulation for a bounded rate, and it supports error analysis. If the rate is unbounded near an endpoint, this bound is unavailable; an improper integral needs a separate limit. Worked example 1 — Recover total cost and reconcile rectangle bounds Suppose marginal cost is m(q) = 6 + 2q dollars per unit for q ≥ 0, and initial cost C(0) = 40 dollars. Accumulated variable cost to q = 4 is ∫ 0 4 (6 + 2q) dq = [6q + q²] 0 4 = 40 dollars. Therefore C(4) = 40 + 40 = 80, matching the total-cost function in the preceding chapter. For a direct sum check, divide [0, 4] into n equal widths 4/n. The rate is increasing, so left endpoint rectangles underestimate and right endpoint rectangles overestimate. The left sum is 24 + (32/n²)Σ i=0 n−1 i = 24 + 16(n − 1)/n = 40 − 16/n. The right sum is 40 + 16/n. The finite sum Σ i=0 n−1 i = n(n − 1)/2 follows by pairing first and last terms; both bounds converge to 40. For n = 4 the bounds are 36 and 44, and for n = 100 they are 39.84 and 40.16. The midpoint rectangles give exactly 40 for this linear rate because their average heights equal the average of the endpoint heights. That special exactness does not hold for every curved rate. Integrating marginal cost identifies changes in cost, not the initial level. If C(0) were 70, the same marginal curve would imply C(4) = 110. Also, the continuous quantity model is an approximation when goods must be purchased in indivisible units. A discrete cost difference may then be the relevant object. Antiderivatives and the fundamental theorem An antiderivative F of f on an interval satisfies F′ = f there. The indefinite integral notation ∫f(x) dx = F(x) + K describes a family differing by a constant K. If F and G have the same derivative on an interval, their difference has zero derivative and is constant by the mean value theorem. Disconnected domains can require different constants on separate components. The fundamental theorem of calculus links the limiting sum definition to derivatives. First, if f is continuous on [a, b] and A(x) = ∫ a x f(t) dt, then A′(x) = f(x) for interior x. Its difference quotient is the average value of f over the small interval between x and x + h. Continuity makes that average approach f(x). More precisely, A(x + h) − A(x) = ∫ x x+h f(t) dt. Subtract f(x)h and divide by h. For sufficiently small nonzero h, continuity makes |f(t) − f(x)| less than ε throughout the relevant interval, so the resulting average error has absolute value less than ε. The reasoning works for either sign of h through oriented integrals. Second, if f is continuous on [a, b], F is continuous on [a, b], and F′ = f on (a, b), then ∫ a b f(x) dx = F(b) − F(a). To justify it, A and F have the same derivative in the interior, hence differ by a constant; compare their endpoint values using continuity. This evaluation rule is often written [F(x)] a b . Thus a differentiable total with continuous marginal rate obeys C(q) = C(0) + ∫ 0 q C′(s) ds. An initial condition sets the constant. If a total has a jump, integrating its ordinary derivative alone omits that jump; fixed participation costs require a separate term. Calculus must reflect the actual piecewise relationship. A small antiderivative toolkit For n ≠ −1, ∫xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) + K where the real power is defined and differentiable. On x > 0 this holds for any real n except −1. The exceptional case is ∫1/x dx = ln|x| + K on intervals excluding zero. The absolute value accommodates the negative component without making a logarithm of a negative number. For constant k ≠ 0, ∫e kx dx = e kx /k + K; when k = 0, the integrand is 1 and its antiderivative is x. Also ∫cos x dx = sin x + K and ∫sin x dx = −cos x + K. Verify each candidate by differentiation. Integration generally has no product rule that simply multiplies antiderivatives. The logarithm can also be constructed by ln x = ∫ 1 x (1/t) dt for x > 0. The fundamental theorem then gives its derivative 1/x. A substitution t = au establishes ln(ab) = ln a + ln b for positive a and b, connecting its accumulated-rate definition to multiplicative growth. Its inverse is the exponential. Substitution: undo a chain rule If u = g(x), the differential notation du = g′(x) dx records the change-of-variable rule. For a continuously differentiable g and continuous f on the relevant image, ∫ a b f(g(x))g′(x) dx = ∫ g(a) g(b) f(u) du. This follows by composing an antiderivative of f with g and applying the chain rule and fundamental theorem. For ∫ 0 2 2x/(1 + x²) dx, set u = 1 + x² and du = 2x dx. The endpoints become 1 and 5, giving ∫ 1 5 du/u = ln 5. Either evaluate entirely in u with transformed endpoints or convert the antiderivative back to x before using the original endpoints. Mixing these conventions produces wrong values. Substitution does not require g to be increasing for this antiderivative identity. Its endpoint order handles a decreasing map. More general changes of variables for domains and probability densities require additional attention to one-to-one mappings and absolute Jacobians; those are taught where needed rather than imported silently here. Integration by parts: undo a product rule The product rule (uv)′ = u′v + uv′ yields ∫ a b u(x)v′(x) dx = [u(x)v(x)] a b − ∫ a b u′(x)v(x) dx, for continuously differentiable functions on the interval. The abbreviated notation is ∫u dv = uv − ∫v du. Choose u so differentiating it simplifies the remaining integral, and choose dv so it has a known antiderivative. For ∫ 0 T t e −rt dt with r > 0, choose u = t and dv = e −rt dt, giving v = −e −rt /r. The result is [−te −rt /r] 0 T + (1/r)∫ 0 T e −rt dt = [1 − (1 + rT)e −rT ]/r². Differentiate the indefinite version −te −rt /r − e −rt /r² to check the cancellation. For ∫ln x dx on x > 0, take u = ln x and dv = dx. This gives x ln x − ∫1 dx = x ln x − x + K. A rule choice should reduce the unresolved problem. If it makes the new integral harder, reconsider the decomposition. Improper integrals and convergence An improper integral replaces an infinite endpoint or an unbounded endpoint integrand by a limit of ordinary integrals. Define ∫ a ∞ f(x) dx = lim T→∞ ∫ a T f(x) dx when that limit is finite. A lower-endpoint singularity uses lim ε↓0 ∫ ε b f(x) dx. If there are multiple problematic endpoints, require each separately to converge. Cancellation under one specially coupled truncation is not ordinary improper convergence. For p ≠ 1, ∫ 1 T x⁻ᵖ dx = (T¹⁻ᵖ − 1)/(1 − p). As T → ∞ this is finite exactly when p > 1, then equal to 1/(p − 1). At p = 1 it is ln T and diverges. Near zero, ∫ ε 1 x⁻ᵖ dx is finite exactly when p < 1, then equal to 1/(1 − p). The same exponent can therefore be harmless at one boundary and problematic at another. For nonnegative integrands, comparison provides a useful convergence argument: if 0 ≤ f ≤ g eventually and ∫g converges, then ∫f converges; if f ≥ g ≥ 0 and ∫g diverges, ∫f diverges. The first conclusion uses monotone bounded accumulation. Sign-changing integrands need more care, and absolute convergence is stronger than convergence with cancellations. Worked example 2 — Discount a growing continuous receipt stream Suppose receipts arrive continuously at flow rate c(t) = 120e 0.02t dollars per year, for t measured in years. A stipulated continuously compounded annual discount rate is r = 0.05. The present-value integrand is c(t)e −rt = 120e −0.03t dollars per year. Over five years, present value is ∫ 0 5 120e −0.03t dt = 120(1 − e −0.15 )/0.03 ≈ 557.168094 dollars. The discount is continuous because the model's receipts are continuous; a year-end payment sequence would require a discrete sum with its own dates. Over an infinite horizon, the improper integral converges to 120/0.03 = 4,000 dollars. The remaining present value after year 5 is 4,000e −0.15 ≈ 3,442.831906, so finite-horizon value plus tail equals 4,000. A large tail here is a consequence of indefinitely persistent growing receipts and a small net discount rate. More generally, with flow c₀e gt and discount r, convergence requires r > g, giving c₀/(r − g). If r = g, discounted flow is constant and accumulation grows linearly without bound. If r < g, discounted flow grows and the integral also diverges. Calling an infinite-horizon expression a valuation formula without checking r − g would be invalid. Rates r and g have inverse-year units, so dividing dollars per year by r − g gives dollars. Continuous compounding and an annual effective rate are different conventions: an effective yearly rate i corresponds to r = ln(1 + i) under this constant-rate convention. The example specifies a benchmark discount rate, not an assessment that it adequately prices uncertain receipts. Accumulation, surplus and limits of interpretation If an inverse-demand curve p(q) is interpreted as marginal willingness to pay, ∫ 0 Q p(q) dq gives the corresponding gross willingness-to-pay area, and subtracting price times Q gives a surplus area. This economic interpretation needs assumptions about preferences, money and whose curve is represented. The integral itself is a mathematical total, not proof of a complete social-welfare measure. For p(q) = 12 − q on [0, 12] and a price of 4, demand is Q = 8 and the area above price is ∫ 0 8 (8 − q) dq = 32. If demand was inferred from selected observations or money has materially changing marginal value, stronger welfare claims require additional economics and evidence. Later microeconomics develops those distinctions. When an antiderivative is unavailable, approximate the definite integral numerically with a specified error criterion. Chapter 12 develops quadrature methods. Never treat an arbitrary sum of samples as an integral without including interval widths, endpoint conventions and enough control of the approximation. Exercises Recover C(q) from C′(q) = 4 + 3q² and C(0) = 15. Compute C(2) and the cost change from q = 1 to q = 2. Explain what would remain unidentified without the initial condition. Evaluate ∫ −2 2 x dx and ∫ −2 2 |x| dx. Interpret the difference. Explain whether changing the integrand's value only at zero alters either Riemann integral. Use substitution to evaluate ∫ 0 1 3x²e x³ dx. Show the transformed endpoints and independently differentiate the resulting antiderivative. Use integration by parts to evaluate ∫ 1 e ln x dx and ∫ 0 1 x e x dx. State u, dv and the endpoint terms for each. Determine convergence and values, when finite, of ∫ 1 ∞ x⁻² dx, ∫ 0 1 x −1/2 dx, and ∫ 0 1 x⁻¹ dx. Express each as an endpoint limit rather than substituting infinity or zero into an undefined expression. A constant continuous receipt flow is 80 dollars per year, discounted continuously at 0.04 per year. Compute its present value over ten years and over an infinite horizon, and its remaining present value after year ten. Find the horizon T making the remaining value less than 100. For marginal willingness to pay p(q) = 20 − 2q on [0, 10] and a posted price of 8, compute demanded quantity, expenditure, gross area and the surplus area. State an assumption needed to interpret the last area economically. Let C(0) = 0 and C(q) = 30 + 5q for q > 0. A student integrates the ordinary marginal rate 5 from zero and reports C(4) = 20. Diagnose the omission. Also evaluate ∫ 0 2 (6 + 2q) dq using n equal left and right rectangles, and give a guaranteed error bound for each. Solutions and endpoint checks C(q) = 15 + 4q + q³ for q ≥ 0. C(2) = 31, C(1) = 20, so the increase is 11. Without C(0), all functions K + 4q + q³ have the same marginal rate; the level K is unidentified. A difference between two quantities remains identified. The signed integral is [x²/2] −2 2 = 0. The absolute-area integral is twice ∫ 0 2 x dx = 4. Positive and negative accumulation cancels only in the signed case. A finite changed value at a single point affects neither integral, since that point can be enclosed in intervals of arbitrarily small width. Set u = x³ and du = 3x²dx; endpoints are 0 and 1. The integral is ∫ 0 1 e u du = e − 1. Differentiating e x³ gives 3x²e x³ , confirming the antiderivative on the stated domain. For ln x, take u = ln x, dv = dx, v = x. The value is [x ln x − x] 1 e = 0 − (−1) = 1. For xe x , take u = x, dv = e x dx, v = e x . The antiderivative is xe x − e x and its endpoint difference is 0 − (−1) = 1. The first is lim T→∞ (1 − 1/T) = 1. The second is lim ε↓0 2(1 − √ε) = 2. The third is lim ε↓0 (−ln ε), which grows without bound, so it has no finite improper value. A finite truncated answer is not evidence of convergence. PV₁₀ = 80(1 − e −0.4 )/0.04 = 2,000(1 − e −0.4 ) ≈ 659.359908. PV∞ = 2,000. The tail is 2,000e −0.4 ≈ 1,340.640092. Require 2,000e −0.04T < 100, hence T > ln(20)/0.04 ≈ 74.893307 years; equality at that horizon gives exactly 100, not less. This long horizon follows from the stipulated persistent flow and rate. 20 − 2Q = 8 gives Q = 6. Expenditure is 48; gross area ∫ 0 6 (20 − 2q) dq = 120 − 36 = 84; surplus area is 84 − 48 = 36. One sufficient interpretation assumes utility equals unspent money plus a benefit u(q), with u′(q) = p(q), so each dollar has a constant utility contribution. This is called a quasi-linear preference model. The geometric area alone does not establish distributional or complete social-welfare conclusions. For positive q there is an initial participation jump of 30, so C(4) = 50. The ordinary derivative on q > 0 is 5 and its integral recovers only the variable increment, not the discontinuity at zero. For the separate linear rate on [0, 2], the integral is 16. With width 2/n, the left sum is 16 − 4/n and right sum is 16 + 4/n, each with exact absolute error 4/n in this case. Increasing-rate ordering establishes which is below and above. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain an integral through units and narrow rectangles, then derive the evaluation rule from accumulation and differentiation. Show how an initial condition, a participation jump and a tail-convergence condition answer three different questions. Delayed check: recover a new total from a marginal curve, evaluate one substitution and one integration-by-parts problem, and assess an infinite horizon before computing its value. Include the exact truncated-tail expression in the answer. Further study and scope MIT's Single Variable Calculus course offers independent integration practice, including definite integrals and standard techniques. The receipt streams and cost cases here are original constructions with explicitly stated conventions. For a proof-oriented treatment, MIT 18.100A Lectures 20–22 develop Riemann sums, integrability, the fundamental theorem, change of variables and integration by parts. Later probability instruction adds expectations and densities; advanced analysis distinguishes broader integration concepts when the applications require them."}, {"title": "02.05 · Vectors, matrices and linear systems", "url": "v02-c05.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Several equations do not necessarily provide several answers A firm may face several resource constraints, an analyst may observe several financial outcomes, and a researcher may estimate several coefficients. Counting equations is insufficient. Some equations can duplicate others, some can contradict them, and some can leave economically important directions unresolved. Linear algebra identifies these possibilities before a solver's output is mistaken for a uniquely determined decision. This chapter develops vectors, matrices, elimination, rank and the spaces that describe solutions. The examples are synthetic and exact. Statistical noise, inference and numerical error are separate issues developed later, but the underlying algebra already reveals what information a system can and cannot supply. Vectors, coordinates and units A vector x ∈ ℝⁿ is an ordered collection of n real coordinates. Unless stated otherwise, vectors are columns; xᵀ is the corresponding row, and T denotes transpose. Coordinatewise addition and scalar multiplication give x + y and cx. A linear combination c₁v₁ + … + cₖvₖ adds specified multiples of vectors of the same dimension. The dot product xᵀy = Σᵢ xᵢyᵢ is a scalar. For a price vector p and quantity vector q in matching order, pᵀq is total expenditure. With p = (3, 5)ᵀ and q = (4, 2)ᵀ, expenditure is 3 × 4 + 5 × 2 = 22. Each product must have a common output unit. Swapping one vector's order without swapping the other changes the meaning. The Euclidean norm ‖x‖₂ = √(xᵀx) measures geometric length. It is useful for errors measured in comparable coordinates. Squaring and adding quantities in dollars and percentages without scaling is not a neutral measurement choice. A norm can be mathematically well defined while economically inappropriate for the task. The standard coordinate vectors e₁, …, eₙ each have a single coordinate equal to 1 and the rest zero. Every x is Σᵢ xᵢeᵢ. This representation is unique because each coefficient determines one separate coordinate. Alternative coordinate systems can be useful, but their meaning and units must also be stated. Matrices are linear mappings An m × n matrix A has m rows and n columns, with entry aᵢⱼ in row i and column j. It maps an n-coordinate input x to an m-coordinate output Ax. The ith output is (Ax)ᵢ = Σⱼ aᵢⱼxⱼ. Equivalently, Ax is the linear combination of A's columns using x's coordinates as coefficients. The row view describes equations; the column view describes attainable outputs. Matrix addition requires matching dimensions. If A is m × n and B is n × k, their product AB is m × k with (AB)ᵢⱼ = Σₗ aᵢₗbₗⱼ. B acts first on an input, then A acts on the result. The shared inner dimension makes composition possible. For mismatched dimensions the product is undefined, not zero. Multiplication is associative and distributes over addition, but is generally not commutative. Even when both AB and BA exist and have the same size, their entries may differ. For A = [[1, 2], [0, 1]] and B = [[2, 0], [1, 3]], AB = [[4, 6], [1, 3]], while BA = [[2, 4], [1, 5]]. Here double brackets list rows. Always preserve the order of transformations. The identity matrix Iₙ has ones on its diagonal and zero elsewhere, giving Iₙx = x. The zero matrix maps every input to zero. Transpose exchanges rows and columns, and (AB)ᵀ = BᵀAᵀ: the entry formula gives the same sum on both sides. This reversed order will matter when deriving least-squares equations. A mapping is linear when it preserves addition and scalar multiplication: A(x + y) = Ax + Ay and A(cx) = cAx. A rule Ax + b with b ≠ 0 is affine, rather than linear, because it does not map the zero vector to zero. Intercepts can be represented through an extra constant coordinate, but that is a modeling representation, not a change in the underlying definition. Solving Ax = b by elimination Write the coefficients and outcomes as an augmented matrix [A | b]. Elementary row operations swap two equations, multiply one by a nonzero constant, or add a multiple of one equation to another. Each is reversible, so it preserves the solution set when applied to both coefficients and outcomes. Gaussian elimination successively creates zeros below chosen nonzero entries called pivots. If a proposed pivot is zero, swap in a row with a nonzero entry in that column if possible; otherwise move to a later column. A zero pivot encountered in one ordering does not itself establish singularity. Back substitution then recovers pivot variables from an upper-triangular system. In reduced row-echelon form, each pivot is 1 and the only nonzero entry in its column, pivots move rightward down the rows, and zero rows are last. A row [0, …, 0 | c] with c ≠ 0 asserts 0 = c and proves inconsistency. A row of zeros on both sides supplies no additional restriction. Columns without pivots correspond to free variables, provided the system is consistent. For a consistent system, choose values for the free variables and solve the pivot variables from the reduced equations. The choices can range over real numbers in pure algebra. Economic constraints such as nonnegative quantities must then be intersected with this solution family. An algebraically unique solution can be economically infeasible, and an algebraically nonunique family can become unique under additional restrictions: x + y = 0 has infinitely many real solutions but only (0, 0) when both coordinates must be nonnegative. Worked example 1 — A uniquely determined resource allocation Two production activities use resource amounts summarized by A = [[2, 1], [1, 3]]. Let activity quantities be x and y. Required resource use b = (14, 17)ᵀ gives 2x + y = 14 and x + 3y = 17. The coefficients record resource units per activity unit; these equations specify exact use, not upper limits or profit maximization. Subtract half the first equation from the second to obtain 2.5y = 10, hence y = 4. Substitution gives x = 5. Check both original equations: 2 × 5 + 4 = 14 and 5 + 3 × 4 = 17. Both quantities are nonnegative, so this solution also respects the stated sign restrictions. There are two pivot columns and no free variable. Uniqueness can also be proved directly: if two solutions differed by (u, v), that difference would satisfy 2u + v = 0 and u + 3v = 0. The first gives v = −2u; the second then gives −5u = 0. Thus u = v = 0, so the original solutions coincide. Changing the outcome vector changes the required activities. For b = (1, 10)ᵀ, the same matrix gives x = −1.4 and y = 3.8. The algebraic solution is unique but violates nonnegativity. Resource equalities, output requirements, feasible inequality sets and optimal choices are distinct objects; interpreting them as interchangeable would invent an economic conclusion. Subspaces, span and independence A subspace of ℝⁿ contains zero and is closed under addition and scalar multiplication. The span of vectors v₁, …, vₖ is the set of all their real linear combinations and is a subspace. A nonnegative orthant is not a subspace because multiplying a nonzero nonnegative vector by −1 leaves it. An affordable set with a finite budget is generally not a subspace either. Vectors are linearly independent if c₁v₁ + … + cₖvₖ = 0 implies every cᵢ = 0. Otherwise a nonzero coefficient combination produces zero, revealing redundancy. This is stronger than merely requiring the vectors to be different. For example (1, 0)ᵀ, (0, 1)ᵀ and (1, 1)ᵀ are different but dependent: the third is the sum of the first two. A basis of a subspace is an independent spanning set. It supplies every vector in the space with a unique coordinate representation. Spanning establishes existence of a representation; independence establishes uniqueness, since subtracting two representations would give a nonzero dependence if their coefficients differed. The number of basis vectors is the subspace's dimension. In finite dimensions, all bases of the same subspace have the same size. One way to see the restriction is that independent vectors expressed using a spanning set cannot outnumber that set: elimination of their coordinate columns would otherwise leave a free coefficient in a zero combination. Applying this argument in both directions gives equal basis sizes. A vector can be independent of a particular list while still belonging to a larger space. Specify the list and ambient space. Independence is an exact algebraic property; vectors very close to dependence can remain independent and still create unstable numerical calculations. The spaces describing solvability The column space Col(A) is the span of A's columns in ℝᵐ. By the column interpretation of Ax, Ax = b has a solution exactly when b ∈ Col(A). The null space Null(A) = {z ∈ ℝⁿ : Az = 0} is a subspace containing input directions invisible to the mapping. The row space is the span of A's rows, viewed as vectors in ℝⁿ. The rank of A is the dimension of its column space and also of its row space, equal to the number of pivots in elimination. Row operations preserve dependencies among columns and the row span; in reduced form the independent pivot columns and nonzero rows each count the same pivots. To obtain a column-space basis from elimination, use the corresponding pivot columns of the original A. Row operations can change the actual column space as a subset of ℝᵐ. The rank-nullity relationship is rank(A) + dim Null(A) = n, where n is the number of input coordinates. In reduced form each nonpivot variable can be freely chosen; its associated special solution forms one null-space basis vector. There are n minus rank(A) such variables. Output dimension m does not replace n in this relationship. If xₚ solves Ax = b, every solution is xₚ + z for some z ∈ Null(A). Indeed A(xₚ + z) = b, and any other solution x gives A(x − xₚ) = 0. Thus consistency and uniqueness can be diagnosed separately: existence needs b in the column space; a consistent system is unique exactly when the null space contains only zero. Full column rank means rank(A) = n, so a solution, when it exists, is unique. Full row rank means rank(A) = m, so every b ∈ ℝᵐ is attainable. A tall matrix with more rows than columns may have unique solutions for attainable outcomes but fail to fit other outcomes exactly. A wide matrix with more columns than rows cannot have full column rank and therefore cannot uniquely determine unrestricted inputs. Worked example 2 — Redundant observations and an invisible direction Consider A = [[1, 2, 3], [2, 4, 6]] and b = (12, 24)ᵀ. The second equation is twice the first. Subtracting twice row 1 from row 2 produces a zero row, leaving x₁ + 2x₂ + 3x₃ = 12. The rank is 1, not 2. Choose x₂ = s and x₃ = t. Then x = (12 − 2s − 3t, s, t)ᵀ = (12, 0, 0)ᵀ + s(−2, 1, 0)ᵀ + t(−3, 0, 1)ᵀ. The two latter vectors form a null-space basis: each maps to zero and they are independent because their second and third coordinates isolate the coefficients. Nullity is 2, consistent with 1 + 2 = 3. A column-space basis is (1, 2)ᵀ; a row-space basis is (1, 2, 3). If activities must be nonnegative, impose s ≥ 0, t ≥ 0 and 2s + 3t ≤ 12. There are still many feasible inputs, including (12, 0, 0)ᵀ and (0, 6, 0)ᵀ. If the recorded outcome instead is (12, 25)ᵀ, elimination gives 0 = 1 in the second row, so there is no exact solution. The new outcome is outside the column space. This could arise from noise or a wrong model, but algebra alone cannot decide which. The next chapter introduces a closest-fit criterion rather than pretending an inconsistent system is exactly solved. Observing the same combination twice does not identify three underlying inputs. Collecting an additional observation helps only if it supplies a genuinely different restriction on the unresolved directions. More rows are not automatically more independent information. Inverse matrices and when to use them A square n × n matrix is invertible if there is an A⁻¹ satisfying A⁻¹A = AA⁻¹ = Iₙ. Then Ax = b has the unique solution x = A⁻¹b for every b. Conversely, a square matrix with rank n maps every outcome uniquely and has an inverse obtained by solving for the inputs corresponding to each identity column. To compute that inverse by elimination, reduce [A | Iₙ] to [Iₙ | A⁻¹]. If the left side cannot be reduced to the identity because rank is deficient, no ordinary inverse exists. For A = [[a, b], [c, d]], determinant ad − bc is nonzero exactly when it is invertible, and A⁻¹ = (ad − bc)⁻¹[[d, −b], [−c, a]]. Multiplying verifies both identity products. The determinant of a square matrix more generally is a scalar detecting whether its columns span the full space; higher-dimensional computation is better handled by elimination than expansion by hand. For the resource matrix above, det A = 5 and A⁻¹ = (1/5)[[3, −1], [−1, 2]], which reproduces (5, 4)ᵀ from (14, 17)ᵀ. An inverse is a useful theoretical expression, but solving a system numerically does not usually require explicitly forming one. Factorization and substitution can be more efficient and more stable. Exact rank and numerical reliability differ. With equations x + y = 2 and x + (1 + ε)y = 2 + ε, ε ≠ 0 determines x = y = 1. A perturbation δ to the second outcome changes y by δ/ε and x by −δ/ε. When ε is tiny, a small data discrepancy is amplified. An invertible matrix can therefore leave practical estimates poorly determined even though their exact mathematical solution is unique. Exercises For prices p = (2, 7, 4)ᵀ and quantities q = (3, 2, 5)ᵀ, compute expenditure, q + (1, 0, −2)ᵀ and ‖q‖₂. Explain why the expenditure has economic units while a norm over goods of different units needs a scaling convention. For A = [[1, 2], [0, 1]] and B = [[2, 0], [1, 3]], compute AB and BA and verify (AB)ᵀ = BᵀAᵀ. Explain the order of operations represented by AB. Solve x + y + z = 6, 2x + y + 3z = 13, and x − y + z = 2 by row elimination. Display the intermediate equations and check the result in all original equations. For A = [[1, 2, −1], [2, 4, −2]], find rank and bases for the column, row and null spaces. Describe all solutions for b = (6, 12)ᵀ. Determine whether b = (6, 13)ᵀ has a solution. Let u = (1, 1, 0)ᵀ, v = (0, 1, 1)ᵀ and w = (1, 2, 1)ᵀ. Determine independence of the three-vector list, give a basis for its span, and express (2, 3, 1)ᵀ in that basis. Prove uniqueness of the two basis coefficients. Find the inverse of A = [[3, 1], [2, 1]] and verify it by multiplication. Solve Ax = (11, 8)ᵀ. State the rank and nullity. A parameter mapping gives observations (α + β, 2α + 2β)ᵀ = (4, 8)ᵀ. Describe all real parameter pairs that fit. If a new observation α − β = 2 is added, determine the pair and explain why the new equation identifies the previous invisible direction. For x + y = 2 and x + 1.001y = 2.001, solve the system. Then change only the second outcome to 2.011 and solve again. Derive the response for arbitrary ε ≠ 0 and perturbation δ. Explain how setting ε = 0 changes the rank and why negative solutions might be disallowed by an economic model. Solutions and space calculations Expenditure is 6 + 14 + 20 = 40. The changed quantity vector is (4, 2, 3)ᵀ, and the norm is √(9 + 4 + 25) = √38. Each price-times-quantity term contributes currency. A raw norm mixes squared goods coordinates; a meaningful distance should specify comparable units or explicit weights/scales. AB = [[4, 6], [1, 3]] and BA = [[2, 4], [1, 5]]. (AB)ᵀ = [[4, 1], [6, 3]]. BᵀAᵀ = [[2, 1], [0, 3]][[1, 0], [2, 1]] gives that same matrix. AB applies B first and A second; the differing BA demonstrates that reversing the transformations changes the result. Subtract twice equation 1 from equation 2 to obtain −y + z = 1. Subtract equation 1 from equation 3 to obtain −2y = −4. Thus y = 2, z = 3, and x = 1. Checks: 1 + 2 + 3 = 6; 2 + 2 + 9 = 13; 1 − 2 + 3 = 2. All three columns are pivots, giving rank 3 and a unique solution. Rank is 1. A column basis is {(1, 2)ᵀ}, a row basis is {(1, 2, −1)}, and a null basis is {(−2, 1, 0)ᵀ, (1, 0, 1)ᵀ}. All consistent solutions are (6 − 2s + t, s, t)ᵀ with s, t ∈ ℝ. For (6, 13)ᵀ the second equation minus twice the first gives 0 = 1, so no exact solution exists. w = u + v, so the list is dependent. u and v are independent: au + bv = 0 forces a = 0 from coordinate 1 and b = 0 from coordinate 3. They span the list, so form a basis. The target is 2u + v; any representation must have coefficient 2 in coordinate 1 and coefficient 1 in coordinate 3, proving uniqueness. det A = 3 − 2 = 1, giving A⁻¹ = [[1, −1], [−2, 3]]. Both identity products can be checked directly. x = (11 − 8, −22 + 24)ᵀ = (3, 2)ᵀ, and A(3, 2)ᵀ = (11, 8)ᵀ. Rank is 2 and nullity is 0. All pairs are (α, β) = (4 − t, t) for real t. The null direction of the original mapping is (1, −1)ᵀ. Combining α + β = 4 and α − β = 2 gives α = 3, β = 1. The new measurement changes along that null direction, so it removes the previous ambiguity; the augmented coefficient matrix has rank 2. Initially subtracting the first equation gives 0.001y = 0.001, so y = x = 1. With the perturbation it gives 0.001y = 0.011, so y = 11 and x = −9. Generally y = 1 + δ/ε and x = 1 − δ/ε. At ε = 0, the two coefficient rows coincide: rank is 1, giving many solutions if their outcomes match and none if δ ≠ 0. If x and y represent nonnegative activities, the perturbed real solution is economically infeasible. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain existence using the column space and uniqueness using the null space. Demonstrate why a repeated observation does not supply new identification and why an invertible system can still amplify measurement error. Delayed check: reduce a new rectangular matrix, report the correct spaces and dimensions, and construct one attainable and one unattainable outcome when rank permits. Verify a general solution family in the original equations, not only in the reduced system. Further study and scope MIT's Column Space and Nullspace materials provide independent study of the geometry of exact systems. This chapter's resource and parameter cases are original and use no observed data. MIT's Independence, Basis and Dimension materials offer further practice. The next chapter adds orthogonal projections, eigenvalues, quadratic forms and singular values; later empirical instruction supplies the statistical assumptions needed to interpret fitted parameters."}, {"title": "02.06 · Eigenvalues, projections and quadratic forms", "url": "v02-c06.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Geometry reveals what equations conceal An inconsistent system can still have a well-defined closest fit. A matrix can stretch one direction strongly and another barely at all. A collection of risks can contain both a common movement and an offsetting movement. Orthogonal projections, eigenvalues and singular values describe these relationships in coordinates suited to the problem. This chapter builds on exact systems and Euclidean norms. It develops least squares as a geometric calculation, symmetric quadratic forms as measures of curvature and dispersion, and eigenvectors as directions preserved by a mapping. The examples are original and synthetic. A mathematical fit does not establish causality, and a covariance calculation does not supply a complete investment decision. Orthogonality and projection onto a line Vectors u and v are orthogonal when uᵀv = 0. For orthogonal vectors, ‖u + v‖₂² = ‖u‖₂² + ‖v‖₂² because the cross term vanishes. This is the vector form of Pythagoras. An orthonormal list consists of mutually orthogonal vectors each of norm 1. For a nonzero vector a, find the multiple ca closest to b. Expand ‖b − ca‖₂² = bᵀb − 2c aᵀb + c²aᵀa. Completing the square gives a unique minimizing coefficient c = (aᵀb)/(aᵀa). The projection is p = a(aᵀb)/(aᵀa), and the residual r = b − p is orthogonal to a. The same conclusion follows without a derivative: for any other multiple p + ta, b − (p + ta) = r − ta, whose squared norm is ‖r‖₂² + t²‖a‖₂². Every nonzero t increases the error. The best approximation is therefore characterized by residual orthogonality, not by an exact equation b = ca. If the columns of Q are orthonormal, the projection onto their span is QQᵀb. Its coordinates in that basis are Qᵀb. For a square orthogonal matrix Q, QᵀQ = QQᵀ = I; it preserves Euclidean lengths. For a rectangular matrix with orthonormal columns, QᵀQ = I still holds, but QQᵀ is generally a projection rather than the identity on the larger output space. Least squares and the normal equations For an m × n matrix A and observed b ∈ ℝᵐ, least squares minimizes ‖b − Ax‖₂² over real x. The fitted output Ax is constrained to lie in Col(A). A closest output exists because this finite-dimensional subspace admits an orthonormal basis and hence a projection. The fitted output is unique even when the coefficient x is not. At a minimizing x̂, the residual r = b − Ax̂ must be orthogonal to each column of A. Otherwise moving along that column with a sufficiently small coefficient of the appropriate sign would reduce squared error. Algebraically, the change for a candidate x̂ + th is −2t rᵀAh + t²‖Ah‖₂²; if the linear coefficient were nonzero, one sign of small t would reduce it. Thus Aᵀr = 0, giving the normal equations AᵀA x̂ = Aᵀb. Conversely, if these equations hold, then for any h, ‖b − A(x̂ + h)‖₂² = ‖r‖₂² + ‖Ah‖₂² ≥ ‖r‖₂². This proves global optimality, rather than merely identifying a stationary candidate. If A has independent columns, AᵀA is invertible: hᵀAᵀAh = ‖Ah‖₂² > 0 for nonzero h, so its null space is trivial. Then x̂ = (AᵀA)⁻¹Aᵀb is unique, and the projection matrix P = A(AᵀA)⁻¹Aᵀ gives the fitted output Pb. P is symmetric, Pᵀ = P, and idempotent, P² = P. The first follows from the symmetry of AᵀA and its inverse; the second cancels the middle AᵀA. Projecting a fitted output again leaves it unchanged. Residuals lie in the orthogonal complement of the column space, meaning the set of vectors orthogonal to every member of that space. If columns are dependent, several coefficients can generate the same fitted output; adding a null-space vector preserves it. A chosen minimum-length coefficient or a regularized coefficient is then an additional selection rule, not evidence that the observations identified the original parameters. Worked example 1 — Fit a line and check residual orthogonality Suppose three synthetic observations have inputs t = −1, 0, 1 and outputs b = (1, 2, 4)ᵀ. Fit bᵢ ≈ α + βtᵢ with equal squared-error weights. The design matrix is A = [[1, −1], [1, 0], [1, 1]]. Its columns represent an intercept and an input slope. AᵀA = [[3, 0], [0, 2]] and Aᵀb = (7, 3)ᵀ. Hence α̂ = 7/3 and β̂ = 3/2. Fitted values are (5/6, 7/3, 23/6)ᵀ, giving residuals (1/6, −1/3, 1/6)ᵀ. Their sum is zero and their dot product with the input column is −1/6 + 1/6 = 0, as the normal equations require. Residual squared norm is 1/36 + 1/9 + 1/36 = 1/6. For any alternative coefficient pair x̂ + h, the error increases by ‖Ah‖₂²; since A has rank 2, this increase is strictly positive for nonzero h. Thus the coefficient pair is uniquely optimal under the stated criterion. The projection matrix is P = (1/6)[[5, 2, −1], [2, 2, 2], [−1, 2, 5]]. Multiplication gives the same fitted values and P² = P. Negative entries do not invalidate a projection; its role is to preserve the subspace and enforce orthogonality, not to serve as a probability matrix. The fit summarizes these three points. Predicting at t = 2 gives 16/3 under the same line, but extrapolation quality needs evidence. Interpreting β̂ as a causal effect additionally requires a valid research design, not only a small residual or independent columns. Constructing orthonormal coordinates Gram–Schmidt converts an independent list v₁, …, vₖ into an orthonormal basis of the same span. Normalize v₁ to obtain q₁. Subtract from v₂ its projection q₁(q₁ᵀv₂), then normalize the nonzero remainder to obtain q₂. At step j subtract every previous component Σ i<j qᵢ(qᵢᵀvⱼ), where the sum runs over i < j, and normalize what remains. Independence ensures the remainder is nonzero in exact arithmetic; a zero remainder identifies dependence. Every new remainder is orthogonal to the preceding basis vectors because their inner products cancel. The span is preserved since the original vector equals the remainder plus previously represented components. For a full-column-rank A this construction gives A = QR, with orthonormal columns in Q and an upper-triangular square R. Least squares can then be solved by R x̂ = Qᵀb. This avoids forming AᵀA, which can worsen sensitivity to rounding. Practical algorithms use numerically stable orthogonal transformations or modified procedures; the conceptual construction here is not a claim that naive Gram–Schmidt is always a robust implementation. Eigenvalues and directions preserved by a mapping For a square matrix M, an eigenvector v ≠ 0 and eigenvalue λ satisfy Mv = λv. The zero vector is excluded because it would satisfy the equation for every λ. Multiplying v by a nonzero scalar yields another vector in the same eigendirection. For a given λ, the eigenspace is Null(M − λI). Eigenvalues satisfy det(M − λI) = 0. For a 2 × 2 matrix, the characteristic equation is λ² − tr(M)λ + det(M) = 0, where trace tr(M) is the sum of diagonal entries. Its roots sum to the trace and multiply to the determinant. After finding a root, solve the homogeneous system to obtain its eigenvectors and normalize when useful. Eigenvectors associated with distinct eigenvalues are independent. For two, if v₂ were a multiple of v₁, applying M would require its nonzero direction to have both eigenvalues, impossible. The general result follows by applying M − λI to a dependence and reducing the number of distinct eigenvalues inductively. If M has n independent eigenvectors, put them into the invertible matrix V and their eigenvalues on diagonal D. Then MV = VD, hence M = VDV⁻¹ and Mᵗ = VDᵗV⁻¹ for nonnegative integer t. The mapping becomes coordinatewise scaling in its eigenbasis. A real matrix need not have real eigenvalues or a complete eigenbasis. [[0, −1], [1, 0]] rotates vectors and has roots ±i, with i² = −1. [[1, 1], [0, 1]] has repeated eigenvalue 1 but only one independent eigenvector; its tth power is [[1, t], [0, 1]], not the identity. The real symmetric case has substantially stronger structure. Symmetry and quadratic forms A real symmetric matrix S satisfies Sᵀ = S. Its eigenvalues are real and it has an orthonormal eigenbasis: S = QΛQᵀ with Q orthogonal and Λ real diagonal. This is the spectral theorem for real symmetric matrices. We use that standard theorem here; its general existence proof can be developed through compactness and extrema on the unit sphere, tools treated more fully later. Orthogonality of distinct-eigenvalue directions follows directly. If Su = λu and Sv = μv, then uᵀSv = μuᵀv but also (Su)ᵀv = λuᵀv because S is symmetric. Thus (λ − μ)uᵀv = 0, so uᵀv = 0 when λ ≠ μ. Within a repeated-eigenvalue eigenspace, an orthonormal basis can be chosen using Gram–Schmidt. A quadratic form is xᵀSx. In eigen-coordinates z = Qᵀx it becomes Σᵢ λᵢzᵢ². S is positive definite if this is positive for every nonzero x, positive semidefinite if nonnegative for every x, negative definite if negative for every nonzero x, and indefinite if it takes both positive and negative values. These conditions correspond respectively to all eigenvalues positive, all nonnegative, all negative, or a mixture of positive and negative eigenvalues. For a nonsymmetric matrix M, xᵀMx equals xᵀ[(M + Mᵀ)/2]x, since its antisymmetric part contributes zero. Use the symmetric part for quadratic-form curvature. Positive diagonal entries alone do not prove positive definiteness: S = [[1, 2], [2, 1]] gives (1, −1)S(1, −1)ᵀ = −2 while its value on (1, 1)ᵀ is positive. For symmetric S = [[a, b], [b, c]], positive definiteness is equivalent to a > 0 and ac − b² > 0. Complete the square: xᵀSx = a(x₁ + (b/a)x₂)² + (c − b²/a)x₂² when a > 0. Both coefficients must be positive. Positive semidefiniteness instead requires a ≥ 0, c ≥ 0 and ac − b² ≥ 0, including boundary cases such as a = 0, which then forces b = 0. Worked example 2 — Common and offsetting risk directions Use the symmetric matrix Σ = [[4, 2], [2, 4]] as a synthetic return covariance matrix, measured in squared percentage points. One explicit zero-mean distribution producing it has four equally likely return pairs: (√6, √6), (−√6, −√6), (√2, −√2), and (−√2, √2), in percentage points. Expected squared returns are 4 and the expected product is 2. Σ(1, 1)ᵀ = 6(1, 1)ᵀ and Σ(1, −1)ᵀ = 2(1, −1)ᵀ. Normalize to q₊ = (1, 1)ᵀ/√2 and q₋ = (1, −1)ᵀ/√2. These orthonormal directions reveal a stronger common movement and a weaker offsetting movement. The decomposition is Σ = 6q₊q₊ᵀ + 2q₋q₋ᵀ. For any exposure vector w, wᵀΣw = 6(q₊ᵀw)² + 2(q₋ᵀw)². Both eigenvalues are positive, so no nonzero exposure has zero variance in this model. Equal fully invested weights w = (1/2, 1/2)ᵀ have variance 3 and standard deviation √3 percentage points. Holding only either asset has variance 4. Why is variance a quadratic form? For returns Rᵢ with means μᵢ, covariance Σᵢⱼ is E[(Rᵢ − μᵢ)(Rⱼ − μⱼ)]. Squaring the centered weighted return and using expectation's linearity gives Var(ΣᵢwᵢRᵢ) = ΣᵢΣⱼwᵢwⱼΣᵢⱼ = wᵀΣw, provided the expected squares are finite. Nonnegative variance establishes positive semidefiniteness of any valid covariance matrix. A small-eigenvalue direction can involve offsetting positions whose weights do not sum to 1. Eigen-directions therefore do not automatically solve a constrained portfolio choice. Expected returns, liability needs, permitted positions, estimation error and preferences enter later investment analysis. Dynamic interpretation in an eigenbasis For a linear recurrence xₜ₊₁ = Mxₜ, xₜ = Mᵗx₀. If M is diagonalizable, each eigen-coordinate evolves as its initial coefficient times λᵗ. An absolute eigenvalue less than 1 makes that component decay; greater than 1 amplifies it; equal to 1 requires more careful analysis. Negative eigenvalues alternate sign, and complex ones can represent rotations. For M = [[0.8, 0.1], [0.1, 0.8]], the common direction (1, 1)ᵀ has eigenvalue 0.9 and the difference direction (1, −1)ᵀ has eigenvalue 0.7. Both components decay, though at different rates. This describes the stipulated linear recurrence; nonlinear systems require a local linearization and additional conditions, developed in Chapter 11. Singular values and weakly measured directions Eigenvectors require a square mapping. A singular value decomposition works for any real m × n A of rank r: A = UᵣDᵣVᵣᵀ, where Uᵣ and Vᵣ have r orthonormal columns and Dᵣ has positive diagonal entries σ₁ ≥ … ≥ σᵣ > 0. These are the thin factors; full orthogonal bases can add zero directions when needed. To derive the construction, AᵀA is symmetric positive semidefinite, since xᵀAᵀAx = ‖Ax‖₂². Choose orthonormal eigenvectors vᵢ for its positive eigenvalues σᵢ² and set uᵢ = Avᵢ/σᵢ. Their pairwise inner products are vᵢᵀAᵀAvⱼ/(σᵢσⱼ), equal to 1 for i = j and 0 otherwise. Thus A maps a right singular direction vᵢ into σᵢ times the left direction uᵢ; vectors in its null space map to zero. A small σ means large input movement in that direction produces little output movement. For square invertible A, its Euclidean condition number is κ₂(A) = σ₁/σₙ. It is a worst-direction amplification ratio, not the error realized under every perturbation. Forming AᵀA squares this ratio, explaining why normal-equation calculations can lose precision compared with orthogonal-factor methods. The pseudoinverse A⁺ = VᵣDᵣ⁻¹Uᵣᵀ provides the least-squares coefficient with smallest Euclidean norm. It reciprocates positive singular values while assigning zero to unseen directions. Other coefficients can have the same fitted output by adding a null-space vector; choosing the smallest norm is a convention about inputs, not identification from outcomes. For A = [[3, 0], [0, 1], [0, 0]], singular values are 3 and 1, right directions are the two coordinate vectors, and left directions are the first two coordinates of ℝ³. With b = (6, 2, 5)ᵀ, the minimum-norm fit has x = (2, 2)ᵀ, fitted output (6, 2, 0)ᵀ and residual (0, 0, 5)ᵀ. The third output direction is unattainable; no inverse manipulation can eliminate that mismatch. Exercises Project b = (3, 0)ᵀ onto the span of a = (1, 2)ᵀ. Compute the coefficient, projection, residual and residual squared norm. Verify orthogonality and the decomposition of ‖b‖₂². Fit y ≈ α + βt by equal-weight least squares to t = 0, 1, 2 and y = 1, 2, 2. Compute AᵀA, Aᵀy, coefficients, fitted values and residuals. Check both orthogonality conditions and the residual sum of squares. Find eigenvalues and orthonormal eigenvectors of S = [[3, 1], [1, 3]]. Reconstruct S from its spectral factors and evaluate xᵀSx at x = (1, −1)ᵀ in both original and eigen-coordinates. Classify [[2, 1], [1, 2]], [[1, 1], [1, 1]], [[1, 2], [2, 1]], and [[−2, 0], [0, −1]] by definiteness. For the semidefinite matrix give a nonzero zero-value vector; for the indefinite matrix give positive- and negative-value vectors. Using Σ = [[4, 2], [2, 4]], compute variance for weights (3/4, 1/4)ᵀ and (1, −1)ᵀ. Compare with equal fully invested weights and explain why the offsetting exposure is not another fully invested long-only allocation. For xₜ₊₁ = [[0.8, 0.1], [0.1, 0.8]]xₜ and x₀ = (3, 1)ᵀ, derive xₜ in common and difference directions. Compute x₁ and x₂ and determine the limit. Explain why a particular coordinate can initially rise despite decay in every eigen-coordinate. For A = [[3, 0], [0, 0.01]], give its singular values and condition number. Solve Ax = (3, 0.01)ᵀ and then Ax = (3, 0.02)ᵀ. Describe the weakly measured direction and why exact invertibility does not guarantee small input errors. Apply Gram–Schmidt to v₁ = (1, 1, 0)ᵀ and v₂ = (0, 1, 1)ᵀ. Give the two orthonormal vectors, check their inner products, and write a thin QR factorization for A = [v₁ v₂]. Solutions and geometric checks aᵀb = 3 and aᵀa = 5, so c = 3/5. Projection is (3/5, 6/5)ᵀ and residual is (12/5, −6/5)ᵀ. Their dot product with a is 12/5 − 12/5 = 0. Residual squared norm is 180/25 = 36/5; projection squared norm is 45/25 = 9/5. Their sum is 9 = ‖b‖₂². A = [[1, 0], [1, 1], [1, 2]], AᵀA = [[3, 3], [3, 5]], Aᵀy = (5, 6)ᵀ. The inverse Gram matrix is (1/6)[[5, −3], [−3, 3]], giving α̂ = 7/6 and β̂ = 1/2. Fitted values are (7/6, 5/3, 13/6)ᵀ; residuals are (−1/6, 1/3, −1/6)ᵀ. They sum to zero and their weighted sum with t is 1/3 − 2/6 = 0. Squared residual norm is 1/6. These are fitting conditions, not causal identification. Eigenvalues are 4 and 2, with q₊ = (1, 1)ᵀ/√2 and q₋ = (1, −1)ᵀ/√2. S = 4q₊q₊ᵀ + 2q₋q₋ᵀ. For x = (1, −1)ᵀ, q₊ᵀx = 0 and q₋ᵀx = √2, so the quadratic value is 2 × 2 = 4. Directly Sx = (2, −2)ᵀ and xᵀSx = 4. The first is positive definite with eigenvalues 3 and 1. The second is positive semidefinite with eigenvalues 2 and 0; x = (1, −1)ᵀ gives zero. The third is indefinite with eigenvalues 3 and −1; (1, 1)ᵀ gives 6 and (1, −1)ᵀ gives −2. The fourth is negative definite with eigenvalues −2 and −1. Zero eigenvalues distinguish semidefinite from definite behavior. Variance for (3/4, 1/4)ᵀ is 4(9/16) + 4(3/16) + 4(1/16) = 13/4 = 3.25, versus 3 for equal weights. For (1, −1)ᵀ it is 4 − 4 + 4 = 4, in squared percentage points. The latter weights sum to zero and include a negative exposure, so they are not a fully invested long-only portfolio. Comparing the numbers without matching exposure constraints would misstate the decision. x₀ = 2(1, 1)ᵀ + (1, −1)ᵀ, giving xₜ = 2(0.9)ᵗ(1, 1)ᵀ + (0.7)ᵗ(1, −1)ᵀ. x₁ = (2.5, 1.1)ᵀ and x₂ = (2.11, 1.13)ᵀ. Both eigen-coefficients tend to zero, so the vector limit is zero. The second original coordinate subtracts the faster-decaying difference component; that subtraction initially shrinks fast enough to let the coordinate rise. The singular values are 3 and 0.01 with coordinate directions on each side; κ₂ = 300. Initial x is (1, 1)ᵀ and perturbed x is (1, 2)ᵀ. The second output changes by only 0.01 while its inferred input changes by 1. The second direction is weakly measured because the mapping scales it down by 0.01. q₁ = (1, 1, 0)ᵀ/√2. Subtract q₁(q₁ᵀv₂) = (1/2, 1/2, 0)ᵀ from v₂, leaving (−1/2, 1/2, 1)ᵀ of norm √(3/2). Thus q₂ = (−1, 1, 2)ᵀ/√6. Both have unit norm and their dot product is zero. With Q = [q₁ q₂], R = [[√2, 1/√2], [0, √(3/2)]], the product QR reconstructs both original columns. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: derive the normal equations through residual geometry and prove their sufficiency. Explain the distinct roles of eigenvalues, singular values and rank; demonstrate why fitting, identification and causal interpretation require different evidence. Delayed check: solve a fresh projection and least-squares problem, classify a symmetric quadratic form with an explicit witness for each failed sign condition, and describe one weak singular direction. Reconstruct an eigenbasis recurrence and verify its first two steps directly. Further study and scope MIT's Projection Matrices and Least Squares materials provide further geometric study. The fitted datasets here are original, and the exercise solutions do not reuse that course's problem bank. MIT's Symmetric Matrices and Positive Definiteness materials offer further study of the spectral theorem and quadratic geometry. MIT's Singular Value Decomposition materials provide deeper matrix-factorization instruction. The next chapters combine this geometry with multivariable derivatives and constrained decisions; statistical and financial applications later add their own empirical assumptions."}, {"title": "02.07 · Multivariable calculus", "url": "curriculum.html#v02-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Calculate gradients, Hessians and total derivatives in economic models. Partial derivatives Jacobian Hessian multivariate chain rule implicit-function theorem"}, {"title": "02.08 · Unconstrained and constrained optimization", "url": "curriculum.html#v02-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve constrained decisions and verify sufficient conditions rather than relying only on first-order conditions. Concavity Lagrange multipliers inequality constraints KKT corner solutions envelopes"}, {"title": "02.09 · Convexity, duality and comparative statics", "url": "curriculum.html#v02-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Use curvature and duality to establish optimization results and parameter responses. Convex sets separating intuition value functions dual problems sensitivity comparative statics"}, {"title": "02.10 · Real analysis and convergence", "url": "curriculum.html#v02-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Prove convergence results and recognize when interchange of limits requires justification. Completeness compactness subsequences uniform convergence contraction mappings"}, {"title": "02.11 · Difference and differential equations", "url": "curriculum.html#v02-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve simple dynamic systems and classify local stability. First-order recursions systems steady states eigenvalue stability differential equations"}, {"title": "02.12 · Numerical methods and dynamic foundations", "url": "curriculum.html#v02-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Implement root finding and approximation with explicit accuracy criteria. Bisection Newton methods interpolation numerical integration conditioning Bellman equation introduction"}, {"title": "03.01 · Probability spaces and counting", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Build coherent event probabilities and explain independence assumptions. Events axioms combinatorics independence inclusion-exclusion sampling with replacement"}, {"title": "03.02 · Conditional probability and Bayes", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Update beliefs correctly and diagnose base-rate errors. Conditional events Bayes rule total probability odds likelihood ratios"}, {"title": "03.03 · Random variables and distributions", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Work with discrete and continuous distributions and their support. PMF PDF CDF Bernoulli binomial Poisson normal exponential"}, {"title": "03.04 · Expectation, variance and covariance", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compute moments and explain diversification using joint distributions. Expected value variance covariance correlation transformations total expectation"}, {"title": "03.05 · Joint distributions and conditional expectation", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive conditional moments and identify dependence beyond correlation. Joint and marginal distributions conditioning total variance iterated expectations"}, {"title": "03.06 · Large samples and convergence", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Apply laws of large numbers and central-limit reasoning with stated conditions. Convergence modes LLN CLT heavy tails dependence finite-sample caveats"}, {"title": "03.07 · Sampling and estimation", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Construct estimators and compare bias, variance and consistency. Sampling designs point estimation standard errors maximum likelihood introduction bootstrap"}, {"title": "03.08 · Confidence intervals and hypothesis tests", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Interpret intervals and tests without confusing statistical and economic significance. Null and alternatives test statistics size power p-values multiple testing"}, {"title": "03.09 · Bayesian inference and decisions", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Combine likelihoods, priors and losses to make transparent decisions. Posterior distributions conjugacy credible intervals predictive distributions sensitivity to priors"}, {"title": "03.10 · Expected utility and risk preferences", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve decisions under uncertainty and identify the limits of expected-utility models. Utility certainty equivalents risk premiums Jensen risk aversion ambiguity"}, {"title": "03.11 · Information, forecasting and scoring", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate predictions and the value of additional information using proper criteria. Calibration proper scoring conditional forecasts expected value of information loss functions"}, {"title": "03.12 · Measure and stochastic foundations", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Explain the formal probability tools needed for graduate econometrics and finance. Sigma-algebras measurability Lebesgue integration dominated convergence conditional expectation martingale introduction"}, {"title": "04.01 · Reproducible work and data provenance", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Create a workflow another analyst can reproduce from the supplied inputs. Files environments README version control data dictionaries vintages licenses"}, {"title": "04.02 · Spreadsheets and financial models", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Build auditable spreadsheet calculations and detect hidden modeling errors. Cell references formulas cash-flow timelines reconciliation sensitivity tables audit checks"}, {"title": "04.03 · Python fundamentals for analysis", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Write clear executable programs for economic calculations. Types loops functions exceptions assertions files standard library"}, {"title": "04.04 · Arrays, tables and SQL", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Transform structured data without corrupting units, keys or populations. Arrays joins aggregation SQL missing values dates duplicate keys"}, {"title": "04.05 · Visualization and descriptive evidence", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Choose and interpret figures that expose rather than conceal evidence. Distributions time series scatterplots scales uncertainty misleading comparisons"}, {"title": "04.06 · Simulation and Monte Carlo", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Estimate quantities by simulation and assess Monte Carlo error. Random seeds simulation design sampling uncertainty variance reduction reproducibility"}, {"title": "04.07 · Regression as projection", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive ordinary least squares and interpret fitted values and residuals. Projection normal equations coefficients residuals fit extrapolation"}, {"title": "04.08 · Identification and confounding", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Distinguish descriptive associations from identified causal effects. Potential outcomes introduction selection confounding controls bad controls identification"}, {"title": "04.09 · Prediction and model validation", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate models using splits that respect economic timing and information availability. Train-validation-test time splits leakage overfitting baseline comparisons losses"}, {"title": "04.10 · Time-series and panel data basics", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Prepare longitudinal data and identify dependence and aggregation pitfalls. Lags trends stationarity intuition panel keys survivorship frequencies deflation"}, {"title": "04.11 · Computational reliability and numerical audits", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Verify outputs through invariants, simple cases and independent calculations. Assertions tests edge cases units precision numerical conditioning peer reproduction"}, {"title": "04.12 · Empirical integration and replication", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Reproduce a small empirical result and document departures from the original analysis. Source retrieval reconstruction robustness replication report limits of inference"}, {"title": "05.01 · Preferences and consumer choice", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive demands and identify corner solutions under a budget constraint. Preferences utility indifference curves budget sets Marshallian demand revealed preference"}, {"title": "05.02 · Demand, duality and welfare", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Decompose price responses and measure welfare with correct income concepts. Hicksian demand expenditure function Slutsky indirect utility compensating variation"}, {"title": "05.03 · Production, costs and firm choice", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive cost functions and output/input choices from technology. Production sets marginal products cost minimization fixed and sunk cost supply"}, {"title": "05.04 · Competitive equilibrium and surplus", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve partial equilibrium and distinguish efficiency from distribution. Market clearing consumer and producer surplus elasticity entry tax incidence"}, {"title": "05.05 · General equilibrium and welfare", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze exchange and production interactions across markets. Edgeworth box Walras law Pareto efficiency welfare theorems missing markets"}, {"title": "05.06 · Monopoly, oligopoly and pricing", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve market-power models and compare price and quantity competition. Monopoly markup price discrimination Cournot Bertrand entry barriers"}, {"title": "05.07 · Game theory and strategic behavior", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Identify equilibria and credible strategies in static and dynamic games. Dominance Nash mixed strategies extensive form backward induction commitment"}, {"title": "05.08 · Information, signaling and selection", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Explain how asymmetric information changes trade and incentives. Adverse selection signaling screening pooling separating equilibria lemons"}, {"title": "05.09 · Contracts, incentives and organizations", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve simple principal-agent problems and evaluate incentive trade-offs. Moral hazard participation incentive compatibility monitoring multitasking firm boundaries"}, {"title": "05.10 · Externalities and public goods", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare corrective instruments under informational and institutional constraints. External costs Pigouvian taxes public goods free riding Coase conditions congestion"}, {"title": "05.11 · Auctions, matching and market design", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze allocation rules and incentive properties in designed markets. Auction formats revenue equivalence conditions matching stability strategy-proofness"}, {"title": "05.12 · Behavioral economics and microeconomic evidence", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate departures from standard choice and the evidence needed to support interventions. Biases reference dependence time inconsistency experiments welfare interpretation replication"}, {"title": "06.01 · National accounts and macro measurement", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Reconcile output, expenditure and income measures and explain what GDP omits. GDP value added expenditure identity deflators real income stocks versus flows"}, {"title": "06.02 · Growth and the Solow model", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive steady states and distinguish level effects from sustained growth. Capital accumulation depreciation population technology convergence growth accounting"}, {"title": "06.03 · Innovation, human capital and institutions", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare mechanisms of long-run development with appropriate evidence. Endogenous growth human capital diffusion institutions geography identification"}, {"title": "06.04 · Consumption, saving and investment", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive intertemporal decisions and connect household and firm behavior to aggregates. Permanent income life cycle liquidity constraints investment adjustment costs expectations"}, {"title": "06.05 · Labor markets and unemployment", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Explain employment dynamics using search, incentives and institutional mechanisms. Participation matching unemployment flows wage setting efficiency wages hysteresis"}, {"title": "06.06 · Money, banking and monetary transmission", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Trace monetary-policy mechanisms without relying on a mechanical deposit multiplier. Money demand policy rates reserves bank balance sheets credit transmission"}, {"title": "06.07 · Inflation, expectations and nominal rigidity", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Explain inflation dynamics and distinguish supply from demand disturbances. Quantity theory conditions Phillips curves expectations price stickiness shocks"}, {"title": "06.08 · Business cycles and stabilization models", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare short-run models and identify their assumptions and predictions. IS-LM aggregate demand-supply RBC introduction New Keynesian introduction stabilization"}, {"title": "06.09 · Fiscal policy and public debt", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive debt dynamics and evaluate fiscal trade-offs with explicit assumptions. Budget constraint debt-to-GDP multipliers automatic stabilizers fiscal space distribution"}, {"title": "06.10 · Trade and comparative advantage", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive gains and distributional effects from trade models. Ricardian factor endowments scale firms tariffs terms of trade"}, {"title": "06.11 · Exchange rates and external balances", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Connect international accounts, exchange-rate mechanisms and policy regimes. Balance of payments net foreign assets exchange rates PPP interest parity capital flows"}, {"title": "06.12 · Crises, policy regimes and macroeconomic judgment", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare crisis mechanisms and evaluate a country case without claiming certainty from one model. Financial crises sudden stops sovereign distress historical comparison scenario analysis"}, {"title": "07.01 · Transactions and double-entry accounting", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Record transactions and preserve the accounting equation. Debits credits journals accruals recognition transaction analysis"}, {"title": "07.02 · Balance sheet, income statement and cash flow", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Explain and reconcile the three principal statements. Assets liabilities equity revenues expenses operating-investing-financing cash flows"}, {"title": "07.03 · Revenue, working capital and operating cycles", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Model operating cash needs and distinguish reported sales from cash collection. Receivables inventory payables revenue timing cash conversion cycle allowances"}, {"title": "07.04 · Long-lived assets and investment accounting", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Track asset investment and distinguish accounting expense from economic depreciation. Capitalization depreciation amortization impairment disposals investment assets"}, {"title": "07.05 · Debt, leases and financing obligations", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze financing obligations and their effects on performance and cash flow. Borrowing interest leases covenants commitments effective interest disclosure"}, {"title": "07.06 · Equity, consolidation and ownership", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Trace equity claims and distinguish ownership, control and reported group results. Share capital retained earnings dilution minority interests consolidation concepts buybacks"}, {"title": "07.07 · Taxes, accruals and earnings quality", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Identify timing differences and assess sustainability of reported earnings. Current and deferred tax concepts accruals provisions one-offs manipulation incentives"}, {"title": "07.08 · Managerial accounting and cost decisions", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Use contribution margins and relevant costs for operational decisions. Cost behavior break-even allocation activity-based costing make-or-buy capacity"}, {"title": "07.09 · Financial ratios and business diagnosis", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Interpret ratios in context and explain accounting comparability limitations. Liquidity leverage margins turnover ROIC DuPont peer selection"}, {"title": "07.10 · Three-statement forecasting", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Build linked forecasts with a transparent financing policy. Revenue drivers operating expenses working capital capex financing cash balancing"}, {"title": "07.11 · Accounting standards, disclosure and comparability", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Identify where accounting-policy differences require adjustment and dated source verification. IFRS and US GAAP concepts disclosure estimates restatements segment reporting jurisdiction"}, {"title": "07.12 · Forensic analysis and accounting integration", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Diagnose a business using reconciled accounts and explicit alternative explanations. Cash-versus-profit gaps covenant risk red flags normalization business model economics"}, {"title": "08.01 · Value creation and capital budgeting", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate incremental project cash flows and reconcile NPV with decision criteria. NPV IRR opportunity costs sunk costs cash flow mutually exclusive projects"}, {"title": "08.02 · Free cash flow and discounting", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive cash flows to the firm and equity and choose consistent discount rates. FCFF FCFE reinvestment terminal value nominal-real consistency currency consistency"}, {"title": "08.03 · Valuation models and terminal assumptions", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Value an operating business using explicit growth and reinvestment economics. DCF perpetuities value drivers ROIC growth fade terminal constraints"}, {"title": "08.04 · Risk, cost of capital and WACC", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Estimate financing costs and explain when WACC is inappropriate. Cost of equity beta introduction debt costs market weights leverage project risk"}, {"title": "08.05 · Capital structure and financing frictions", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare financing policies under taxes, distress and incentive problems. Modigliani-Miller assumptions trade-off pecking order agency debt overhang"}, {"title": "08.06 · Payout, liquidity and financial policy", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Choose payout and liquidity policies consistent with investment and constraints. Dividends buybacks retained cash signaling working capital financial flexibility"}, {"title": "08.07 · Relative valuation and market evidence", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Use comparable valuations with economically meaningful denominators and peer choices. Enterprise-equity bridge multiples normalization comparability cycle effects triangulation"}, {"title": "08.08 · Real options and investment timing", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Value flexibility and identify where static NPV understates contingent choices. Delay expand abandon staged investment decision trees information value"}, {"title": "08.09 · Mergers, acquisitions and control", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate acquisition economics and distinguish synergy from wealth transfers. Purchase price synergy integration control premiums financing dilution versus value"}, {"title": "08.10 · Private equity, venture capital and entrepreneurship", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze illiquid ownership structures and financing incentives. LBO venture rounds cap tables preferred claims exits fund economics selection"}, {"title": "08.11 · Distress, restructuring and governance", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare restructuring alternatives and their incentive and valuation consequences. Insolvency concepts priority recovery refinancing governance stakeholder conflicts"}, {"title": "08.12 · Corporate finance integration", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend a financing and investment plan under alternative operating and market conditions. Integrated valuation capital allocation downside covenants scenario consistency board memo"}, {"title": "09.01 · Returns, payoffs and investment opportunity sets", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Measure investment outcomes with correct timing, distributions and benchmark conventions. Simple-log returns total return compounding cash flows gross-net returns opportunity sets"}, {"title": "09.02 · Portfolio mathematics and diversification", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compute portfolio risk and explain the role of covariance and concentration. Mean covariance matrix correlations diversification estimation error leverage"}, {"title": "09.03 · Mean-variance optimization and allocation", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive efficient portfolios and assess sensitivity to estimated inputs. Efficient frontier minimum variance tangency constraints optimization shrinkage"}, {"title": "09.04 · CAPM and equilibrium risk pricing", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive the CAPM mechanism and distinguish its theoretical assumptions from empirical evidence. Market portfolio beta security market line alpha equilibrium testing limitations"}, {"title": "09.05 · Arbitrage, state prices and discount factors", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Price contingent payoffs using no-arbitrage and stochastic discount-factor reasoning. Replication state prices pricing kernel completeness fundamental pricing equation"}, {"title": "09.06 · Factor models and empirical asset pricing", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Estimate factor exposures and distinguish explanatory fits from investable premiums. APT multifactor models cross-sectional pricing factor construction omitted factors"}, {"title": "09.07 · Market efficiency, anomalies and behavior", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate apparent predictability with information, risk and implementation costs in view. Efficiency forms joint hypothesis anomalies behavioral mechanisms limits to arbitrage"}, {"title": "09.08 · Active management and performance evaluation", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Assess whether performance reflects skill, risk, selection or luck. Benchmark choice alpha attribution information ratio persistence fees survivorship"}, {"title": "09.09 · Strategic and dynamic asset allocation", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Design allocation policies across horizons and investor constraints. Lifecycle liabilities hedging demand rebalancing tactical decisions estimation risk"}, {"title": "09.10 · Alternative assets and illiquidity", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare nontraditional investments with transparent valuation and liquidity assumptions. Real estate private markets commodities hedge funds appraisal smoothing lockups"}, {"title": "09.11 · Execution, trading costs and market microstructure", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate execution and liquidity effects on feasible investment strategies. Bid-ask spread impact order types information execution benchmarks capacity"}, {"title": "09.12 · Investment policy and portfolio integration", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend a mandate and portfolio using economic objectives and robust evidence. IPS governance benchmarks risk budget costs stress scenarios reporting"}, {"title": "10.01 · Bond cash flows and yield curves", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Price cash flows with spot discount factors and distinguish yield conventions. Coupon schedules clean-dirty price discount curves YTM spot-forward rates day-count assumptions"}, {"title": "10.02 · Duration, convexity and immunization", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive rate sensitivity and construct hedges with explicit residual risks. Macaulay-modified duration convexity key rates immunization nonparallel shifts"}, {"title": "10.03 · Term-structure models and rate risk", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare curve models and distinguish physical from risk-neutral dynamics. Expectations term premiums short-rate models no-arbitrage calibration curve scenarios"}, {"title": "10.04 · Credit risk and recovery", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Value defaultable claims and separate hazard, recovery and liquidity assumptions. Credit spreads structural models intensity recovery transition correlation"}, {"title": "10.05 · Forwards, futures and swaps", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive contract values and explain financing, collateral and basis effects. Cost of carry forward-futures distinction swaps collateral basis margin"}, {"title": "10.06 · Options and binomial replication", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Construct replicating portfolios and price options through state-contingent payoffs. Payoffs put-call parity binomial tree risk-neutral probability American exercise"}, {"title": "10.07 · Brownian motion and Ito calculus", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive stochastic differentials and explain quadratic variation. Filtrations Brownian motion stochastic integral Ito lemma SDE drift-diffusion"}, {"title": "10.08 · Black-Scholes and risk-neutral valuation", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive the pricing equation and distinguish a model's assumptions from market conventions. Replication PDE risk-neutral measure Black-Scholes formula dividends boundaries"}, {"title": "10.09 · Greeks, volatility and hedging", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze hedge sensitivities and the implications of changing volatility assumptions. Delta gamma vega theta implied volatility smiles discrete hedging"}, {"title": "10.10 · Numerical pricing and Monte Carlo", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Implement pricing methods with convergence and error diagnostics. Trees finite differences simulation variance reduction early exercise numerical checks"}, {"title": "10.11 · Incomplete markets and advanced derivatives", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Explain why some contingent claims cannot be uniquely priced by replication. Incompleteness stochastic volatility jumps exotic payoffs credit derivatives bounds"}, {"title": "10.12 · Quantitative model governance", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Validate pricing and risk models against independent benchmarks and failure scenarios. Calibration model risk implementation errors limitations validation controlled use"}, {"title": "11.01 · Financial intermediation and bank economics", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Explain the economic functions and fragilities of financial intermediaries. Screening monitoring maturity transformation deposits bank income capital"}, {"title": "11.02 · Bank balance sheets, capital and liquidity", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Reconcile bank accounts and distinguish liquidity shortages from insolvency. Assets funding equity capital ratios concepts liquidity buffers accounting-economic value"}, {"title": "11.03 · Credit analysis and lending decisions", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate creditworthiness using cash flow, incentives and downside protection. Debt service leverage covenants collateral repayment sources credit underwriting"}, {"title": "11.04 · Market risk and portfolio loss", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare risk measures and state their information and distributional limitations. VaR expected shortfall scenarios tails dependence horizon backtesting"}, {"title": "11.05 · Liquidity, funding and asset-liability management", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze funding gaps and contingent cash needs across scenarios. Liquidity ladders ALM rollover collateral margin maturity mismatch"}, {"title": "11.06 · Operational, legal and model risk", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Design controls around human, operational and modeling failure mechanisms. Process controls cyber concepts fraud legal uncertainty model validation incident learning"}, {"title": "11.07 · Stress testing and risk aggregation", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Build internally consistent stress scenarios without double counting exposures. Scenario design nonlinear losses contagion concentration reverse stress aggregation"}, {"title": "11.08 · Insurance, pensions and household finance", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate long-horizon liabilities and intermediary incentives. Insurance pooling adverse selection pensions longevity guarantees household credit"}, {"title": "11.09 · Currency, sovereign and cross-border risk", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze international claims with explicit currency and jurisdiction assumptions. FX exposure hedging sovereign debt capital flows country risk convertibility"}, {"title": "11.10 · Financial regulation and incentives", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate regulatory purposes and trade-offs using dated jurisdiction-specific sources. Prudential regulation resolution conduct systemic externalities arbitrage regulatory capture"}, {"title": "11.11 · Financial crises and systemic mechanisms", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Trace balance-sheet feedback, runs and contagion in a crisis model. Runs fire sales leverage cycles networks lender of last resort resolution"}, {"title": "11.12 · Risk governance and institutional integration", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend an institution's risk appetite and response to a combined stress scenario. Risk limits board oversight escalation incentives decision logs contingency plans"}, {"title": "12.01 · Identification, estimands and asymptotic tools", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Separate what data identify from what an estimator computes and justify limiting arguments. Estimands identification consistency asymptotic normality delta method stochastic orders"}, {"title": "12.02 · Linear models and robust inference", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive linear estimators and choose inference appropriate to dependence and heterogeneity. OLS GLS heteroskedasticity clustering serial correlation finite-sample concerns"}, {"title": "12.03 · Instrumental variables and GMM", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive moment-based estimators and evaluate instrument relevance and validity. IV 2SLS weak instruments exclusion GMM overidentification local effects"}, {"title": "12.04 · Likelihood and nonlinear models", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Estimate nonlinear models and distinguish functional-form identification from causal identification. MLE score information binary response count selection marginal effects"}, {"title": "12.05 · Panel data and longitudinal identification", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate within-unit identification and dynamic-panel assumptions. Fixed-random effects correlated heterogeneity dynamic panels attrition dependence"}, {"title": "12.06 · Experiments and causal inference", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Design and evaluate interventions with appropriate estimands and assignment mechanisms. Potential outcomes randomization compliance interference attrition power randomization inference"}, {"title": "12.07 · Difference-in-differences and event studies", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend counterfactual trends and handle treatment timing and heterogeneous effects. Parallel trends anticipation staggered adoption event studies weighting sensitivity"}, {"title": "12.08 · Regression discontinuity and synthetic controls", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate local and comparative designs with transparent support and validity conditions. Continuity manipulation bandwidth fuzzy RD donor pools synthetic control placebo analysis"}, {"title": "12.09 · Time series, forecasting and financial econometrics", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze persistent and dependent economic series with model-specific inference. ARMA VAR unit roots cointegration volatility GARCH forecast evaluation"}, {"title": "12.10 · Structural estimation and economic mechanisms", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Connect economic models to data and distinguish calibration from identified estimation. Structural parameters moments simulation equilibrium indirect inference sensitivity"}, {"title": "12.11 · Bayesian, high-dimensional and machine-learning methods", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Apply modern methods while preserving valid inference and honest model evaluation. Bayesian computation regularization cross-fitting double robustness causal ML multiple testing"}, {"title": "12.12 · Econometric research audit", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Independently audit a complete empirical paper and defend methodological choices. Identification robustness inference specification search reproducibility external validity"}, {"title": "13.01 · Preference representation and choice theory", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Establish representation and choice results under precise axioms. Preferences continuity utility representation revealed preference rationalizability"}, {"title": "13.02 · Consumer and producer duality", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive duality identities and characterize economically admissible demand and cost functions. Expenditure profit integrability Slutsky symmetry homogeneity envelope identities"}, {"title": "13.03 · Uncertainty and information", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze risk attitudes and the economic value of information formally. Expected utility stochastic dominance risk aversion Bayesian information Blackwell ordering"}, {"title": "13.04 · General equilibrium and existence", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Explain and apply equilibrium existence arguments and their assumptions. Exchange-production economies fixed points excess demand Walras law existence uniqueness limits"}, {"title": "13.05 · Welfare, aggregation and social choice", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Prove welfare results and assess aggregation and distributional restrictions. Welfare theorems aggregation social welfare impossibility collective choice"}, {"title": "13.06 · Static games and refinements", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Establish strategic equilibria and analyze equilibrium selection. Mixed-strategy existence Bayesian games best responses refinements correlated equilibrium"}, {"title": "13.07 · Dynamic and repeated games", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze sequential incentives and intertemporal cooperation with credible strategies. Subgame perfection sequential equilibrium repeated games reputation folk-theorem conditions"}, {"title": "13.08 · Information economics and screening", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve screening and signaling models with participation and incentive constraints. Single crossing menus adverse selection signaling equilibrium selection"}, {"title": "13.09 · Contracts and dynamic incentives", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive optimal contracts under hidden action and limited commitment. Principal-agent sufficient statistics limited liability dynamic contracts relational incentives"}, {"title": "13.10 · Mechanism design and auctions", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive incentive-compatible allocation and payment rules with explicit informational assumptions. Revelation principle implementation optimal auctions incentive compatibility transfers"}, {"title": "13.11 · Matching, networks and market design", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze stable allocations and strategic interactions on markets and networks. Matching stability assignment network games externalities design constraints"}, {"title": "13.12 · Theory synthesis and research critique", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate a theoretical contribution through proofs, counterexamples and economic relevance. Model choice theorem scope proof checking robustness interpretation theoretical referee report"}, {"title": "14.01 · Dynamic optimization and recursive methods", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive Bellman and Euler conditions and establish valid numerical solutions. Bellman principle contraction Euler equations transversality policy iteration value iteration"}, {"title": "14.02 · Stochastic growth and real business cycles", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve a stochastic growth model and evaluate its empirical mechanisms. Planner competitive equilibrium shocks labor perturbation calibration moments"}, {"title": "14.03 · Incomplete markets and heterogeneous agents", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze distributional state variables and precautionary decisions in incomplete markets. Aiyagari Huggett borrowing constraints distributions aggregation numerical methods"}, {"title": "14.04 · Search, matching and labor dynamics", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive dynamic labor-market equilibria and evaluate their implications. Matching functions bargaining vacancy creation unemployment dynamics shocks"}, {"title": "14.05 · New Keynesian models and monetary policy", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive a nominal-rigidity model and compare policy rules under explicit approximations. Household-firm problems Calvo NK Phillips curve dynamic IS Taylor rules determinacy"}, {"title": "14.06 · Fiscal policy and sovereign debt", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze dynamic public finance and sovereign default mechanisms. Government constraints fiscal-monetary interaction default sustainability redistribution"}, {"title": "14.07 · Consumption-based asset pricing", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive stochastic discount factors and evaluate major pricing puzzles. Euler equation consumption risk equity premium risk-free rate habits long-run risks"}, {"title": "14.08 · Investment, debt and financial frictions", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive how financing constraints alter investment and aggregate dynamics. q theory agency collateral debt overhang financial accelerator balance sheets"}, {"title": "14.09 · Intermediaries, liquidity and leverage cycles", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze financial-sector amplification and endogenous risk. Intermediary capital leverage liquidity runs risk-bearing capacity feedback"}, {"title": "14.10 · International macro-finance", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve open-economy dynamic models with financial and exchange-rate mechanisms. Risk sharing sudden stops sovereign risk currencies global financial conditions"}, {"title": "14.11 · Quantitative solution and model evaluation", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Assess macroeconomic models through transparent estimation and numerical diagnostics. Perturbation global methods likelihood-moment methods identification validation sensitivity"}, {"title": "14.12 · Macro-finance research frontier", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Critically compare frontier contributions and develop a tractable research question. Heterogeneity financial networks monetary transmission intermediary pricing current literature protocol"}, {"title": "15.01 · Public economics and taxation", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze tax and spending decisions with efficiency and distribution in view. Tax incidence optimal taxation public goods social insurance welfare weights"}, {"title": "15.02 · Labor economics and human capital", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate wage, employment and education mechanisms using credible designs. Human capital returns to education labor supply-demand discrimination mobility"}, {"title": "15.03 · Industrial organization and competition", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Assess market conduct and competitive policy using models and evidence. Demand estimation entry pricing merger analysis platforms market definition"}, {"title": "15.04 · Development, poverty and structural change", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare poverty-reduction mechanisms and their institutional constraints. Poverty measurement credit health education structural transformation field experiments"}, {"title": "15.05 · International trade and global production", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze firm and worker responses to trade and production networks. Heterogeneous firms trade costs global value chains tariffs distribution adjustment"}, {"title": "15.06 · Political economy and institutions", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze collective decisions and institutional incentives with defensible identification. Voting accountability lobbying bureaucracy state capacity capture"}, {"title": "15.07 · Economic history and historical inference", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Use historical evidence to distinguish mechanisms and avoid anachronistic causal claims. Industrialization institutions crises historical data natural experiments comparative inference"}, {"title": "15.08 · Behavioral and experimental economics", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Design and critique evidence about nonstandard preferences and beliefs. Behavioral models experiments replication external validity welfare intervention"}, {"title": "15.09 · Environmental, energy and climate economics", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate externalities and long-horizon choices under uncertainty. Carbon pricing discounting resource economics damages energy markets distribution"}, {"title": "15.10 · Health, education and demographic economics", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Assess service provision and demographic change through incentives and evidence. Insurance health markets education production fertility aging public policy"}, {"title": "15.11 · Urban economics, housing and inequality", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze spatial decisions and distribution with equilibrium feedback. Location housing supply amenities sorting migration inequality mobility"}, {"title": "15.12 · Policy synthesis and institutional feasibility", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend an implementable policy with evidence, uncertainty and distributional consequences. Cost-benefit political constraints administrative capacity counterfactuals monitoring evaluation"}, {"title": "16.01 · Choosing a field and a research agenda", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Select a tractable specialization and define an economically significant question. Comparative advantage scope feasibility contribution field boundaries macro-finance default"}, {"title": "16.02 · Literature mapping and critical reading", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Map a field by questions, methods and disagreements rather than accumulating citations. Search strategy seminal papers frontier updates evidence tables citation chains"}, {"title": "16.03 · Replication and reproduction", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Reproduce a research result and document discrepancies without overstating their meaning. Data provenance code reconstruction measurement versions replication limits reporting"}, {"title": "16.04 · Referee reports and research judgment", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Write a fair report that distinguishes fatal identification issues from improvements. Contribution correctness evidence novelty major-minor concerns constructive criticism"}, {"title": "16.05 · Question design and identification strategy", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Formulate a research question with an explicit path from data or assumptions to conclusions. Estimand mechanism identification counterfactual feasibility alternative explanations"}, {"title": "16.06 · Theory building and model discipline", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Develop a parsimonious model that produces testable or interpretable implications. Baseline assumptions equilibrium comparative statics falsifiable implications extensions"}, {"title": "16.07 · Data construction, measurement and ethics", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Build a defensible research dataset with documented measurement and access constraints. Collection linkage proxies privacy consent concepts missingness research integrity"}, {"title": "16.08 · Research design, power and preregistration", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Plan an empirical study that separates design from outcome-driven specification choices. Power assignment analysis plan preregistration multiple outcomes contingency decisions"}, {"title": "16.09 · Robustness, uncertainty and external validity", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate whether a conclusion survives plausible alternative assumptions and populations. Sensitivity robustness specification curves transportability structural stability limits"}, {"title": "16.10 · Writing a scholarly paper", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Organize a paper around its question, contribution, evidence and limitations. Abstract introduction methods results tables appendices transparent claims"}, {"title": "16.11 · Seminars, revision and oral defense", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend research under probing questions and revise based on justified criticism. Presentation questions response memo revision defense disagreement"}, {"title": "16.12 · Independent research portfolio", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Assemble original work and an evaluation protocol for independent expert judgment. Original contribution reproducibility ethics audit external review research agenda next papers"}, {"title": "17.01 · Learning objectives and prerequisite design", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Translate disciplinary knowledge into assessable learning capabilities. Objectives prerequisite maps cognitive load course alignment transfer"}, {"title": "17.02 · Explaining intuition, mathematics and evidence", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Teach a concept across levels without changing its meaning or hiding assumptions. First principles representations notation derivation empirical interpretation analogies"}, {"title": "17.03 · Designing an undergraduate course", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Build a coherent course from prerequisites to cumulative assessments. Syllabus sequencing pacing assignments readings integration"}, {"title": "17.04 · Designing a graduate seminar", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Organize a research seminar around questions, methods and scholarly disagreement. Paper selection presentations discussion critique replication research proposals"}, {"title": "17.05 · Problem design and meaningful assessment", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Write assessments that diagnose reasoning and transfer rather than template recognition. Difficulty validity original problems unseen transfer rubrics exam security"}, {"title": "17.06 · Grading, feedback and remediation", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Grade consistently and give feedback that identifies the next learning action. Calibration fairness partial credit misconceptions remediation reassessment"}, {"title": "17.07 · Teaching cases and leading discussion", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Lead analytical discussion while making assumptions and evidence visible. Cases questioning participation alternative decisions synthesis facilitation"}, {"title": "17.08 · Misconceptions and learning evidence", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Diagnose persistent misunderstandings and evaluate whether instruction changed them. Diagnostic questions retrieval formative feedback retention learning measurement"}, {"title": "17.09 · Research supervision and mentoring", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Guide research toward independence with explicit responsibilities and ethical boundaries. Scope milestones feedback authorship conflict mentorship independence"}, {"title": "17.10 · Communication to policymakers and boards", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Translate technical findings into decisions without overstating certainty. Executive memos uncertainty graphs trade-offs audience actionable recommendations"}, {"title": "17.11 · Observed teaching and reflective practice", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate teaching using observation and evidence of learner understanding. Observation rubric peer feedback student work revision accessibility reflection"}, {"title": "17.12 · Teaching and supervision portfolio", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend a complete teaching program and its assessment evidence. Syllabi materials teaching demonstration exam design supervision plan external evaluation"}, {"title": "18.01 · Institutional purpose and strategy", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Translate an institution's mission into coherent choices and resource priorities. Mission stakeholders advantage trade-offs strategy success measures"}, {"title": "18.02 · Resource allocation and institutional budgets", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Build budgets and allocate resources under opportunity cost and uncertainty. Operating budgets capital budgets endowments research funding P&L scenario planning"}, {"title": "18.03 · Organizations, incentives and culture", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Design organizational responsibilities and incentives that support desired behavior. Structure principal-agent delegation culture coordination measurement unintended incentives"}, {"title": "18.04 · Hiring, evaluation and talent development", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Build defensible hiring and development processes for expert organizations. Selection evidence evaluation promotion feedback succession bias controls"}, {"title": "18.05 · Negotiation, conflict and stakeholder decisions", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Negotiate institutional decisions using incentives, alternatives and principled process. BATNA interests bargaining coalition conflict resolution contracts"}, {"title": "18.06 · Governance, fiduciary duties and integrity", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Map decision rights and accountability using jurisdiction-aware legal and ethical analysis. Boards duties conflicts disclosure academic freedom research integrity escalation"}, {"title": "18.07 · Academic institution leadership", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Design academic strategy while protecting scholarship and educational standards. Faculty curriculum research funding admissions governance donors endowment autonomy"}, {"title": "18.08 · Financial institution leadership", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Integrate business strategy, capital, liquidity, risk and operating accountability. Business model capital allocation risk appetite controls compensation regulatory relations"}, {"title": "18.09 · Operations, innovation and change", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Implement organizational improvements with measurable feedback and adaptation. Process bottlenecks change experimentation technology AI evaluation execution"}, {"title": "18.10 · Crisis leadership and high-stakes judgment", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Make and revise accountable crisis decisions with incomplete information. Triage uncertainty scenarios communication decision logs contingency after-action review"}, {"title": "18.11 · Real responsibility and leadership evaluation", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Assess leadership through observed decisions and outcomes rather than self-description. Project ownership people budgets delivery external feedback retrospective attribution"}, {"title": "18.12 · Institutional leadership portfolio and final integration", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend an integrated strategy and demonstrate the limits and strengths of one's preparation. Academic-financial cases governance research teaching capital risk external evaluation continuing development"}];
